    "0c52ead2-f379-4662-a614-fffb8e804074": {
      "id": "0c52ead2-f379-4662-a614-fffb8e804074",
      "name": "Automated Testing",
      "content": "<p>❯ I want you to create a new outline tool called IdeaM - TESTS. That outline will be continuously&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; updated (add to .md file) as new features are added or changed. This tool will allow you to run &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; tests of every IdeaM feature and generate a log of test results that will allow you to addrss any&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; identified defienciencies. Do you think you can do it?&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>⏺ Yes, I can build this. Here's my plan:&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; <strong>IdeaM - TESTS Outline Structure:</strong> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Organized by feature area (Outline Management, Node Operations, AI Features, Import/Export, etc.) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Each test case has: description, steps, expected result, last tested date, status &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Covers all documented features from the User Guide and Help Chat&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; <strong>Test Runner Script:</strong> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Python script that reads the test outline &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - For each test: displays steps, prompts for pass/fail/skip &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Updates the outline with results and timestamps &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Generates a summary log file&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; <strong>Continuous Updates (in </strong><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" class=\"text-blue-500 hover:text-blue-600 underline cursor-pointer\" href=\"http://CLAUDE.md\"><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong></a><strong>):</strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - When implementing new features, add corresponding test cases&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - When fixing bugs, add regression tests&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Run test suite before major releases&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; <strong>Limitations:</strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - UI testing requires manual execution (I can't click browser buttons)&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;=&gt; can you find a way to click buttons on the electron dev app?&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;⏺ Yes! I can use <strong>Playwright</strong> to automate the Electron app. It can click buttons, fill forms, and verify UI &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; state. Let me set it up: &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - The script will guide you through tests and record results&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; - Automated tests possible for: file operations, API endpoints, data validation &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; Should I proceed with this approach? The first version would cover: &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 1. Core outline operations (create, delete, rename) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 2. Node operations (add, edit, move, indent/outdent)&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 3. Import/export formats&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 4. AI features&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 5. Settings and subscription tiers&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; 6. Welcome flow we just built &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": true,
      "prefix": "8"
    },
    "df576dc1-6596-492a-861e-605c4d6a824e": {
      "id": "df576dc1-6596-492a-861e-605c4d6a824e",
      "name": "Verdict: What Is and Isn't a Problem",
      "content": "<p><strong>NOT a problem (your friend's concern):</strong></p><p>Bundling third-party npm packages (TipTap, Radix UI, Mermaid, Excalidraw, etc.) is <strong>fully allowed</strong>. Apple's \"self-contained\" rule means the app cannot <em>download and execute code at runtime</em>. All our 76+ npm packages are compiled and bundled into the app binary at build time — this is standard practice and Apple explicitly approves it.</p><p><strong>WHAT IS a problem:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Guideline 5.1.2(i) — AI Data Sharing</strong>: We send user content to Google Gemini and OpenAI without explicit, named consent. This is a <em>rejection-level</em> violation added November 2025.</li><li><strong>No In-App Purchase</strong>: We reference Premium tiers and rate limits but have no StoreKit IAP implementation. Apple requires IAP for all digital content/subscriptions.</li><li><strong>Missing Privacy Policy &amp; Nutrition Labels</strong>: Required for App Store Connect submission.</li><li><strong>API Key Security</strong>: Keys should be server-side only, never in client bundles.</li></ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.1"
    },
    "720df529-3ba7-42a9-8aba-027f543f273e": {
      "id": "720df529-3ba7-42a9-8aba-027f543f273e",
      "name": "External API Calls Inventory",
      "content": "<p>All runtime external API calls IdeaM makes:</p><p></p><ul><li><p><strong>Google Gemini 2.0 Flash</strong> — Sends: user outline content, prompts. Purpose: AI generation, content expansion, source extraction. <em>Requires Guideline 5.1.2(i) consent.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>OpenAI TTS</strong> — Sends: podcast script text. Purpose: text-to-speech synthesis. <em>Requires consent + privacy disclosure.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>AssemblyAI</strong> — Sends: audio recordings. Purpose: transcription with diarization. <em>Requires consent + privacy disclosure.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>YouTube oEmbed</strong> — Sends: video URL. Purpose: fetch video title. <em>Low risk — no user data.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>Google Fonts</strong> — Sends: nothing (preconnect only). Purpose: typography. <em>No issue — bundle fonts locally instead.</em></p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Good news:</strong> All API calls go through our Next.js server routes (not directly from the client). The architecture is correct — we just need consent UI and privacy disclosures.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.2"
    },
    "271e9c43-32f5-4aa7-9593-22effed83737": {
      "id": "271e9c43-32f5-4aa7-9593-22effed83737",
      "name": "Guideline 5.1.2(i) — AI Consent (CRITICAL)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Apple's November 2025 rule:</strong> Apps that share personal data with third-party AI services must:</p><ol><li>Clearly disclose that data is shared with third-party AI</li><li>Identify the AI provider <strong>by name</strong> (e.g., \"Google Gemini,\" \"OpenAI\")</li><li>Obtain <strong>explicit user permission</strong> before sharing</li><li>Proxying through your own server does NOT exempt you</li></ol><p><strong>Workaround — Implementation Plan:</strong></p><ul><li>Add a one-time <strong>AI Consent Dialog</strong> on first use of any AI feature</li><li>Text: \"IdeaM uses Google Gemini for AI text generation and OpenAI for podcast audio synthesis. Your outline content is sent to these services to generate results. No data is stored by these providers beyond processing your request.\"</li><li>Two buttons: \"I Agree\" (saves consent to localStorage) and \"Learn More\" (links to privacy policy)</li><li>If user declines, AI features are disabled but the app remains fully functional for manual outlining</li><li>Add consent status to Settings dialog so user can revoke/re-grant</li></ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.3"
    },
    "52345f34-eead-4d67-a51a-fcb33ace253d": {
      "id": "52345f34-eead-4d67-a51a-fcb33ace253d",
      "name": "In-App Purchase Requirements",
      "content": "<p><strong>Current state:</strong> Code references FREE/BASIC/PREMIUM/ACADEMIC tiers with rate limiting, but the plan is hardcoded to PREMIUM for beta. No actual payment implementation.</p><p><strong>Apple's rule:</strong> All digital content and subscription features sold within iOS apps must use Apple's StoreKit In-App Purchase system (Apple takes 15-30% commission).</p><p><strong>Options:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Implement StoreKit IAP</strong> via RevenueCat or direct StoreKit 2 — safest approach, works globally</li><li><strong>External payment link (US only)</strong> — May 2025 court ruling allows a single external link. Apple's 27% commission on external purchases is barred while injunction holds. Risky if ruling is overturned.</li><li><strong>Make it free during beta</strong> — Ship with all features unlocked, add IAP later. This is the simplest short-term approach and avoids App Review friction.</li></ol><p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Ship beta with features free. Implement RevenueCat IAP before commercial launch. RevenueCat handles subscription management, receipt validation, and cross-platform sync.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.4"
    },
    "bf3b9fc2-5bf3-4fbd-830a-fa02a976d096": {
      "id": "bf3b9fc2-5bf3-4fbd-830a-fa02a976d096",
      "name": "Privacy Policy & App Privacy Labels",
      "content": "<p><strong>Required before App Store submission:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong> — Must be hosted and linked in App Store Connect. Must disclose:</li><ul><li>Data sent to Google Gemini (outline text, prompts)</li><li>Data sent to OpenAI (podcast script text for TTS)</li><li>Data sent to AssemblyAI (audio recordings for transcription)</li><li>Local-only data storage (outlines on device, no cloud sync)</li><li>Microphone usage (speech recognition, recording)</li></ul><li><strong>App Privacy \"Nutrition Labels\"</strong> — Declare in App Store Connect:</li><ul><li>Data type: \"User Content\" — Purpose: \"App Functionality\" — Linked to User: No — Tracking: No</li><li>Data type: \"Audio Data\" — Purpose: \"App Functionality\" (transcription)</li></ul><li><strong>SDK Privacy Manifests</strong> — Apple requires privacy manifests for certain SDKs. Check if any of our dependencies are on Apple's required list (Xcode will flag these during build).</li></ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.5"
    },
    "ad6c413a-92ae-4f5c-8ca9-01d4e83e9330": {
      "id": "ad6c413a-92ae-4f5c-8ca9-01d4e83e9330",
      "name": "API Key Security",
      "content": "<p><strong>Current risk:</strong> API keys in <code>.env.local</code> are used server-side via Next.js API routes — this is architecturally correct. However:</p><ul><li>Keys must never be committed to git or bundled in client code</li><li>For iOS production: keys live on the Vercel backend (environment variables in Vercel dashboard), not in the app binary</li><li>The iOS app loads from Vercel, so API calls go: iOS → Vercel → Google/OpenAI. Keys never leave the server.</li></ul><p><strong>Action items:</strong></p><ol><li>Verify .env.local is in .gitignore (it is)</li><li>Set API keys in Vercel dashboard environment variables for production</li><li>Rotate current keys before any public release (they've been visible in dev logs)</li><li>Add rate limiting per-user on API routes to prevent abuse</li></ol>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.6"
    },
    "20639806-ddf9-4b6f-9f87-ac0e425cabf2": {
      "id": "20639806-ddf9-4b6f-9f87-ac0e425cabf2",
      "name": "Capacitor & WKWebView Compliance",
      "content": "<p><strong>Status: LOW RISK</strong></p><p>Apple allows Capacitor/WKWebView apps as long as they provide \"substantial functionality\" beyond a simple website wrapper (Guideline 4.2). IdeaM qualifies because it provides:</p><ul><li>Native file system access (Capacitor Filesystem plugin)</li><li>Native share sheet (Capacitor Share plugin)</li><li>Microphone access for recording</li><li>Rich interactive outlining, AI generation, diagram creation, podcast production</li><li>Local data storage and offline document management</li></ul><p><strong>One concern:</strong> Production iOS app currently loads from <code>https://idiam-pro.vercel.app</code>. For App Store reliability, consider bundling a static export in the app binary so it works fully offline, with Vercel as an optional update source.</p><p><strong>No dynamic code execution found:</strong> No eval(), no remote script loading, no downloaded executables. All code is bundled at build time. This is fully compliant.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "c532b50b-823b-4309-b717-2ec786dc73a2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "10.1.7"
    },
    "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165": {
      "id": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "name": "Gemma 4 vs llama3.2 — Migration Evaluation",
      "content": "<p><strong>Research date:</strong> April 9, 2026</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> Recommendation pending review</p><p>Google released Gemma 4 on April 2, 2026. This evaluation compares it against the current local-AI fallback (llama3.2 running in Ollama) and recommends a migration path.</p><p><strong>Important clarification:</strong> Ollama is the runtime/inference engine; Gemma 4 is a model that runs <em>inside</em> Ollama. The real question is whether to switch the default model from llama3.2 to Gemma 4 — not whether to replace Ollama itself.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "2f759835-9c75-462e-9901-4509889e81e2",
      "childrenIds": [
        "1dca5035-5422-4826-8ef7-98f0aeb0e3a3",
        "934b2963-d637-4e6c-a0ee-583b126f42f6",
        "b7c9ef15-bd5e-4641-8c90-d81138c5f3bd",
        "f45a56ac-af6c-4620-b6ea-69480cb6087b",
        "934eb7a2-3507-41cd-a5e1-e131006f47f8",
        "1383793d-c1a9-4116-84d4-59ff82088253"
      ],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5"
    },
    "1dca5035-5422-4826-8ef7-98f0aeb0e3a3": {
      "id": "1dca5035-5422-4826-8ef7-98f0aeb0e3a3",
      "name": "Background",
      "content": "<p>IdeaM currently uses Ollama with the llama3.2 model as a local-AI fallback for when the Gemini API is unavailable. Ollama is installed at <code>/usr/local/bin/ollama</code>. The fallback path is invoked from server actions in <code>src/app/actions.ts</code> via the <code>generateWithOllama()</code> helper in <code>src/lib/ollama-service.ts</code>.</p><p>Google's release of Gemma 4 introduces a new family of open models with day-0 Ollama support, raising the question of whether we should migrate.</p>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.1"
    },
    "934b2963-d637-4e6c-a0ee-583b126f42f6": {
      "id": "934b2963-d637-4e6c-a0ee-583b126f42f6",
      "name": "Current Setup: llama3.2 in Ollama",
      "content": "<ul><li><p><strong>Runtime:</strong> Ollama (latest)</p></li><li><p><strong>Model:</strong> llama3.2 (3B parameters)</p></li><li><p><strong>Modality:</strong> Text only</p></li><li><p><strong>Context window:</strong> 128K tokens</p></li><li><p><strong>Memory footprint:</strong> ~2-3GB RAM</p></li><li><p><strong>Maturity:</strong> Battle-tested, integrated in production code</p></li><li><p><strong>Use case:</strong> Local fallback when Gemini API is unavailable or rate-limited</p></li></ul><p></p>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.2"
    },
    "b7c9ef15-bd5e-4641-8c90-d81138c5f3bd": {
      "id": "b7c9ef15-bd5e-4641-8c90-d81138c5f3bd",
      "name": "Gemma 4 Overview",
      "content": "<p><strong>Released:</strong> April 2, 2026 by Google DeepMind</p><p><strong>License:</strong> Apache 2.0 (full commercial use, modification, distribution)</p><p><strong>Variants:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>E2B</strong> — runs under 1.5GB RAM, suitable for mobile/edge devices</p></li><li><p><strong>E4B</strong> — 4.5B active parameters, beats Gemma 3 27B across benchmarks</p></li><li><p><strong>26B MoE</strong> — Mixture of Experts, activates only 3.8B params per inference, optimized for latency</p></li><li><p><strong>31B Dense</strong> — maximum quality, outperforms Llama 4 on major benchmarks</p></li></ul><p><strong>Capabilities:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Multimodal:</strong> Text + image (all variants); video + audio natively on E2B/E4B</p></li><li><p><strong>Context window:</strong> 128K (edge models), 256K (workstation models)</p></li><li><p><strong>Languages:</strong> 140+ with cultural context understanding</p></li><li><p><strong>Native function calling</strong> for tool-use patterns</p></li><li><p><strong>Configurable thinking modes</strong> — trade speed for quality</p></li></ul><p><strong>Ollama support:</strong> Day-0, requires Ollama 0.20+</p>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.3"
    },
    "f45a56ac-af6c-4620-b6ea-69480cb6087b": {
      "id": "f45a56ac-af6c-4620-b6ea-69480cb6087b",
      "name": "Advantages of Gemma 4 over llama3.2",
      "content": "<ol><li><p><strong>Multimodal capability</strong> — can process images locally. This is a significant unlock for IdeaM: local outline-from-screenshot, local diagram analysis, and image-aware operations without sending data to Gemini API.</p></li><li><p><strong>Better reasoning and coding benchmarks</strong> — Gemma 4 31B outperforms Llama 4 on AIME 2026 Math (89.2% vs 88.3%), LiveCodeBench v6 (80.0% vs 77.1%), and GPQA Diamond (84.3% vs 82.3%).</p></li><li><p><strong>Native function calling</strong> — useful for tool-use patterns; better integration with structured outputs.</p></li><li><p><strong>Configurable thinking modes</strong> — let users choose speed vs quality.</p></li><li><p><strong>Strong multilingual support</strong> — 140+ languages with cultural awareness, valuable for international users.</p></li><li><p><strong>Larger effective context</strong> — better long-context retention than llama3.2.</p></li><li><p><strong>Apache 2.0 license</strong> — full commercial use guaranteed.</p></li></ol>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.4"
    },
    "934eb7a2-3507-41cd-a5e1-e131006f47f8": {
      "id": "934eb7a2-3507-41cd-a5e1-e131006f47f8",
      "name": "Tradeoffs and Risks",
      "content": "<ul><li><p><strong>Memory footprint:</strong> llama3.2 (3B) is much lighter than even the smallest Gemma 4 variant. The E4B needs ~3-4GB RAM minimum; the 26B/31B need 16GB+.</p></li><li><p><strong>Speed on smaller hardware:</strong> llama3.2 will remain faster on Macs with limited RAM.</p></li><li><p><strong>Maturity:</strong> llama3.2 is battle-tested in our existing code; Gemma 4 just released (April 2, 2026) and may have undiscovered issues.</p></li><li><p><strong>Migration effort:</strong> Need to update <code>getBestAvailableModel()</code> in <code>ollama-service.ts</code> to detect Gemma 4 variants and choose appropriately based on available system memory.</p></li><li><p><strong>User download time:</strong> Larger models mean longer first-time setup for end users.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.5"
    },
    "1383793d-c1a9-4116-84d4-59ff82088253": {
      "id": "1383793d-c1a9-4116-84d4-59ff82088253",
      "name": "Recommendation: MIGRATE (with smart model selection)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Yes, migrate — but carefully and with hardware-aware model selection.</strong></p><p><strong>Specific plan:</strong></p><ol><li><p><strong>Default to Gemma 4 E4B</strong> for the local fallback path on most user systems. This gives multimodal capabilities and significantly better quality than llama3.2 with a still-modest memory footprint.</p></li><li><p><strong>Keep llama3.2 as a low-memory fallback</strong> for users on machines with less than 8GB RAM. The existing fallback chain should detect available memory and choose the right model.</p></li><li><p><strong>Offer 26B MoE as a power-user option</strong> for systems with 16GB+ RAM. Its MoE architecture activates only 3.8B params per inference, so it's fast despite the higher quality.</p></li><li><p><strong>Do NOT replace Ollama</strong> — it remains the right runtime for both old and new models.</p></li><li><p><strong>Update <code>getBestAvailableModel()</code></strong> in <code>src/lib/ollama-service.ts</code> to: (a) prefer Gemma 4 variants when available, (b) select the largest variant the system can comfortably run, (c) fall back to llama3.2 if no Gemma 4 variant is installed.</p></li><li><p><strong>Document the migration</strong> in the user-facing setup guide so users know to run <code>ollama pull gemma4:e4b</code> after upgrading Ollama to 0.20+.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Biggest unlock:</strong> The multimodal capability. Being able to process images locally — without sending to Gemini API — would be a meaningful privacy improvement and would enable features like local outline-from-screenshot generation.</p><p><strong>Sources:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Google DeepMind: deepmind.google/models/gemma/gemma-4/</p></li><li><p>Google Blog: blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/gemma-4/</p></li><li><p>NVIDIA Technical Blog on edge deployment</p></li><li><p>Ollama setup guide for Gemma 4</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "e4e10724-ead3-438b-9ea0-151794e1d165",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "6.5.6"
    },
    "ec15f121-7462-4696-a3c3-1d521cbbdaba": {
      "id": "ec15f121-7462-4696-a3c3-1d521cbbdaba",
      "name": "Development Log",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "eb8821e4-e918-41a3-aede-ee7cabe6ecea"
      ],
      "type": "chapter",
      "content": "<p>Chronological record of major development sessions, what was shipped, and decisions made.</p>"
    },
    "eb8821e4-e918-41a3-aede-ee7cabe6ecea": {
      "id": "eb8821e4-e918-41a3-aede-ee7cabe6ecea",
      "name": "April 9-17, 2026 — Gemma 4, iOS Fixes, MCP, Vision",
      "parentId": "ec15f121-7462-4696-a3c3-1d521cbbdaba",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<h3>Session: April 9-17, 2026 — Gemma 4 Migration, iOS Fixes, MCP Validation, Vision Feature</h3>\n\n<p><strong>5 commits shipped:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gemma 4 migration</strong> — Adopted Google's Gemma 4 (gemma4:e4b) as the default local AI model. Hardware-aware selection picks the right variant by system memory. Fixed tag format (colons, not hyphens), upgraded Ollama to v0.20.5, pulled the model, verified end-to-end. Built a 6-test Playwright smoke suite that drives Settings, Help Chat, Knowledge Chat (both modes), Research dialog, and context menus.</li>\n<li><strong>Three iOS gesture/layout bugs</strong> — (a) Single-finger tap was being suppressed by an unconditional preventDefault on touch pointerdown; removed it. (b) Tap-again on an already-selected node now enters edit mode on iOS, matching the gesture model. (c) iPhone always uses stacked vertical layout regardless of orientation — no more unusably-narrow horizontal split when the phone rotates.</li>\n<li><strong>Gemma 4 thinking-mode fix</strong> — Gemma 4's built-in reasoning mode was silently consuming the entire token budget, returning empty content. Added think:false to all four Ollama API call sites. Response quality and length improved measurably across Help Chat and Knowledge Chat.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge Chat truncation fix + MCP test suite</strong> — Replaced naive top-of-string context slicing with keyword-based relevance ranking. Outlines containing the user's query terms are packed first, so relevant content is no longer dropped. Also wrote a 24-test automated MCP server test suite covering all 17 tools (outline CRUD, node CRUD, tags, export, auth). Improved Playwright stability with stale-dialog cleanup.</li>\n<li><strong>Gemma 4 vision feature</strong> — New describeImageAction routes images to Gemma 4 vision (local) or Gemini (cloud) based on provider setting. Every embedded image in the content editor now has a \"Describe with AI\" button. When running locally, no image data leaves the device. Verified OCR-quality text extraction from screenshots.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Quality comparison (Gemma 4 vs llama3.2):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gemma 4 produces tighter, less repetitive prose. llama3.2 is ~30% faster on simple queries.</li>\n<li>llama3.2 stays as a fast/lightweight fallback for low-RAM systems.</li>\n<li>Gemma 4 multimodal (vision) has no llama3.2 equivalent — unique capability.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Test infrastructure established:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>gemma4-smoke-test.js — 6 Playwright UI tests for the local AI path</li>\n<li>mcp-server-test.js — 24 automated MCP server tool tests</li>\n<li>\"TEST EVERYTHING\" trigger phrase in CLAUDE.md runs all test suites unattended</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Known remaining items:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>electron-test.js selectors need updating for the current UI (0/8 — stale, not blocking)</li>\n<li>TestFlight needs a new upload to include these changes</li>\n<li>Gemma 4 smoke test All-Outlines timing is intermittently flaky (test infra, not product bug)</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "3c6a28a8-5939-4ea1-b878-9be7cfca18ec": {
      "id": "3c6a28a8-5939-4ea1-b878-9be7cfca18ec",
      "name": "Second Brain Feature & Rebrand Plan",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "7fa6e013-5ceb-4895-b9d2-7032fae5e8d9",
        "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742",
        "3d8925bd-5802-4541-9f92-67b251fc138f"
      ],
      "type": "chapter",
      "content": "<p>Transform IdeaM into SecondBrainWare — a personal knowledge operating system. Domain: SECONDBRAINWARE.COM (owned).</p>"
    },
    "7fa6e013-5ceb-4895-b9d2-7032fae5e8d9": {
      "id": "7fa6e013-5ceb-4895-b9d2-7032fae5e8d9",
      "name": "Concept Overview",
      "parentId": "3c6a28a8-5939-4ea1-b878-9be7cfca18ec",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Core idea:</strong> In addition to individual outlines, create a special reserved outline type called the \"Second Brain.\" This is a single, always-present outline where users accumulate everything they want to remember across their entire life — articles, notes, research, ideas, conversations, images, anything.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Why it works:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All existing IdeaM features apply directly: import from any source, merge under shared themes, search via Knowledge Chat, export in any format, AI-powered expansion and synthesis.</li>\n<li>The Second Brain outline is always queryable via Knowledge Chat — \"What do I know about X?\" becomes a one-tap question.</li>\n<li>Local AI (Gemma 4 vision + text) means the entire knowledge base stays private on-device.</li>\n<li>Reframes the product from \"outlining tool\" (small market) to \"personal knowledge OS\" (massive market).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Competitive positioning:</strong> Notion, Obsidian, and Roam are note-taking tools. SecondBrainWare is the app that makes the Second Brain method actually work — structured, searchable, AI-powered, and private.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Domain:</strong> SECONDBRAINWARE.COM (owned and ready).</p>"
    },
    "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742": {
      "id": "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742",
      "name": "Implementation Plan",
      "parentId": "3c6a28a8-5939-4ea1-b878-9be7cfca18ec",
      "childrenIds": [
        "4b78817e-5a50-45a5-9bc0-301b2bb444f5",
        "5f4436fd-0c93-47ab-b9ec-3329624d22f6",
        "3a7561bf-02c9-4db3-be03-52db7d2a7920"
      ],
      "type": "chapter",
      "content": "<p>Three phases: core mechanics, UX polish, rebrand launch.</p>"
    },
    "4b78817e-5a50-45a5-9bc0-301b2bb444f5": {
      "id": "4b78817e-5a50-45a5-9bc0-301b2bb444f5",
      "name": "Phase 1 — Core Mechanics",
      "parentId": "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Reserved Second Brain outline:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create a new outline type flag (isSecondBrain) that marks exactly one outline as the Second Brain.</li>\n<li>On first launch (or migration), auto-create the Second Brain outline if it doesn't exist.</li>\n<li>Pin it at the top of the sidebar with a distinct brain icon. Cannot be deleted or renamed.</li>\n<li>Uses the same node structure, content editor, and storage as regular outlines.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>\"Save to Second Brain\" action:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Context menu item on any node: \"Save to Second Brain\" — copies the node (and optionally its subtree) into the Second Brain outline.</li>\n<li>Context menu item on any outline in the sidebar: \"Add to Second Brain\" — merges the entire outline into the Second Brain using the existing merge logic (theme grouping, deduplication).</li>\n<li>After saving, show a brief toast: \"Saved to Second Brain\" with an undo option.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Knowledge Chat integration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Add a third mode to Knowledge Chat: \"Second Brain\" — queries only the Second Brain outline, which is typically the largest and most comprehensive.</li>\n<li>The existing \"All Outlines\" mode continues to work and includes the Second Brain.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "5f4436fd-0c93-47ab-b9ec-3329624d22f6": {
      "id": "5f4436fd-0c93-47ab-b9ec-3329624d22f6",
      "name": "Phase 2 — UX & Discovery",
      "parentId": "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Quick-save affordances:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keyboard shortcut (e.g., Cmd+Shift+B) to save selected node to Second Brain.</li>\n<li>\"Save to Second Brain\" button in the Research & Import results dialog — after importing research, one click sends it all to the Second Brain.</li>\n<li>Drag-and-drop from any outline to the Second Brain in the sidebar.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Second Brain dashboard:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the Second Brain outline is selected, show a special header: total nodes, total words, last updated, top tags, recent additions.</li>\n<li>Quick-search bar scoped to the Second Brain (backed by Knowledge Chat).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Smart suggestions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After importing research or creating a substantial outline, suggest: \"Add this to your Second Brain?\"</li>\n<li>When Knowledge Chat finds no answer in the current outline, suggest: \"Try searching your Second Brain.\"</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "3a7561bf-02c9-4db3-be03-52db7d2a7920": {
      "id": "3a7561bf-02c9-4db3-be03-52db7d2a7920",
      "name": "Phase 3 — Rebrand & Launch",
      "parentId": "b2ce4441-54b2-475b-b2fe-07b985f74742",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Rebrand from IdeaM to SecondBrainWare:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Update app name, icons, splash screen, marketing site.</li>\n<li>Point SECONDBRAINWARE.COM to the marketing/landing page.</li>\n<li>Update App Store listing: name, description, screenshots, keywords.</li>\n<li>Keep \"IdeaM\" as the internal/legacy name in code for continuity (avoid breaking changes).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Marketing angle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Your Second Brain, actually built.\" — Not another note-taking app. A knowledge operating system.</li>\n<li>Emphasize: AI-powered search across everything you know. Private (local AI, no cloud required). Imports anything. Structured, not flat.</li>\n<li>Target audience: knowledge workers, researchers, students, lifelong learners, anyone who reads/watches/listens and wants to remember.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Launch sequence:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ship Phase 1 as an IdeaM update (Second Brain feature, no rebrand yet).</li>\n<li>Gather user feedback, iterate on UX.</li>\n<li>Ship Phase 2 polish.</li>\n<li>Rebrand to SecondBrainWare, update all public-facing assets, announce on Product Hunt / Hacker News.</li>\n</ol>"
    },
    "3d8925bd-5802-4541-9f92-67b251fc138f": {
      "id": "3d8925bd-5802-4541-9f92-67b251fc138f",
      "name": "Branding & Legal Notes",
      "parentId": "3c6a28a8-5939-4ea1-b878-9be7cfca18ec",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>\"Second Brain\" terminology:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The term \"Second Brain\" was popularized by Tiago Forte (Building a Second Brain, 2022). It is NOT trademarked — it's a generic concept/methodology.</li>\n<li>\"SecondBrainWare\" is distinct: it's the <em>software</em> for building a Second Brain, not the methodology itself. Analogous to \"spreadsheet software\" vs \"accounting method.\"</li>\n<li>Other apps use \"Second Brain\" freely in marketing (Notion, Obsidian, Mem, etc.) without legal issues.</li>\n<li>The .COM domain ownership (SECONDBRAINWARE.COM) gives strong brand positioning.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Risk mitigation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not claim to be affiliated with or endorsed by Tiago Forte or the BASB methodology.</li>\n<li>Position as \"the app that makes the Second Brain method work\" — complementary, not competitive with the book/course.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "04546fba-4cdd-4550-97e3-7d4766615f5f": {
      "id": "04546fba-4cdd-4550-97e3-7d4766615f5f",
      "name": "API Margin Revenue Model",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "5bb5e959-0007-4c01-9288-8db7c36a5374",
        "84bc4c91-a605-4b6f-8fc4-038115bcbff3",
        "75727364-afe1-46ad-9f78-c1be6a665ddb"
      ],
      "type": "chapter",
      "content": "<p>Revenue strategy: bundle AI access into Premium subscriptions using enterprise API agreements with providers. The volume discount we negotiate becomes our margin.</p>"
    },
    "5bb5e959-0007-4c01-9288-8db7c36a5374": {
      "id": "5bb5e959-0007-4c01-9288-8db7c36a5374",
      "name": "Business Model",
      "parentId": "04546fba-4cdd-4550-97e3-7d4766615f5f",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Two-tier approach:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Free/Basic tier:</strong> User brings their own API key (the Settings UI we built with guided setup). Zero cost to us per user.</li>\n<li><strong>Premium tier ($9.99/month):</strong> AI is included — calls route through our enterprise API keys. User never touches an API key. \"It just works.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Power user override:</strong> Premium users can still configure their own keys in Settings if they prefer a specific provider or model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Margin math (example with Gemini):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retail API cost per active user: ~$1-3/month (typical usage patterns)</li>\n<li>Enterprise volume discount: 20-40% off retail</li>\n<li>Our cost per user: ~$0.60-2.40/month</li>\n<li>Premium subscription: $9.99/month</li>\n<li>Gross margin on AI: $7.59-9.39/month per user</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key insight:</strong> Most users make far fewer API calls than the maximum. The average user's actual cost is well below the worst case, making this highly profitable at scale.</p>"
    },
    "84bc4c91-a605-4b6f-8fc4-038115bcbff3": {
      "id": "84bc4c91-a605-4b6f-8fc4-038115bcbff3",
      "name": "Enterprise Agreements to Pursue",
      "parentId": "04546fba-4cdd-4550-97e3-7d4766615f5f",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Priority order (based on current usage and strategic value):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> (highest priority) — Already our default provider. Apply to Cloud Partner program + Startup program. Covers text generation, vision, content synthesis.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAI</strong> — Currently used for podcast TTS only. Enterprise agreement would let us offer GPT-4o as an alternative text provider in Premium. Contact via openai.com/contact-sales.</li>\n<li><strong>Anthropic Claude</strong> — Strong for long-form reasoning and research synthesis. Would be the premium-tier AI option for Knowledge Chat. Contact via anthropic.com/contact-sales.</li>\n<li><strong>Groq</strong> — Extremely fast inference. Use as the \"fast fallback\" for simple queries. Has startup programs with free credits.</li>\n<li><strong>Mistral</strong> — Good multilingual support. Lower priority but worth pursuing for European market positioning.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Tasks created for each application. Howard handles the business applications; I handle the technical integration once agreements are signed.</p>"
    },
    "75727364-afe1-46ad-9f78-c1be6a665ddb": {
      "id": "75727364-afe1-46ad-9f78-c1be6a665ddb",
      "name": "Managed API Proxy (Technical)",
      "parentId": "04546fba-4cdd-4550-97e3-7d4766615f5f",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Architecture:</strong> Server-side API proxy route that authenticates the user's subscription tier and routes AI calls through our enterprise keys.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Route: /api/ai-proxy</li>\n<li>Authenticates user subscription (Free vs Premium)</li>\n<li>Free tier → rejects, tells user to add own key in Settings</li>\n<li>Premium tier → routes to provider using our enterprise key</li>\n<li>Tracks per-user usage for billing and quota management</li>\n<li>Enterprise keys stored as server environment variables (never exposed to client)</li>\n<li>Smart routing: picks the best provider per task type (Gemini for vision, Claude for reasoning, Groq for speed)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Blocked by:</strong> At least one enterprise agreement signed (start with Google Gemini).</p>"
    },
    "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0": {
      "id": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "name": "Session Summary — May 15–17, 2026 (plain-language status)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "674a7338-73ae-4358-8425-39dd14085a60",
        "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407",
        "3769ebd7-395d-4c82-9c6a-03c203d7a446",
        "b2005c71-306e-4a84-854c-642ae9e6cf8d",
        "ddbc6267-32d8-49f4-b38f-fd7f810267b6",
        "e992860c-019b-4a82-b5ba-43d06f0c790d"
      ],
      "type": "chapter",
      "content": "<p>Bottom line: Machine problem permanently fixed; the login/billing system is fully built (dormant until account keys are added); the LIVE BOOKS feature is built and tested; the website is updated and live; the roadmap is captured. Everything is saved and backed up on GitHub.</p>"
    },
    "674a7338-73ae-4358-8425-39dd14085a60": {
      "id": "674a7338-73ae-4358-8425-39dd14085a60",
      "name": "Machine fix",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Machine fix: The project was moved out of iCloud into a clean local folder. The slowdown/crash problem that was making the Mac unusable is gone for good. Nothing was lost. Saving and publishing were made more reliable and more secure than before.</p>"
    },
    "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407": {
      "id": "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407",
      "name": "Built — Login & billing",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [
        "6acb8d3d-8357-4274-80a1-cac31e8ec217",
        "18f0a721-5062-471d-8694-fed1169000c6",
        "4b84a91c-e2fb-460c-b0e7-fbe3517950cc"
      ],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Built — Login &amp; billing (all dormant and harmless until account keys are added, so nothing changed for users yet):</p>"
    },
    "6acb8d3d-8357-4274-80a1-cac31e8ec217": {
      "id": "6acb8d3d-8357-4274-80a1-cac31e8ec217",
      "name": "Phase 1",
      "parentId": "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Phase 1: user accounts + plan tiers (Free / Pro / Power).</p>"
    },
    "18f0a721-5062-471d-8694-fed1169000c6": {
      "id": "18f0a721-5062-471d-8694-fed1169000c6",
      "name": "Phase 2",
      "parentId": "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Phase 2: Stripe and Apple billing plumbing.</p>"
    },
    "4b84a91c-e2fb-460c-b0e7-fbe3517950cc": {
      "id": "4b84a91c-e2fb-460c-b0e7-fbe3517950cc",
      "name": "Phase 3",
      "parentId": "4e42467d-155d-44c8-997a-fe5085eae407",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Phase 3: the paid-vs-free rules. The Free plan stays generous (unlimited outlines, editing, private local AI, and bring-your-own-key). Monthly limits apply only to the heavy cloud-AI features; premium templates, premium features, and podcast generation are reserved for paid plans. When someone hits a limit they see a friendly &quot;here&#x27;s how to upgrade&quot; message, not a broken-feeling wall. The actual checkout button is a polite &quot;coming soon&quot; placeholder for now. It was verified that with no keys set, the app behaves exactly as it does today.</p>"
    },
    "3769ebd7-395d-4c82-9c6a-03c203d7a446": {
      "id": "3769ebd7-395d-4c82-9c6a-03c203d7a446",
      "name": "Built — LIVE BOOKS",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Built — LIVE BOOKS: refresh any outline against the latest information from the web. Manual trigger only; nothing changes until you preview and approve it; sources are shown; your hand-edited notes are protected from being overwritten. The core is verified working; a full live web-refresh still wants one quick check with a real AI key.</p>"
    },
    "b2005c71-306e-4a84-854c-642ae9e6cf8d": {
      "id": "b2005c71-306e-4a84-854c-642ae9e6cf8d",
      "name": "Roadmap & website (live now)",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Roadmap &amp; website (live now): removed the outdated &quot;March&quot; launch date; added concrete plain examples of how each profession uses the tool; added honest &quot;coming soon&quot; multilingual highlights (language translation, and a multilingual collaboration board). Language translation is now a high-priority roadmap item; the multilingual collaboration board is recorded as a flagship selling point.</p>"
    },
    "ddbc6267-32d8-49f4-b38f-fd7f810267b6": {
      "id": "ddbc6267-32d8-49f4-b38f-fd7f810267b6",
      "name": "Health & progress",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Health &amp; progress: the full automated test suite passed 38/38; a new automated test for LIVE BOOKS was added; overall launch progress is about 46%.</p>"
    },
    "e992860c-019b-4a82-b5ba-43d06f0c790d": {
      "id": "e992860c-019b-4a82-b5ba-43d06f0c790d",
      "name": "Your to-dos (not blocking)",
      "parentId": "cab89bc9-222e-41b9-a50c-864ef99e35f0",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "topic",
      "content": "<p>Your to-dos (no rush, not blocking): create the Clerk / Stripe / RevenueCat accounts when you want login and billing switched on; do one quick manual check of a real LIVE BOOKS web-refresh (needs an AI key); the May 19 Gemini keynote plan is queued and ready.</p>"
    },
    "5cd29e01-dd3d-4ccc-a3d2-440661b5559b": {
      "id": "5cd29e01-dd3d-4ccc-a3d2-440661b5559b",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-03 (Auth Phase 3 + BYOK UI)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "decision-auth-wall-2f737d6d"
      ],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> Auth Phase 3 (tier enforcement, #33) + BYOK UI (#34) shipped. Decisions below are non-obvious code-side calls I took as unilateral defaults — each is reversible.</p><p></p><p><strong>1. Soft-warn toast at 80% — duration 8s, fires once when crossing.</strong></p><p>Rationale: 8s is long enough to read but short enough not to obstruct. Fires once (not on every subsequent gen) so it doesn't nag.</p><p>Reverse: change <code>duration: 8000</code> in <code>useAIUsageGate</code>'s <code>softWarnCopy</code> path, or change the <code>before &lt; threshold</code> guard.</p><p></p><p><strong>2. Toast / dialog copy is conversational, BYOK-as-escape-hatch.</strong></p><p>Rationale: matches the project's natural-language error tone memory — never CLI-speak. For free-trial the copy is \"You've used all 25 of your free trial AI generations. Add a free API key in Settings — Gemini takes about a minute and stays free — or upgrade to keep going.\" For paid tiers it includes \"Your counter resets in N days\" + the BYOK bypass option.</p><p>Reverse: edit <code>softWarnCopy</code> + <code>hardBlockReason</code> in <code>src/lib/use-ai-usage-gate.tsx</code>.</p><p></p><p><strong>3. Progress bar color thresholds — emerald &lt; 80%, amber 80–99%, red 100%+.</strong></p><p>Rationale: traffic-light familiarity. Amber starts at the soft-warn threshold so the visual + toast agree.</p><p>Reverse: edit the ternary in <code>src/components/settings-dialog.tsx</code> AI Usage section.</p><p></p><p><strong>4. BYOK keys stored in localStorage (NOT Electron safeStorage) for v1.</strong></p><p>Rationale: simpler, ships now. Plaintext-key disclosure already lives in the export flow text. Migration to Keychain-backed safeStorage is a clean one-file swap in <code>src/lib/byok-keys.ts</code> (signatures already shaped for it).</p><p>Reverse: implement <code>app.safeStorage.encryptString</code> in <code>byok-keys.ts</code>; touch nothing else.</p><p></p><p><strong>5. Pro-only features (podcast, image gen): visible-with-Pro-badge + upgrade dialog on click.</strong></p><p>Rationale: locked by Howard. Discovery + upsell beats hiding.</p><p>Implementation: the central gate maps <code>feature: 'podcastGeneration' | 'imageGeneration'</code> to a Pro-only check that fires the upgrade dialog. The podcast dialog also runs the legacy <code>canUseFeature('podcastGeneration')</code> check after the gate, so both layers agree.</p><p>Reverse: add other feature keys to <code>PRO_ONLY_FEATURES</code> in <code>tier-detection.ts</code>, or remove them.</p><p></p><p><strong>6. Free-trial counter does NOT reset on month rollover.</strong></p><p>Rationale: locked by Howard — \"25 total, one-time trial.\" Implementation: <code>ai-usage-counter.ts</code> writes the monthKey but Free-trial cap (25) is checked against the running count regardless; we never zero it on rollover. (Counter month-key still rolls so Student/Pro users get clean monthly buckets.)</p><p>Reverse: change the Free-trial branch in <code>getTierCap</code> to UNLIMITED, or convert it to monthly by zeroing the counter on month change for free-trial users.</p><p></p><p><strong>7. Fail-open on gate exceptions.</strong></p><p>Rationale: locked by Howard — \"RevenueCat unreachable → fail-open.\" Generalized: any throw inside the gate (storage corruption, missing context, etc.) returns <code>true</code> (allow). Better to over-serve than lock paying users out.</p><p>Reverse: change the <code>catch</code> in <code>gate()</code> to return false + show a recovery toast.</p><p></p><p><strong>8. Counter displayed in Settings under its own \"AI Usage\" section (NOT folded into \"Account\").</strong></p><p>Rationale: there's no \"Account\" section in Settings yet (Clerk auth Phase 3.x). When Account ships, the counter can move into it; the section is self-contained and easy to relocate.</p><p>Reverse: move the JSX block into the future Account section in <code>settings-dialog.tsx</code>.</p><p></p><p><strong>9. Reset usage (debug) button only renders in NODE_ENV !== production.</strong></p><p>Rationale: lets us drive Playwright tests + lets Howard reset manually during dev, but won't ship to real users.</p><p>Reverse: drop the <code>process.env.NODE_ENV</code> guard if we want it permanently visible (not recommended).</p><p></p><p><strong>10. Test seeds via localStorage shim, not via real AI calls.</strong></p><p>Rationale: <code>_seedUsageForTest</code> + <code>_seedPaidTierForTest</code> let the Playwright suite drive every tier × counter state in &lt;1s each without needing real model traffic. Tests live in <code>tests/tier-enforcement-test.js</code> and <code>tests/byok-ui-test.js</code>; both green as of 2026-06-03.</p><p>Reverse: N/A — these are test-only helpers.</p><p></p><p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Plans &amp; Tiers node rewritten for the launch model. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — replaced old Pro/Power copy with launch tiers. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same update. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> Plans/Tiers/Enforcement node — rewritten end-to-end with the new tier model + the new modules (<code>tier-detection</code>, <code>ai-usage-counter</code>, <code>use-ai-usage-gate</code>). Both copies in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log section. ✓</p></li></ul>"
    },
    "decision-prosw-reenable-2026-06-05": {
      "id": "decision-prosw-reenable-2026-06-05",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Professional mode OFF re-enables ALL hints + toast)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Turning Settings → Tips → Professional mode OFF now re-enables ALL discovery hints (including ones previously dismissed) and surfaces a confirmation toast. The ON-direction behavior is unchanged.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-05</p><p><strong>Context:</strong> The Discovery Hints system shipped earlier today defaulted to: turning Pro mode OFF only stopped suppressing FUTURE hints — already-dismissed hints stayed dismissed. Howard's clarification: flipping OFF should fully restore the new-user discovery experience, so every hint becomes eligible to fire again the next time its trigger event happens.</p><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>OFF clears the dismissed-hints set entirely.</strong> Implemented inside <code>setProfessional</code> in <code>src/hooks/use-discovery.tsx</code>: when the new value is false, the in-memory state and the <code>discovery:dismissedHints</code> localStorage key are both reset to an empty Set. Same persistence backend the rest of the hook uses (no DB-coupling change).</p></li><li><p><strong>OFF fires a transient toast.</strong> The existing <code>useToast</code> system (NOT the sticky discovery card) is used here — this is a confirmation of a user action, not a discovery surface. Copy: title \"Welcome tips re-enabled\", body \"You'll see a 'Did You Know?' card next time you try a feature for the first time.\" Conversational tone per the natural-language-error-tone memory; never CLI-speak.</p></li><li><p><strong>ON direction unchanged.</strong> Toggling Pro mode ON still just clears currently-visible hints. No new toast on ON — the suppression IS the change, and announcing it would be redundant + chatty.</p></li><li><p><strong>Toast chosen over a confirm-dialog.</strong> A dialog would feel disproportionate to flipping a settings switch. The toast is brief, non-blocking, and lets the user keep clicking around Settings.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Inside the <code>else</code> branch of <code>setProfessional</code> in <code>src/hooks/use-discovery.tsx</code>, delete the lines that reset <code>dismissed</code> and call <code>toast()</code>. The OFF path returns to its prior \"do nothing extra\" shape.</p><p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Did You Know? tips node updated. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — Discovery Hints bullet updated. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edit, kept in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> Discovery Hints node — updated in docs/outlines and synced to the user folder. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. ✓</p></li></ul><p><strong>Test:</strong> <code>tests/_pro-mode-toggle-check.js</code> — disposable web-mode Playwright probe that loaded the running dev server, dismissed the Reformat hint, toggled Pro mode ON via storage + UI to OFF via the actual Switch component, verified the toast appeared with the expected copy, and re-fired <code>editor-first-text-selection</code> to confirm the Reformat hint reappears. All assertions passed; probe deleted after verification.</p><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> shows zero new errors in <code>use-discovery.tsx</code>; pre-existing errors elsewhere in the codebase are unrelated to this change.</p>"
    },
    "decision-refresh-from-web-move-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-refresh-from-web-move-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Refresh from Web moved to Import menu + 5 orphan file deletions + Quick Capture folded into Brain menu)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision A:</strong> Move the \"Refresh from Web\" menu entry from the Smart Tools (sparkles) menu to the Import menu (BookDown icon). The dialog, the keyboard shortcut (Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R), and the Command Palette entry are unchanged — only the menu home moved.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-06</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Refresh from Web brings external web content INTO existing nodes. That's the Import metaphor: pull stuff in from the outside world. Smart Tools is the AI-driven action menu; refresh isn't an AI feature you reach for by intent — it's an import-shaped action that happens to use AI under the hood. Putting it in the Import menu also reduces Smart Tools clutter (Smart Tools is now: Quick Command, Reformat with AI, Generate Subtree from Topic, Translate this section, Ask Your Outlines).</p><p><strong>Placement in Import menu:</strong> appended at the bottom of the existing list — Research &amp; Import, Import Outline, Link to Outline…, Restore All Outlines, <em>Refresh from Web</em>. End-of-menu position keeps the most-used import actions (Research &amp; Import, Import Outline) at the top, and visually groups Refresh from Web with the other \"work-on-an-existing-outline\" entries (Link to Outline…, Restore All).</p><p><strong>Disabled state:</strong> the new Import-menu item is disabled until a node is selected, matching the Smart Tools menu's previous <code>hasSelectedNode</code> gate. Tooltip explains \"Select a node first.\"</p><p><strong>Code change scope:</strong> <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> (add the Import menu item, import the <code>RefreshCw</code> icon), <code>src/components/ai-menu.tsx</code> (remove the Smart Tools menu item, drop the now-unused <code>onOpenLiveBooks</code> prop). The dialog itself (<code>live-books-dialog.tsx</code>) and all wiring in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> are unchanged.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> add the menu item back to <code>ai-menu.tsx</code> (restore the <code>onOpenLiveBooks</code> prop on the AIMenu interface + the menu entry) and delete the new entry from the Import-menu dropdown in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>.</p><p></p><p><strong>Decision B:</strong> Delete five orphan component files from <code>src/components/</code>: <code>import-dialog.tsx</code>, <code>ingest-source-dialog.tsx</code>, <code>pdf-export-dialog.tsx</code>, <code>state-check-dialog.tsx</code>, <code>tag-manager.tsx</code>.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> a precise import-grep showed none of these files are imported anywhere in <code>src/</code>. They are refactor leftovers from earlier work — replaced by <code>file-import-dialog.tsx</code>, the unified ingest-preview flow, the <code>export-dialog.tsx</code> pdf path, etc. Carrying them adds build-time noise and confuses future code-reading.</p><p><strong>Verification:</strong> grep for both the file name and each file's exported symbol (e.g. <code>ImportDialog</code>, <code>TagManager</code>) returned only self-references. <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> after deletion shows zero new errors attributable to the missing files.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> <code>git restore</code> the files from the prior commit. They're standalone — no other code depends on them, so restoring is mechanical.</p><p></p><p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Smart Tools menu node + Refresh from Web how-to: location updated to Import menu. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — Refresh from Web location updated; Sparkles-icon enumeration updated. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edits, kept in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> — Import menu enumeration now lists Refresh from Web; LIVE BOOKS reachability paragraph updated. Both copies in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. ✓</p></li></ul><p><strong>Visual verification:</strong> disposable web-mode Playwright probe drove the running dev server, opened the Import menu (confirmed Refresh from Web present), clicked it (confirmed the dialog opened with title \"Refresh from Web\"), then opened the Smart Tools menu (confirmed Refresh from Web absent). Probe deleted after passing.</p><p></p><p><strong>Decision C:</strong> Retire the standalone Quick Capture (Inbox icon) toolbar button. Quick Capture is now reachable ONLY through the Brain dropdown — and through the Cmd+Shift+I keyboard shortcut, which is unchanged. The Brain icon itself stays in the toolbar at its existing slot.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-06</p><p><strong>Howard's prompt (verbatim):</strong> \"Wanted to merge quick capture button with a second brain button. Leave the second brain icon. If you wanna put a quick capture button somewhere else, that might be a good idea. If you can find a place where it fits on all platforms.\"</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> The standalone Inbox icon sat two slots from the Brain icon and competed for the same mental category (\"second-brain stuff\"). New users couldn't tell whether \"Inbox\" and \"Brain\" were the same feature, two parts of one feature, or two unrelated features. Folding Quick Capture into the Brain dropdown eliminates the ambiguity, frees a toolbar slot for future use, and makes the relationship between the second-brain actions explicit (they all hang off one icon).</p><p><strong>Menu order chosen:</strong> Open Second Brain → Quick Capture → Save Selection to Second Brain → [separator] → Search Second Brain → View Dashboard → Import to Second Brain. Cognitive flow: enter the second brain, drop a thought in, save a selection, then explore (search, dashboard, import).</p><p><strong>What still works without the standalone button:</strong> (1) Cmd+Shift+I keyboard shortcut (the handler lives in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> as a global keydown listener, not on the deleted button). (2) Mobile/iPad users get the same single tap they always did — they tap the Brain icon, then tap Quick Capture. The keyboard-less workflow is one tap longer than before but the discoverability is much better.</p><p><strong>Code change scope:</strong> <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> only — deleted the standalone Tooltip+Button block for Quick Capture, reordered the existing Brain DropdownMenu so Open Second Brain comes first and Quick Capture second. The Inbox icon import is kept because the menu item still uses it. <code>onOpenQuickCapture</code> prop and parent wiring unchanged.</p><p><strong>Documentation (mandatory five places):</strong> User Guide node (sb-quick-capture, sb-menu) updated to describe the menu home and reorder; help-chat-dialog APP_CONTEXT + help-chat route APP_CONTEXT updated to note the toolbar change and the new path; <em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> Quick Capture node updated with the merge story; CLAUDE.md Rosetta Stone Quick Capture row updated to \"Brain menu\" on every platform.</p><p><strong>Visual verification:</strong> short Playwright probe against the running dev server confirmed the standalone Inbox button is no longer in the toolbar, the Brain dropdown opens with Quick Capture present (in the expected order), clicking Quick Capture opens the floating dialog, and Cmd+Shift+I still opens the same dialog without the standalone button.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> add the deleted Tooltip+Button block back to outline-pane.tsx just before the Brain DropdownMenu (the diff is small and self-contained). The menu item inside the dropdown can stay — having both surfaces is harmless if Howard ever wants the one-click access back.</p>"
    },
    "be1559d4-4d71-4bf8-a010-dcbc5647c03a": {
      "id": "be1559d4-4d71-4bf8-a010-dcbc5647c03a",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-04 (Launch Metrics dashboard)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Launch Metrics admin dashboard shipped at <code>/admin/metrics</code> with 7 metrics, manual refresh, mock-data layer, and a localStorage admin gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-04</p>\n<p><strong>Context:</strong> Launch P0 #5 — Howard needed a single page where the launch-week vitals are visible in 10 seconds. No real telemetry is wired yet (Clerk, RevenueCat, internal events all post-launch), so v1 had to ship without blocking on those.</p>\n<p><strong>Decisions made (delegated, all reversible):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Route:</strong> <code>/admin/metrics</code> (under a new <code>/admin/*</code> namespace with <code>robots: noindex/nofollow</code>). Keeps it clearly separate from public marketing pages and the app surface.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Access gate:</strong> v1 reads <code>localStorage.isAdmin === 'true'</code>. Stop-gap until Clerk admin roles land. Non-admins see a friendly \"access required\" card with instructions for enabling the flag from DevTools.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Metrics chosen (7):</strong> Signups this week, Activation rate, Day-1 retention, Day-7 retention, Free→Paid conversion, AI runs today, MRR. Picked from the 10-item menu in the brief. These are the seven I'd actually look at during launch week — funnel top (signups), funnel middle (activation), stickiness (D1/D7), monetization (conversion + MRR), and load (AI runs).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Data source:</strong> <code>src/lib/launch-metrics.ts</code> is a typed data layer. Today every <code>fetch*</code> function returns a mock object with <code>source: 'mock'</code>. Wiring to real data later means swapping the body of one function at a time — the UI never changes.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Refresh model:</strong> Manual button + \"Last updated N min ago\" timestamp. A 60s setInterval re-renders the timestamp string but does NOT auto-refetch. Howard decides when to look.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Layout:</strong> Cards grid (1 col mobile / 2 col sm / 3 col lg). Big number, trend arrow with delta, one-sentence description. \"Demo\" badge on every mock metric plus a single banner at the top so there's zero ambiguity about whether the numbers are real.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Ship the surface now, wire real data after launch. The structure of <code>LaunchMetric</code> (id, label, value, format, trend, source, lastUpdated) is a clean contract that makes the post-launch data wire-in mechanical.</p>\n<p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Delete <code>src/app/admin/</code> and <code>src/lib/launch-metrics.ts</code>. Remove the new node from <code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> and the ADMIN DASHBOARD blocks from the two help-chat APP_CONTEXT strings.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "1142b051-1c36-414a-b6fd-dc852ebaad6f": {
      "id": "1142b051-1c36-414a-b6fd-dc852ebaad6f",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-04 (LIVE BOOKS live-refresh test, #57)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Added Playwright coverage for the LIVE BOOKS live-refresh round-trip + per-node post-refresh cards via <code>tests/livebooks-live-refresh-test.js</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-04</p>\n<p><strong>Context:</strong> Launch P0 #3 / issue #57. The existing <code>tests/livebooks-test.js</code> covered the configure-phase safety UI (mandatory preview, merge/overwrite, auto-apply-off) but NOT the AI round-trip or the per-node diff cards that render in the preview phase. Real AI calls are slow, non-deterministic, and cost money / require a live key, so the round-trip needed to be mocked.</p>\n<p><strong>Mock strategy (non-obvious choice):</strong> The refresh runs through a Next.js server action that POSTs to the current page URL with a <code>next-action</code> header and expects an RSC Flight-stream response (<code>text/x-component</code>). Forging the Flight stream byte-for-byte from outside Next/React is brittle, so the test uses a TWO-PRONG fallback design:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Prong 1 (default, <code>LIVEBOOKS_MOCK_MODE=fulfill</code>):</strong> <code>page.route()</code> intercepts the <code>next-action</code> POST and fulfills it with a minimal Flight-shaped response containing fake refreshed content + citations. If the React client decodes it, we land in the preview phase with real DIFF CARDS (Before/After + Accepted/Rejected toggles) and the test asserts per-card toggle behavior + the <em>Apply N approved</em> footer count.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Prong 2 (auto-fallback OR <code>LIVEBOOKS_MOCK_MODE=abort</code>):</strong> If React refuses the forged stream, the action throws on the client — the transform engine catches per-node and records an error proposal. The dialog STILL transitions to the preview phase and renders the per-node ERROR card section (\"Could not refresh\") plus the summary header. This still proves the post-refresh card rendering code path executes.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other choices:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>BYOK exemption:</strong> Sets <code>localStorage.apiKey_gemini</code> to a dummy value to push the user into the <code>free-byok</code> tier so the AI usage gate doesn't block the Refresh button. The dummy key never reaches Gemini because the route interceptor stops the network call.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Either-or assertion model:</strong> The per-node card assertion passes if EITHER success diff cards OR error cards render — both are valid post-refresh UI surfaces, and the choice depends only on whether the Flight forgery decodes. The report records which path actually ran, so a future tightening (when Flight encoding is solved cleanly) is easy.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Phase-transition assertion:</strong> Watches for the running-phase \"Refreshing content against the latest information…\" text + Loader2 spinner before the preview, but tolerates missing it (the action can transition faster than the 4s probe). The hard requirement is the preview phase appearing within 60s.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Delete <code>tests/livebooks-live-refresh-test.js</code>. No app code was touched.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "a3f4e9c8-1b2d-4e5f-8a7c-9d6e0f2b1c4a": {
      "id": "a3f4e9c8-1b2d-4e5f-8a7c-9d6e0f2b1c4a",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-04 (Import/Export toolbar buttons + AI menu cleanup)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Added two new top-level toolbar icon buttons — <strong>Import</strong> (BookDown) and <strong>Export</strong> (BookUp) — and migrated file-management actions out of the Wrench menu and the AI Features menu into them.</p>\n<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-04</p>\n<p><strong>Context:</strong> The Wrench menu had grown into a five-item file admin grab-bag (Import Outline, Export Current Outline, Backup All, Restore All, Refresh Guide). Discoverability was poor — Howard wanted Import and Export to be first-class toolbar surfaces, not three clicks deep. At the same time, \"Research & Import\" was sitting under the AI Features menu, which made source-merging feel like an AI-only oddity instead of part of the general import story.</p>\n<p><strong>What moved where:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>New Import button</strong> (BookDown icon, tooltip \"Import\") contains: Research & Import (from AI menu), Import Outline (from Wrench), Restore All Outlines (from Wrench).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>New Export button</strong> (BookUp icon, tooltip \"Export\") contains: Share Subtree as… (NEW top-level entry that delegates to the existing per-node action; disabled with a tooltip when no node selected), Export Current Outline (from Wrench), Backup All Outlines (from Wrench).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Wrench kept but slimmed</strong> to a single item: Refresh User Guide. Kept rather than collapsed because Settings already has its own toolbar icon, and a one-item dropdown still beats moving Refresh-Guide into Settings (it's a destructive-ish action that belongs near the outline tree, not buried in preferences).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Right-click Share Subtree preserved</strong> on every node — context-menu remains the primary discovery surface for per-node export; the Export menu is the second discoverable surface for users who don't naturally right-click.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Decisions made (delegated, all reversible):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Button placement:</strong> Import → Export → Wrench, grouped together in the title row to the right of the Tell-AI message-square button. Reads left-to-right like a sentence and keeps file-management visually adjacent.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Tooltip wording:</strong> single-word tooltips (\"Import\", \"Export\", \"Admin\") per the two-word-max UI convention. Long-form explanation lives in aria-label (\"Import — bring data into your outline\", \"Export — send your outline data out\").</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Share Subtree at top of Export menu:</strong> placed first because it's the single most-likely user intent when reaching for Export (per-node export is more common than whole-outline export). Disabled state shows \"Select a node first\" via the title attribute.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>AI menu post-cleanup:</strong> Tell AI, Generate Subtree, LIVE BOOKS, Translate, Knowledge Chat. Research & Import removed.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>onOpenBulkResearch prop kept on AIMenu interface:</strong> Marked optional and left in the interface signature for backward compatibility, but no longer consumed in render. Removing the destructure silences the unused-var warning. Safe to delete the prop entirely in a later cleanup pass.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Managing Outlines node and Import/Export node both rewritten to describe the new dual-button surface. AI Menu node updated to note Research & Import has moved. ✓</p></li>\n<li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — Outline management + Export/Share + Import bullets all rewritten. ✓</p></li>\n<li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edits, kept in sync. ✓</p></li>\n<li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> (docs/outlines copy) — Toolbar bullets in the Outline Tree section + Import/Export node both rewritten. User-folder copy NOT updated this session because the file may be loaded in the running Electron — flag this for a follow-up cp once Howard isn't on that outline. ✓ (project-folder only)</p></li>\n<li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. Same user-folder caveat. ✓ (project-folder only)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Test:</strong> <code>tests/import-export-toolbar-test.js</code> added (Playwright) — verifies both icon buttons render, both dropdowns open with the expected items, Share Subtree disables when no node is selected, AI menu no longer contains Research & Import, and Wrench menu no longer contains the migrated items. Not run this session (Howard's Electron is live on port 9002).</p>\n<p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> shows no new errors in <code>ai-menu.tsx</code> or <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>; pre-existing errors elsewhere in the codebase are unrelated.</p>\n<p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert the changes to <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> (restore the single Wrench dropdown with all five items) and <code>src/components/ai-menu.tsx</code> (re-add the Research & Import item + Library import). All other files (initial-guide, help-chat APP_CONTEXTs, outlines) describe surfaces only and can be edited back to match.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "42601094-76f7-4a5d-9924-dc1841904a25": {
      "id": "42601094-76f7-4a5d-9924-dc1841904a25",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-04 (Cross-outline link nodes Phase 1 + value-based naming pass)",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-04\nCONTEXT: Howard requested a Phase 1 cross-outline link node (click to navigate to another outline) plus a value-based naming pass that strips implementation-detail language from button labels.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. SCHEMA: Added a new NodeType value 'outline-link' and a new optional field linkedOutlineId on OutlineNode. Backward-compatible — existing .idm files load fine; older app builds reading the new field just ignore it.\n\n2. PHASE 2 DEFERRED (now shipped, see the next Decisions Log entry): Sidebar nesting of linked outlines, inline preview of linked content, AI features traversing links, and loop detection were all deferred to post-launch.\n\n3. PICKER UI: A new dialog OutlineLinkPickerDialog lists candidate outlines with search-as-you-type filtering, excludes the currently-open outline, and shows a small Guide / Second Brain badge on the relevant entries. Picker dismisses on Enter or double-click. Rejected the dropdown approach because users with many outlines need search.\n\n4. ICON: Used ExternalLink from lucide-react for the link-node icon (and the menu items). Link2 was already taken by the existing 'link' node type for URL links.\n\n5. INSERTION RULES: Link nodes are inserted as a CHILD of the currently-selected node, or as a top-level child of the root if nothing is selected. The link's name defaults to the linked outline's name (user can rename freely). Link nodes are leaves — no children allowed in Phase 1.\n\n6. CLICK BEHAVIOUR: Clicking a link node navigates immediately to the linked outline (sets currentOutlineId to linkedOutlineId, selects the linked outline's root). Rationale — this matches the existing 'link' node type, which opens URLs on click without a confirm. If the target outline has been deleted, a destructive toast appears ('The outline you linked to has been deleted.') and no navigation happens; the link remains so the user can rename, delete, or re-target it.\n\n7. NAMING PASS (value-based labels):\n   - 'AI Features' menu trigger tooltip + aria-label became 'Smart Tools'\n   - 'Tell AI…' menu item became 'Quick Command'\n   - 'Knowledge Chat' menu item + dialog title + Welcome outline + AI Consent dialog + Second Brain copy became 'Ask Your Outlines'\n   - 'LIVE BOOKS: Refresh from the web' menu item, dialog title, command palette entry, toast messages became 'Refresh from Web'\n   - The internal code identifiers (LIVE_BOOKS_*, liveBooks*, file names like live-books-dialog.tsx) are PRESERVED. Renaming code paths would be churn with no user benefit.\n\n8. ASK AI TOOLBAR TOOLTIP — kept as 'Ask AI (Cmd+K)'. Considered renaming to match 'Quick Command' but 'Ask AI' is the discoverability framing first-time users need, and the cost-cap conversation (about counting AI generations) lives nearby in Settings. Inside the menu the label is more functional ('Quick Command'); on the toolbar it remains a hook.\n\n9. NL INTERPRET-COMMAND PROMPT — updated the SCHEMA description from 'open LIVE BOOKS (refresh from web)' to 'open Refresh from Web (the web-refresh feature, formerly called LIVE BOOKS)' so the LLM can still parse the old name in user prompts. The example 'unknown' reason was reworded to drop LIVE BOOKS in favour of 'refresh from the web'.\n\n10. CONTEXT MENU — Added 'Insert Link to Outline…' to the node right-click menu, between 'Add Sibling Node' and 'Rename Node'. Wired through onInsertOutlineLink from outline-pro down through outline-pane and node-item (including the recursive descent).\n\n11. IMPORT MENU ORDERING — 'Link to Outline…' appears between 'Import Outline' and 'Restore All Outlines' because conceptually a link IS a kind of import (you are pulling another outline into the current outline's tree).\n\nREVERSAL: Each rename is a one-line copy change. Removing the link feature requires reverting types/index.ts (drop 'outline-link' from NodeType union, drop linkedOutlineId), deleting outline-link-picker-dialog.tsx, and removing the prop/state wiring in outline-pro + outline-pane + node-item. Existing .idm files that already have link nodes would render them with the unknown-type FileText fallback but would not crash.\n\nFILES TOUCHED:\n- src/types/index.ts\n- src/components/outline-link-picker-dialog.tsx (NEW)\n- src/components/outline-pro.tsx\n- src/components/outline-pane.tsx\n- src/components/node-item.tsx\n- src/components/node-icon.tsx\n- src/components/ai-menu.tsx\n- src/components/live-books-dialog.tsx\n- src/components/command-palette.tsx\n- src/components/knowledge-chat-dialog.tsx\n- src/components/ai-consent-dialog.tsx\n- src/lib/welcome-outline.ts\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts\n- src/ai/flows/interpret-command.ts\n- src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx\n- tests/cross-link-test.js (NEW)\n\nOUTLINE SAFETY: Howard's Electron is currently running, so this entry has been written ONLY to docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm. The user-folder copy at ~/Documents/IDM Outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm requires a manual sync once the parent agent confirms the active outline is not Development. The same caveat applies to the How-it-works outline (NOT updated this turn for the same reason).",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "b7e1d5c0-2f4a-4d8e-9c3a-1e6b7f8a9c0d": {
      "id": "b7e1d5c0-2f4a-4d8e-9c3a-1e6b7f8a9c0d",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-04 (Cross-outline link nodes Phase 2: sidebar nesting)",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-04\nCONTEXT: Howard requested Phase 2 of the cross-outline link feature — linked outlines should appear INDENTED beneath their parent outline in the left sidebar list, expandable/collapsible per parent. Phase 1 (link node type + click-to-jump + naming pass) shipped earlier the same day.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. MULTI-PARENT — appear under EVERY parent that links to the target. If outline B is linked from both A and C, B shows up nested under BOTH A and C. Considered showing the target only once (under the first parent) for visual minimalism but rejected — if a user puts a link in two places they expect to see it in two places. Correctness over minimalism. A user setting to flip this is a post-launch consideration if real-world libraries get noisy.\n\n2. TOP-LEVEL VISIBILITY — a linked outline ALSO stays at the top level of the sidebar. Discoverability matters: a user shouldn't have to know which parent links to which child in order to find it. Linked outlines therefore appear in BOTH places (root + nested under each parent). This is intentional duplication.\n\n3. LOOP HANDLING — visited-set per render path. When the renderer would recurse into an outline that already appears as an ancestor in the current render path, it renders a non-expandable leaf marked with a circular-arrow indicator (RotateCw from lucide-react) and a tooltip 'Already shown above — this outline links back into the chain'. Rationale — the tooltip copy must be conversational, not CLI-speak ('Error', 'Cycle detected', 'Reference loop'). 'Already shown above' is what a user would say out loud if explaining it to a friend.\n\n4. DEDUPE WITHIN A PARENT — if a single parent outline has two link nodes both pointing to the same target, the sidebar shows that target once under that parent. Implemented by collecting linkedOutlineId values into a Set before rendering. A user almost never wants to see two identical nested branches in a row; if they do, they can rename one of the link nodes for clarity (the rename doesn't change what's shown in the sidebar but does change what they see in the in-outline tree).\n\n5. PERSISTENCE — per-outline expand/collapse state stored in browser localStorage. Key naming: 'sidebarExpanded:<outlineId>'. Chose per-outline state (not a single global 'all open' flag) because users will want different parents open at different times depending on what they're working on. Default state is COLLAPSED so a freshly-opened library is not visually noisy.\n\n6. INDENTATION — 16 pixels per nesting depth, applied as inline padding-left. Matches the visual rhythm of the in-outline tree (NodeItem). Considered using Tailwind utility classes but the depth value is dynamic per row so inline style is cleaner.\n\n7. NESTED-ROW ICON — re-used ExternalLink (lucide-react) for nested rows, the same icon used by the 'outline-link' node type in the in-outline tree. Keeps the visual story consistent: 'this row is here because something links to it'.\n\n8. CHEVRON BEHAVIOUR — chevron on the LEFT of any row that has visible linked children. Click chevron toggles expansion only (with stopPropagation so the row's own click handler doesn't ALSO load the outline). Click on outline NAME loads the outline (same as a top-level row). A blank width-stable slot is reserved for rows without children so names align at each depth level.\n\n9. SEARCH BEHAVIOUR — when the user is typing in the sidebar search box, nested outlines match against their names just like top-level outlines, regardless of nesting state. A nested branch is force-expanded if it contains (or transitively contains) a matching name, so the match is visible without the user having to click any chevrons. When no children match, the chevron is hidden (no point expanding into nothing).\n\n10. PERFORMANCE — parent→children map memoised with useMemo, recomputed only when the outlines array changes. The O(N×M) walk over outlines × link nodes is negligible for typical libraries (~50 outlines, maybe 200 link nodes total). No pre-optimisation past that.\n\n11. RECURSIVE COMPONENT vs FLATTENED RENDER — chose a recursive OutlineRow component over a flattened render-with-depth-field. Recursive is easier to reason about for the loop-breaking logic (the ancestorIds array is passed naturally down the recursion), and React's reconciler handles the recursion fine at typical sidebar depth (rarely more than 3-4 levels).\n\n12. CYCLE LEAF IS NOT CLICKABLE — a cycle-leaf row doesn't navigate or toggle anything. Considered making it click-through to the cycle target (since the user might still want to jump there), but that would be confusing — the user would expect navigation behaviour that matches a normal nested row, and we're explicitly signalling 'this isn't a real branch'. If they want to navigate to that outline, they can click its top-level row instead.\n\n13. NO CHANGES TO THE IN-OUTLINE TREE — Phase 2 is sidebar-only. The 'outline-link' node inside an outline's tree still renders as a leaf with click-to-jump behaviour (Phase 1). Inline preview of linked content remains a future phase. AI features (Ask Your Outlines, Quick Command) do NOT traverse cross-outline links yet — that's also future.\n\nREVERSAL: Remove the OutlineRow recursive component from sidebar-pane.tsx and restore the flat .map() of filteredOutlines that existed before this change. Delete the parentToChildrenIds memo, the usePersistedExpansion hook, the CycleLeafRow component, and the matchesSearch helper. localStorage entries with the 'sidebarExpanded:' prefix can be left in place — they're cheap and harmless if the feature is later restored.\n\nFILES TOUCHED:\n- src/components/sidebar-pane.tsx (significant rewrite — added recursive nesting render + persistence hook + cycle-leaf component)\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts (extended the cross-link node's description with Phase 2 behaviour)\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx (APP_CONTEXT updated to describe Phase 2 sidebar nesting)\n- src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (duplicated APP_CONTEXT updated to match)\n- docs/outlines/IdeaM - How it works.idm (cross-link section now describes sidebar nesting in plain language)\n- docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm (this Decisions Log entry)\n- tests/sidebar-nesting-test.js (NEW — Playwright test covering nest/expand/collapse/persist/cycle/multi-parent/dedupe/delete)\n\nOUTLINE SAFETY: Howard's Electron is currently running, so this entry has been written ONLY to docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm. The user-folder copy at ~/Documents/IDM Outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm requires a manual sync after the parent agent confirms the active outline is not Development. The same caveat applies to the How-it-works outline (updated this turn in docs/ only).",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "d3c9f7e1-5a4b-4c2d-8e1f-7b6a9c0d4e5f": {
      "id": "d3c9f7e1-5a4b-4c2d-8e1f-7b6a9c0d4e5f",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Launch P0 #7: Web payment via Stripe + RevenueCat)",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-05\nCONTEXT: Launch P0 task #7 / issue #22 — wire web-side Stripe Checkout into the existing RevenueCat-orchestrated tier model. Architecture decision (locked 2026-05-26): RevenueCat is the single source of truth for entitlements regardless of payment processor. Stripe handles web/desktop charges; Apple IAP handles iOS charges per App Store guideline 3.1.1. The webhook bridges Stripe → RevenueCat so a user who pays on web has the entitlement available everywhere RevenueCat is read.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. STRIPE CHECKOUT (hosted page) over Stripe Elements. Rationale: simpler integration, smaller compliance surface for v1, Apple Pay + Google Pay surface automatically with no extra wiring. Trade-off — slightly less brand control on the payment screen. Reversible by swapping the checkout route to create Elements-compatible PaymentIntents instead of sessions.\n\n2. CUSTOMER PORTAL (Stripe-hosted) instead of a custom subscription management UI. Cancel, change plan, update card, view invoices all delegated to Stripe. Rationale: cancel-anytime is a trust signal; building a custom portal is weeks of work that Stripe already solved. Manage Subscription button in Settings opens a one-time portal URL.\n\n3. THREE PLANS, THREE PRICES: pro-monthly ($9.99/mo), pro-annual ($89/yr — save 25%), student-monthly ($4.99/mo). Free is not sold (BYOK is free forever, gated only by the user supplying their own AI key). Two RevenueCat entitlements: 'pro' and 'student'. Pro monthly + Pro annual both grant the 'pro' entitlement — the same tier with two billing rhythms.\n\n4. PLATFORM-AWARE ROUTING on the /upgrade page. Web/Electron go to Stripe Checkout. iOS Capacitor goes to the RevenueCat native purchase flow (stubbed today with a toast — the real call lands when @revenuecat/purchases-capacitor is wired). Detection is via the existing getPlatformContext() helper. Inline comments reference App Store guideline 3.1.1 so future maintainers don't accidentally route iOS users to Stripe and get the app rejected.\n\n5. STUB-MODE FALLBACK in every billing endpoint. When STRIPE_SECRET_KEY or REVENUECAT_API_KEY is unset, the route logs a clear 'not configured — stubbing response' message and returns a mock success (checkout returns /upgrade/success?stub=1; portal returns /upgrade?portal=stub; webhook returns 200 without provisioning). Lets the entire upgrade UX be exercised end-to-end before Howard wires real keys. The success page seeds the paid tier into localStorage in stub mode so the app behaves as if the user is paid, which lets the test suite drive every code path without a Stripe account.\n\n6. NO TRIAL ON WEB. The existing 25-generation free trial IS the trial — bolting a second 14-day trial on top would muddy the value story. The pricing page on the marketing site mentions a 14-day free trial (separate marketing copy) but the checkout creates a subscription immediately. Reversible: add `subscription_data: { trial_period_days: 14 }` to the checkout session config.\n\n7. WEBHOOK IDEMPOTENCY via a process-local ring buffer (last 500 event ids). Cheap, correct within a single Node process. For a multi-instance production deployment, swap in a Redis-backed dedupe store — out of scope for v1. Stripe retries with the same event id, so this is sufficient to avoid double-provisioning.\n\n8. WEBHOOK STUB SIGNATURE SKIP — when STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET is unset, the route parses the request body as JSON without verifying the signature, logs a loud warning, and still returns 200. Used by the Playwright test to POST synthetic events. The warning is intentionally loud so this can never accidentally ship to production unguarded — a 'WEBHOOK_SECRET unset' line in the logs would surface in Sentry immediately.\n\n9. REVENUECAT REST API for entitlement provisioning (NOT a native SDK on the server). The Stripe webhook is server-side; the native RevenueCat plugin lives on the client. The webhook calls REST endpoints (/subscribers/[userId]/entitlements/[entId]/promotional) with duration='lifetime' on grant and revoke_promotionals on delete. Rationale: lets the server stay light and stateless. The 'lifetime' duration means Stripe is the lifecycle source of truth — when a subscription is cancelled, revoke fires; until then, the entitlement is active.\n\n10. TIER REFRESH WITH 5-MIN CACHE. refreshTier() in tier-detection.ts re-reads the entitlement (currently from a localStorage shim; will become a fetch to a /api/billing/entitlements route once Clerk is wired). 5 minutes balances 'show new tier right after Checkout' against 'don't hammer RevenueCat on every render'. The /upgrade/success page forces a refresh with { force: true } so the user sees the new tier immediately.\n\n11. ANNUAL SAVINGS LABELED AS '25%' (not '$30'). Percentages communicate value better than dollars at this price point — a 25% discount feels meaningfully better than 'save $30/year', and the user does the dollar math anyway. The annual button copy reads '$89/yr — save 25%' which gives both numbers.\n\n12. ENV-VAR NAMING: new launch-model names (STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO_MONTHLY, STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO_ANNUAL, STRIPE_PRICE_ID_STUDENT_MONTHLY, REVENUECAT_API_KEY, REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_STUDENT) added without removing the legacy STRIPE_PRICE_PRO / STRIPE_PRICE_PREMIUM / REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_PREMIUM names. The legacy names still resolve in billing-config.ts as fallbacks. Reversible: drop the fallback lookups once nothing reads them.\n\n13. UPGRADE PROMPT CTA REPLACED. The existing UpgradePromptProvider dialog's 'Upgrade (coming soon)' placeholder now reads 'See plans' and routes to /upgrade. Old text honored intent without a destination; new text routes to a real purchase flow.\n\n14. SETTINGS MANAGE SUBSCRIPTION button is now tier-aware. Paid users (student or pro) see 'Manage Subscription' (opens Stripe Customer Portal); free / trial users see 'See plans' (goes to /upgrade). The old AIPlanDialog ('Legacy plan info') is preserved as a ghost button below — kept rather than deleted because it documents the older FREE/BASIC/PREMIUM/ACADEMIC plan strings that some code paths still reference (ai-context, ai-service).\n\n15. NO ANGLE BRACKETS in TSX strings (recurring rule). Every reference to a userId placeholder in APP_CONTEXT strings is written as [userId] not <userId> — angle brackets in a TSX string literal would have torn the app down again like the help-chat bug yesterday morning.\n\nREVERSAL: Delete src/app/upgrade/, src/app/api/billing/portal/, src/lib/billing/revenuecat-provisioning.ts; revert the rewrites of src/app/api/billing/checkout/route.ts, src/app/api/billing/webhook/route.ts; remove the LAUNCH_STRIPE_PRICES block from src/config/billing-config.ts; revert the upgrade-prompt.tsx CTA and the Settings 'Manage Subscription' block. Env vars added to .env.example are harmless to leave in place. tests/billing-test.js can be deleted.\n\nFILES TOUCHED:\n- src/config/billing-config.ts (LaunchPlanId + LAUNCH_STRIPE_PRICES + REVENUECAT_API_KEY + REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_*_ID)\n- src/app/api/billing/checkout/route.ts (planId-based, stub fallback, userId metadata)\n- src/app/api/billing/webhook/route.ts (RevenueCat provisioning + idempotency + stub mode)\n- src/app/api/billing/portal/route.ts (NEW — Stripe Customer Portal)\n- src/lib/billing/revenuecat-provisioning.ts (NEW — REST grant/revoke + stub mode)\n- src/lib/tier-detection.ts (refreshTier + 5-min cache + clearTierCache)\n- src/app/upgrade/page.tsx (NEW — three-plan upgrade page)\n- src/app/upgrade/success/page.tsx (NEW — calls refreshTier on return)\n- src/app/upgrade/cancel/page.tsx (NEW — friendly re-pitch)\n- src/components/upgrade-prompt.tsx (CTA now routes to /upgrade)\n- src/components/settings-dialog.tsx (tier-aware Manage Subscription button)\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts (new 'Upgrade & Manage Subscription' node under Plans & Tiers)\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx (APP_CONTEXT: new BILLING & SUBSCRIPTIONS section)\n- src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT: same section)\n- .env.example (new launch-model env vars + comments)\n- tests/billing-test.js (NEW — Playwright suite, not run this turn due to Electron-in-use)\n\nQUALITY GATE: `npx tsc --noEmit` shows zero new errors in any of the files touched. Pre-existing errors elsewhere in the codebase (tiptap, excalidraw, ai-service legacy plans) are unrelated.\n\nOUTLINE SAFETY: Howard's Electron is currently running. This entry is written to docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm only. A manual sync to ~/Documents/IDM Outlines/ is required once the active outline is not Development. The How-it-works outline mirror is updated in docs/ only with the same caveat.",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "c8f2e1d3-4a5b-6c7d-8e9f-0a1b2c3d4e5f": {
      "id": "c8f2e1d3-4a5b-6c7d-8e9f-0a1b2c3d4e5f",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Launch P0 #6: Onboarding email sequence, #46)",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-05\nCONTEXT: Launch P0 task #6 / issue #46 — the email deliverability guide already existed (Resend + Google Workspace recommended); what was missing was the actual sending infrastructure + welcome/drip copy + trigger wiring. Built end-to-end during a parallel session with the Stripe web-payment agent; touched no billing files.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. RESEND over SendGrid / Postmark / SES. Locked in the task brief. Cheapest free tier (3,000 emails/mo / 100/day), simple REST API. From address: 'IdeaM <welcome@2ndbrainware.com>' — matches the support-email guide.\n\n2. NO SDK DEPENDENCY. We call Resend's REST endpoint directly with fetch instead of importing the official @resend/node SDK. Zero new packages, zero npm install needed, ships immediately. Trade-off: no streaming / batch APIs. Reversible — swap activeTransport in src/lib/email/send.ts to call the SDK once npm install is run.\n\n3. NO REACT EMAIL. The brief suggested @react-email/components for templates. Rejected for v1 — four templates is too few to justify a build-time-rendered React component pipeline, and the package isn't installed. Templates live as pure TypeScript functions in src/emails/*.ts that return { subject, html, text }. The shared layout (header, footer, brand colours, unsubscribe footer, plain-text fallback) is one file (src/emails/_layout.ts) that every template calls. Reversible: add @react-email/components and rewrite the four template files as .tsx components; the call signature of renderXxxEmail() can stay identical.\n\n4. ENV-GATED, NO-OP WHEN OFF. Same philosophy as Sentry/Clerk/Stripe. With RESEND_API_KEY unset, sendWelcomeEmail and sendDripEmail log '[email/send] Resend not configured — skipping' and return { status: 'skipped-no-key' }. Signup, webhook, cron all keep working. This means the app behaves exactly as it does today until Howard sets the env var.\n\n5. CLERK WEBHOOK STUB MODE. With CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET unset, the webhook route logs a clear warning and accepts the POST without verifying the Svix signature. Lets the Playwright test exercise the route end-to-end without a real Clerk account. Production with the secret set enforces signature verification automatically — no flag to flip, no code change needed.\n\n6. SVIX SIGNATURE VERIFICATION IN-HOUSE (no svix npm package). Svix's scheme is a single HMAC-SHA256 over '{svix-id}.{svix-timestamp}.{rawBody}' with a base64-decoded key derived from a 'whsec_'-prefixed secret. Implemented directly with Node's built-in crypto. Zero new dependencies.\n\n7. UNSUBSCRIBE TOKEN = HMAC-SHA256(secret, 'unsubscribe:' + userId). Hex-encoded. No expiry, no JWT. Rationale: an unsubscribe link is 'I no longer want emails'; if a malicious actor unsubscribes a user, the user can re-opt-in by signing in. The cost of getting it wrong is low; the cost of dragging in jsonwebtoken or jose is not. Constant-time compare via crypto.timingSafeEqual. Secret read from EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET; dev fallback works out of the box but logs a warning in production.\n\n8. UNSUBSCRIBE STATE = JSON FILE ON DISK. v1 strategy: a JSON file under .idiampro/unsubscribed.json (configurable via EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_STORE_PATH for tests). Atomic write via temp-file + rename so a torn write can't corrupt the JSON. Fine for launch volume (O(thousands) of users for several months). Post-launch swap to Vercel KV / Postgres is a single-file change — every caller talks to four exported functions (isUnsubscribed, markUnsubscribed, markResubscribed, _resetUnsubscribeStoreForTest) so only those four functions need to repoint.\n\n9. DRIP SCHEDULE = 3 / 7 / 14 DAYS. Brief's recommendation, kept as-is. Drips are scheduled by Vercel Cron post-launch (config documented in src/app/api/cron/drip/route.ts header comments — path: '/api/cron/drip', schedule: '0 14 * * *', secret: CRON_SECRET). Welcome email is the only one that's live on launch day; drip cron is wired but the user-iterator stub returns [] until Clerk integration is wired up at getActiveUsersForDripCron().\n\n10. CRON ROUTE TEST MODE. The cron POST accepts a body of { users: [{ id, email, firstName, day }] } that bypasses the production user iterator and directly sends the requested drips. Used by the Playwright test to assert that day 3 / 7 / 14 each fire the right template without standing up a Clerk-backed user store. Both modes are protected by the same CRON_SECRET (with stub fallback when unset). Keeping the test path in production code means the test can't drift from real behaviour.\n\n11. LIST-UNSUBSCRIBE HEADER on every send. Sets 'List-Unsubscribe: <unsubscribeUrl>' so Gmail / Apple Mail / Outlook surface a one-click unsubscribe in their UI chrome. Combined with the visible footer link this gives CAN-SPAM compliance + reduces spam-folder placement.\n\n12. EMAIL COPY = CONVERSATIONAL, SECOND-PERSON, ONE CTA PER EMAIL. Matches the natural-language-error-tone memory. No marketing-speak, no exclamation marks (almost), no aggressive scarcity language. Day 14 upgrade pitch leads with 'No sales pitch — just a quick heads-up' and mentions the BYOK escape hatch up front so the user never feels cornered.\n\n13. INTRO VIDEO URL is a defaulted-to-app-URL placeholder. The welcome email links to a 'two-minute intro video' that doesn't exist yet (post-launch content task). The renderer accepts an optional introVideoUrl prop; when unset, it falls back to the app URL so the link still works.\n\n14. NO ANGLE BRACKETS IN APP_CONTEXT STRINGS. Per the no-angle-brackets-in-tsx memory, every reference to userId / token in the help-chat APP_CONTEXT strings uses [userId] and [hmacToken] instead of <userId> / <hmacToken>. Tested by tsc check — zero new errors.\n\n15. EMAIL_FROM IS OVERRIDABLE. Default 'IdeaM <welcome@2ndbrainware.com>'. Env var EMAIL_FROM overrides if Howard later wants to send from a different verified domain (e.g. 'IdeaM <hello@idiampro.com>').\n\nREVERSAL: Delete src/emails/, src/lib/email/, src/app/api/webhooks/clerk/, src/app/api/cron/drip/, src/app/api/emails/unsubscribe/, src/app/unsubscribe/, tests/onboarding-emails-test.js. Revert the ONBOARDING EMAILS section in both APP_CONTEXTs and the 'What we'll email you' node in initial-guide.ts. Env vars added to .env.example are harmless to leave in place.\n\nFILES TOUCHED:\n- src/emails/_layout.ts (NEW — shared HTML shell, brand colors, ctaButton helper, greeting helper)\n- src/emails/welcome-email.ts (NEW)\n- src/emails/day-3-features.ts (NEW)\n- src/emails/day-7-tips.ts (NEW)\n- src/emails/day-14-upgrade.ts (NEW)\n- src/lib/email/send.ts (NEW — Resend wrapper, env-gate, unsubscribed-check, _setResendTransportForTest hook)\n- src/lib/email/unsubscribe-token.ts (NEW — HMAC sign / verify / buildUnsubscribeUrl)\n- src/lib/email/unsubscribe-store.ts (NEW — atomic JSON file store, four exported functions)\n- src/app/api/webhooks/clerk/route.ts (NEW — Svix signature verify + stub mode + sendWelcomeEmail)\n- src/app/api/emails/unsubscribe/route.ts (NEW — GET + POST verify-and-mark)\n- src/app/api/cron/drip/route.ts (NEW — production + test modes, CRON_SECRET enforcement)\n- src/app/unsubscribe/page.tsx (NEW — public page, friendly confirmation, four result kinds)\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts ('What we'll email you' node added under root)\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx (APP_CONTEXT: new ONBOARDING EMAILS section)\n- src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT: same section)\n- .env.example (RESEND_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM, NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL, EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET, EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_STORE_PATH, CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET, CRON_SECRET)\n- tests/onboarding-emails-test.js (NEW — boots a scratch Next dev server on port 9099, drives every route with fetch, asserts SendOutcome.status='skipped-no-key' as proof the send layer was reached; not run this turn due to Electron-in-use on 9002)\n\nQUALITY GATE: `npx tsc --noEmit` shows zero new errors in any of the new files. Pre-existing errors elsewhere (tiptap, excalidraw, ai-service legacy plans) are unrelated.\n\nWHAT HOWARD NEEDS TO DO POST-LAUNCH: 1) Create a Resend account, verify the 2ndbrainware.com domain, set RESEND_API_KEY in Vercel + local .env.local. 2) Create the Clerk webhook in the Clerk dashboard pointing at https://<host>/api/webhooks/clerk subscribed to user.created; copy the signing secret to CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET. 3) Add to vercel.json: { 'crons': [{ 'path': '/api/cron/drip', 'schedule': '0 14 * * *' }] }; set CRON_SECRET in Vercel project env vars. 4) Wire getActiveUsersForDripCron() in src/app/api/cron/drip/route.ts to walk the real Clerk user list (or the Postgres mirror of it) once that infrastructure exists. 5) Set EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET to a sufficiently random string (openssl rand -hex 32). 6) Set NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL to the production origin so unsubscribe URLs embed the right host.\n\nOUTLINE SAFETY: Howard's Electron is currently running. This entry is written to docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm only. Sync to ~/Documents/IDM Outlines/ deferred to a moment when neither Development nor How-it-works is the active outline. The How-it-works outline mirror was updated in docs/ this turn with the new 'Onboarding emails' node (id onboarding-emails-2026-06-05).",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "e4b9d2a6-7c81-4f3e-9d2a-6b8f1c4e0d7a": {
      "id": "e4b9d2a6-7c81-4f3e-9d2a-6b8f1c4e0d7a",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Invite-only signup allowlist)",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-05\nCONTEXT: Howard wants every person who accesses the app to have his explicit permission while the IP is protected during beta. The auth wall already gates /app behind a sign-in, but anyone with an email can complete signup. This work adds a Howard-curated allowlist layer on top.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. ALLOWLIST STORAGE V1 = COMMA-SEPARATED ENV VAR. The INVITE_ALLOWLIST env var holds approved emails. To add a tester, Howard edits the value in Vercel project settings and redeploys. Cheap, simple, ships in a single subagent turn. Rationale: a database-backed admin dashboard is the right v1.1 work but would block launch; an env var is enough for the friend-and-family beta. Reversible: drop the env var and the allowlist check returns 'allowed' for everyone.\n\n2. EXTENSION POINT FOR V1.1. src/lib/access/allowlist.ts exposes two helpers: isEmailAllowed(email) and getAllowedEmails(). Both consumers (the signup page's /api/invite-check route and the Clerk user.created webhook) call only those helpers. When v1.1 lands a database-backed admin dashboard at /admin/invites, only the bodies of those two helpers change — no call site touches. /admin/invites is already created as a stub page today (gated by the existing isAdmin localStorage flag, same pattern as /admin/metrics) that tells the admin 'editing happens via the env var until v1.1 ships'.\n\n3. EXACT, CASE-INSENSITIVE EMAIL MATCH. No wildcards, no domain-level matching. Rationale: domain-level matching ('@example.com') would accidentally let in random employees at a partner company — exactly the kind of leak the gate is meant to prevent. If we ever need broader rules they get added to isEmailAllowed() and every consumer picks them up automatically.\n\n4. TWO ENFORCEMENT POINTS (defense in depth). (a) Pre-signup UX: the /signup page renders a small email-check form FIRST. The user types an email and clicks Continue; the page POSTs to /api/invite-check; if not allowed, the page shows a conversational gate message and never reveals Clerk's SignUp component. If allowed, Clerk's SignUp renders with the approved email pre-filled via initialValues. (b) Post-signup webhook: in /api/webhooks/clerk, when a user.created event lands we re-run isEmailAllowed() against the user's primary email. If not allowed, we call Clerk's REST API (DELETE /v1/users/[userId]) with CLERK_SECRET_KEY to remove the account, log the block ('Blocked unauthorized signup: [email] (userId=[id]) — account deletion requested.'), and return 200 so Clerk doesn't retry. Rationale: an attacker (or a curious tester) who saves the Clerk-hosted signup URL could bypass the page UI; the webhook is the backstop that makes the gate real.\n\n5. PRE-SIGNUP UX = SEPARATE EMAIL-CHECK FORM (NOT INLINE INTERCEPTION OF CLERK). Clerk's prebuilt SignUp component does not expose hooks for intercepting submission, so we don't fight it — we run the gate as step 1 and reveal Clerk as step 2. Trade-off: the user types their email twice (once into the check form, once into Clerk's form, though we pre-fill via initialValues). Acceptable for a friend-and-family beta; cleaner than a brittle attempt to wedge inside Clerk's flow. Reversible: when Clerk's SDK exposes an onBeforeCreateUser hook (or similar), the check moves inline and the two-step UX collapses.\n\n6. CONVERSATIONAL GATE MESSAGE (helpful, not vague). The message reads: 'IdeaM is in invite-only beta right now. Your email isn't on the invite list yet — drop us a note at hello@2ndbrainware.com and we'll get you in.' Conventional UX (helpful, gives a next step) wins over conventional security (vague, doesn't reveal who is/isn't on the list). Rationale: at friend-and-family beta volume, the email-enumeration risk is low and the cost of a frustrated tester unable to figure out why they can't sign up is high. Locked in src/lib/access/allowlist.ts as GATE_MESSAGE so swapping in a vaguer message later is a one-line change.\n\n7. WELCOME-EMAIL ORDERING FIX. The welcome email send in /api/webhooks/clerk previously ran unconditionally on user.created. It now runs ONLY after the allowlist check passes. Rationale: don't welcome a user we're about to delete. The send.ts module itself is unchanged — only the call-site ordering moved.\n\n8. STUB-SAFE PATTERN (matches Sentry / Clerk / Stripe / Resend). With INVITE_ALLOWLIST unset OR empty, isEmailAllowed() returns true for everyone and logs a single dev warning the first time it's called. Both consumers therefore behave exactly like a normal Clerk sign-up flow until Howard sets the env var. Once set, the gate goes live in production with no code change.\n\n9. CLERK DELETE-USER STUB BEHAVIOUR. With CLERK_SECRET_KEY unset, the deleter logs a loud warning ('CLERK_SECRET_KEY not set — cannot delete unauthorized user. The account will remain in Clerk until manually removed.') and returns ok:false. The webhook still returns 200 (don't trigger retries). Rationale: in stub mode there's no Clerk to delete from anyway; in production the secret will be set. The loud warning surfaces in Sentry if the secret is ever accidentally unset in production.\n\n10. RATE LIMIT ON /api/invite-check. 20 requests per minute per IP via the existing enforceRateLimit helper. Rationale: a vague gate message could be flipped to in the future and a generous-but-finite rate limit avoids the route becoming an email-enumeration oracle. Cheap insurance.\n\n11. NO DOMAIN-LEVEL RESERVATION. Considered allowing 'hjachter@gmail.com' alone to grant Howard a permanent slot. Decided against — Howard's own email goes in INVITE_ALLOWLIST like every other approved email. Treats Howard the same as every other user, which is simpler and avoids special cases.\n\n12. NO ANGLE BRACKETS IN TSX STRINGS (per memory). Every reference to a userId / hostname placeholder in TSX string literals uses [placeholder] notation. The help-chat-dialog.tsx APP_CONTEXT addition explicitly uses '[1]' / '[2]' for the enforcement-point bullets and 'https://[host]/api/webhooks/clerk' in the Clerk route comment block (the route comment is a TS file so safer either way, but kept consistent).\n\nREVERSAL: Delete src/lib/access/, src/app/api/invite-check/, src/app/admin/invites/. Revert src/app/signup/[[...rest]]/page.tsx to render Clerk's SignUp directly without the InviteGate wrapper. Revert src/app/api/webhooks/clerk/route.ts to remove the isEmailAllowed() check and the activeDeleter machinery, restoring the unconditional sendWelcomeEmail call. Remove the INVITE-ONLY ACCESS section from both APP_CONTEXTs and the invite-only paragraph from the 'Accounts and sign-in' node in initial-guide.ts. Remove the new node from this outline + the How-it-works outline. Env vars in .env.example are harmless to leave in place.\n\nFILES TOUCHED:\n- src/lib/access/allowlist.ts (NEW — isEmailAllowed, getAllowedEmails, GATE_MESSAGE, _resetAllowlistWarningForTest)\n- src/app/api/invite-check/route.ts (NEW — POST endpoint returning { allowed, message })\n- src/app/signup/[[...rest]]/page.tsx (rewrote — two-step InviteGate wrapping Clerk's SignUp)\n- src/app/api/webhooks/clerk/route.ts (added allowlist check + Clerk REST delete + _setClerkDeleterForTest hook)\n- src/app/admin/invites/page.tsx (NEW — stub page, isAdmin gate, edit-env-var instructions)\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts ('Accounts and sign-in' node opens with the invite-only paragraph)\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx (APP_CONTEXT: new INVITE-ONLY ACCESS section)\n- src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT: same section)\n- .env.example (new INVITE_ALLOWLIST env var with comments)\n- tests/invite-only-test.js (NEW — Playwright suite, parse-checked only; not run this turn due to Electron-in-use)\n\nQUALITY GATE: npx tsc --noEmit — verified zero new errors in the touched files. Pre-existing errors elsewhere in the codebase are unrelated.\n\nWHAT HOWARD NEEDS TO DO TO TURN THIS ON: 1) Set INVITE_ALLOWLIST in the Vercel project env vars to a comma-separated list of approved emails (his own email plus each tester's email). 2) Confirm CLERK_SECRET_KEY is set in Vercel so the webhook can delete unauthorized accounts. 3) Redeploy. 4) Share the /signup link with each approved tester. To add a new tester later: edit INVITE_ALLOWLIST, redeploy, share the link.\n\nOUTLINE SAFETY: This entry is written to docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm only because Howard's Electron is running on port 9002. A manual sync to ~/Documents/IDM Outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm is required once the active outline is not Development. The How-it-works outline mirror was updated and synced this turn (the Authentication node already had a paragraph appended describing the invite-only mechanism).",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-auth-wall-2f737d6d": {
      "id": "decision-auth-wall-2f737d6d",
      "name": "2026-06-05 - Auth wall gates /app and /admin behind sign-in",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Protect the IP and capture leads by requiring authentication to use the actual outliner, while keeping marketing pages public for SEO and the conversion funnel.</p><p><strong>Public routes (no auth):</strong> /, /marketing, /pricing, /privacy, /upgrade, /upgrade/success, /upgrade/cancel, /stress-test, /unsubscribe, /signin, /signup, /api/webhooks/* (Clerk / Stripe), /api/billing/webhook, /api/health, /api/emails/unsubscribe.</p><p><strong>Auth-required routes:</strong> /app/*, /admin/*, /api/help-chat, /api/knowledge-chat, /api/synthesize-podcast, /api/generate-podcast, /api/generate-podcast-script, /api/extract-pdf, /api/billing/checkout, /api/billing/portal.</p><p><strong>Stub-safe pattern.</strong> When CLERK_SECRET_KEY or NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is unset, the middleware logs a single dev warning and lets everyone through. The Account avatar in the toolbar simply doesn't render. The moment both env vars are set in Vercel, the wall goes live in production with no code change. Matches the Sentry integration philosophy.</p><p><strong>Homepage CTA design.</strong> Hero shows <em>Sign up to try IdeaM free</em> (primary violet-to-indigo gradient) with a secondary <em>I already have an account</em> text link, both gated by Clerk's SignedOut wrapper. Already-signed-in visitors see <em>Open IdeaM</em> routing to /app instead, gated by SignedIn. Existing hero copy and the rest of the marketing page untouched.</p><p><strong>Post-signup redirect:</strong> /app. <strong>Post-signin redirect:</strong> /app, with redirect_url preserved across the signup / signin transition so the user lands on whatever they were originally trying to open. <strong>Sign-out destination:</strong> / (homepage).</p><p><strong>API route gating policy.</strong> Centralized in src/middleware.ts at the edge - a single source of truth. API endpoints that touch user content return 401 JSON instead of an HTML redirect. Webhooks stay public (verified by signing secrets, not user auth).</p><p><strong>UserButton placement.</strong> Top-right of the app toolbar in src/components/outline-pane.tsx, immediately after the Wrench admin dropdown. Renders nothing in stub mode.</p><p><strong>Files touched.</strong> Added: src/middleware.ts, src/app/signin/[[...rest]]/page.tsx, src/app/signup/[[...rest]]/page.tsx, src/lib/auth/signed-gates.tsx, src/lib/auth/user-button.tsx, tests/auth-wall-test.js. Modified: src/app/page.tsx (hero CTA), src/components/outline-pane.tsx (UserButton), src/lib/tier-detection.ts (getServerUserId + isAuthAware helpers), src/lib/initial-guide.ts (Accounts and sign-in section), src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx + src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT).</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "5cd29e01-dd3d-4ccc-a3d2-440661b5559b",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-paste-text-fidelity-2026-06-05": {
      "id": "decision-paste-text-fidelity-2026-06-05",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Paste behavior reversed to text-fidelity)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Replace the markdown-on-paste behavior shipped earlier today with a text-fidelity paste behavior. When the user pastes plain text (or plain text with a cosmetic Terminal-style HTML twin on the clipboard), the editor shows the text exactly as copied — every line break preserved, blank lines preserved, no double vertical spacing, and no interpretation of markdown markers like <code>#</code>, <code>**</code>, <code>-</code>, or <code>[link](url)</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Howard's verbatim ask: 'I would be satisfied with just text to text… When I pasted, it looked like the text that I originally copied. We don't really have to do any fancy formatting to get that.' Three iterations of the markdown-rendering path failed to satisfy this simpler goal. Text fidelity is cheaper to reason about and matches the way most copy/paste UX is expected to behave outside specialized editors.</p><p><strong>What changed.</strong> In <code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> the paste handler had two markdown blocks: a MARKDOWN-FIRST GATE (added earlier 2026-06-05) and a lower MARKDOWN-ON-PASTE block. Both are now replaced by a single TEXT-FIDELITY GATE that runs before the rich-HTML preservation branch. The gate fires when the clipboard's HTML twin (if any) is absent, contains the macOS Terminal signature 'Cocoa HTML Writer', or is a thin styled-span wrapper (stripped-tag length is at least 80% of total HTML length, indicating no real structure). Genuinely structural HTML — real tables, real headings, real lists, embedded style blocks — is allowed through to the existing Rich Email HTML preservation path, which is unchanged.</p><p><strong>Paragraph CSS.</strong> Not touched. Existing <code>.tiptap p</code> margin is already <code>0.5em 0</code>, which renders cleanly without doubled spacing. Headings, lists, and other block margins are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Verification.</strong> Playwright probe in web mode (headless chromium against localhost:9002) covered two scenarios: plain-text-only paste, and plain-text + cosmetic Terminal HTML paste. Both produce identical visual output: markdown markers stay literal, blank source lines render as a single blank line, no <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> in the resulting editor HTML. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool, not just assertion checks. Probe deleted after use per scratch-script convention.</p><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> In <code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code>, restore the previous MARKDOWN-FIRST GATE and lower MARKDOWN-ON-PASTE block. Both relied on a <code>looksLikeMarkdown</code> heuristic plus <code>marked.parse</code> (the <code>marked</code> import is still present in the file). Search for the comment block beginning 'TEXT-FIDELITY GATE (2026-06-05, replaces earlier markdown-on-paste)' to find the section to swap.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-reformat-with-ai-2026-06-05": {
      "id": "decision-reformat-with-ai-2026-06-05",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Reformat with AI)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Shipped <em>Reformat with AI…</em> — a single-shot AI transform for one node's content (or just a selection) driven by a plain-language instruction.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-05</p><p><strong>Context:</strong> Howard already does this externally — paste content into an AI, type \"turn this into a bulleted list,\" paste it back. Bringing the same workflow into the app removes the round-trip and keeps content in the editor's HTML the whole way.</p><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Architecture:</strong> NOT built on the LIVE BOOKS / Translate transform engine. That engine is for subtree fan-out (one transform applied to N nodes). Reformat operates on exactly ONE HTML fragment in / one HTML fragment out, so it skips the engine and uses a flat <code>reformatContentAction → reformatContent</code> flow. Same shape as <code>translateNodeContent</code> (no web grounding, no citations) but no preview engine, no per-node skip rules.</p></li><li><p><strong>Naming:</strong> \"Reformat with AI…\" in all menu surfaces; dialog title <em>Reformat content</em>. Subtitle: \"Tell me how to reformat what's in this node (or the selected text). I'll show you the result before applying.\"</p></li><li><p><strong>Reach surfaces (four):</strong> Smart Tools menu (between Quick Command and Generate Subtree, gated on hasSelectedNode), editor right-click context menu (under the existing Generate AI Content entry, gated on !isRoot), the floating formatting toolbar (bubble menu) when text is selected, and the Cmd+K command palette. Icon throughout: <code>WandSparkles</code> in violet.</p></li><li><p><strong>Scope rules:</strong> Bubble menu / context menu WITH a selection → reformats just the selection (uses ProseMirror's DOMSerializer to extract selected HTML; replaces via <code>deleteRange + insertContentAt</code>). Smart Tools menu OR no selection → reformats the whole node (parent's onApply writes <code>node.content</code>).</p></li><li><p><strong>Dialog UX:</strong> Three phases — input → running → preview. Six example chips populate the input on click: \"Turn into a bulleted list\", \"Make each line a heading\", \"Convert to a markdown table\", \"Tighten spacing and remove empty lines\", \"Add headings where it makes sense\", \"Convert to clean prose\". Preview is side-by-side Before / After (not a diff — full content view). Buttons: Apply / Modify instruction / Cancel. Modify returns to input with the instruction preserved.</p></li><li><p><strong>Tier gate:</strong> Added <code>'reformat'</code> to <code>AIFeatureKey</code> in <code>use-ai-usage-gate.tsx</code>. NOT Pro-only — available on Free trial, Student, Pro, and free with BYOK. One reformat = 1 generation, regardless of content length.</p></li><li><p><strong>Output constraint:</strong> The AI prompt restricts output to a Tiptap-safe HTML subset (<code>p, h1-h6, ul, ol, li, strong, em, code, pre, br, blockquote, hr, a</code>). The dialog further sanitises with DOMPurify before applying. The model is told to prefer <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> for tabular data since Tiptap's StarterKit doesn't render <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Error tone:</strong> Conversational copy throughout (per <em>project-natural-language-error-tone</em>). \"I couldn't reformat that. [reason]. You can try a different wording, switch to local AI, or check your API key in Settings.\" The \"no change\" path also surfaces in plain language: \"The model thought the content was already in that format and didn't change anything. Try a more specific instruction.\"</p></li><li><p><strong>Local AI option:</strong> Same checkbox as Translate (\"Use local AI (Ollama)\"). Falls back to local automatically if cloud fails (mirrors translate-node-content).</p></li><li><p><strong>Apply path:</strong> The parent (outline-pro) holds two pieces of dialog state: <code>reformatSelectionHtml</code> (string or null) + <code>reformatApplySelectionFn</code> (replacer fn or null). If both are set the dialog was opened from a selection and <code>handleApplyReformat</code> calls the replacer (which lives in content-pane, drives Tiptap directly). Otherwise it patches the node's <code>content</code> in the outlines state. Both paths flow through normal save/autosave.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong> <code>src/ai/flows/reformat-content.ts</code> (new), <code>src/components/reformat-dialog.tsx</code> (new), <code>src/app/actions.ts</code>, <code>src/components/ai-menu.tsx</code>, <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code>, <code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code>, <code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code>, <code>src/components/command-palette.tsx</code>, <code>src/lib/use-ai-usage-gate.tsx</code>.</p><p><strong>Docs synced:</strong> <code>initial-guide.ts</code> (new node + Smart Tools listing update), both help-chat <code>APP_CONTEXT</code> strings, <em>How it works</em> outline (AI Features parent content), and this Decisions Log entry. ✓</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Delete the two new files, drop the action wrapper, remove <code>'reformat'</code> from <code>AIFeatureKey</code>, and remove the menu / context-menu / bubble-menu entries. The Translate path is unaffected.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-discovery-two-tier-dismissal-2026-06-05": {
      "id": "decision-discovery-two-tier-dismissal-2026-06-05",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Discovery Hints: two-tier dismissal refinement)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> The first cut of Discovery Hints (same date, earlier entry) treated &ldquo;Got it&rdquo; as a one-shot permanent dismissal and used Professional-mode-OFF as a heavy hammer that wiped the entire dismissed list. Howard refined the model after wearing the new-user shoes — one dismissal isn&rsquo;t enough; some tips need to surface a few times before they stick, and the user should be the one to decide when a tip is &ldquo;forever done.&rdquo;</p><p><strong>What changed:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Two-tier dismissal on every &ldquo;Did You Know?&rdquo; card.</strong> A new <em>&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t show me this again&rdquo;</em> checkbox sits beside the &ldquo;Got it&rdquo; button. (a) Clicking Got it with the checkbox UNCHECKED is a <strong>soft dismiss</strong> — the toast closes; the same hint is eligible to fire again on the next trigger occurrence. (b) Checking the box and clicking Got it is a <strong>hard dismiss</strong> — the hint id is appended to a persistent never-show list and the tip never appears again, even if Professional mode is later switched off.</li><li><strong>Storage rework.</strong> The single <em>discovery:dismissedHints</em> bucket is split: only <em>discovery:hardDismissedHints</em> persists (plus the existing <em>discovery:professionalMode</em>). There is intentionally no persisted soft-dismissed set — soft just means &ldquo;not in the active queue right now,&rdquo; and the natural state machine re-enqueues on the next trigger fire. Tiny migration: on first read, if the legacy <em>discovery:dismissedHints</em> key is present, all of its ids are promoted to <em>hardDismissedHints</em> (safest interpretation — the user already dismissed those tips) and the legacy key is removed.</li><li><strong>Professional mode OFF simplified.</strong> Previously it wiped the dismissed list entirely. Now it just unsets the suppression flag; future triggers can fire again, but the hard-dismissed list is untouched. Anything the user explicitly marked &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t show again&rdquo; stays hidden forever.</li><li><strong>New Pro-mode-OFF confirmation copy.</strong> Title: <em>&ldquo;Welcome tips re-enabled.&rdquo;</em> Body: <em>&ldquo;You&rsquo;ll see them on next-trigger — except the ones you marked &lsquo;Don&rsquo;t show again.&rsquo;&rdquo;</em> — keeps the toast conversational (per the natural-language-error-tone memory) and accurately describes the new semantics.</li></ol><p><strong>Why this design (rationale):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Realistic learning model.</strong> A single &ldquo;Got it&rdquo; treats users as having photographic memory. Two tiers respect the reality that a card may need to surface a handful of times before the tip really lands.</li><li><strong>User-controlled permanence.</strong> The user — not the framework — decides when a hint is permanently gone. The checkbox is the explicit consent gesture.</li><li><strong>No state explosion.</strong> By not persisting soft-dismissed state, the state machine has exactly two persistent pieces (hard-dismissed list + Pro flag). Easy to reason about, easy to migrate to a per-user DB row later.</li><li><strong>Pro-mode-OFF respects hard-dismissals.</strong> Wiping the dismissed list on every Pro-mode-OFF would punish users who flip Pro mode for unrelated reasons (e.g. screen sharing, demo recording). The new behaviour treats Professional mode as a master switch, not a reset button.</li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong> <em>src/lib/discovery/hints.ts</em> (registry comment update), <em>src/hooks/use-discovery.tsx</em> (storage split + migration + DismissOptions signature), <em>src/components/discovery-toast.tsx</em> (checkbox UI + per-card local state). Plus the documentation quartet: <em>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</em>, both APP_CONTEXT strings, and the &ldquo;How it works&rdquo; outline.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Restoring the previous behaviour means (a) collapsing <em>dismissHint(id, options?)</em> back to <em>dismissHint(id)</em> with permanent semantics, (b) restoring the single-key storage backend, and (c) re-introducing the Pro-mode-OFF dismissed-list wipe. The toast component would lose its <em>DiscoveryToastCard</em> sub-component and the checkbox row.</p>",
      "type": "node",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-discovery-hints-2026-06-05": {
      "id": "decision-discovery-hints-2026-06-05",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-05 (Discovery Hints system + Reformat polish)",
      "content": "<p><strong>What we built today (Howard direction, Claude implementation):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Discovery Hints system</strong> — a reusable, app-wide framework for surfacing &ldquo;Did You Know?&rdquo; tips to new users.</li><li><strong>Reformat-with-AI bubble-menu polish</strong> — moved to the first position with a violet accent and a &ldquo;Most Powerful&rdquo; badge.</li><li><strong>Five v1 hints registered</strong> covering the highest-leverage feature discoveries.</li></ol><p><strong>1) Discovery Hints architecture</strong></p><ul><li>Registry as a typed const array in <em>src/lib/discovery/hints.ts</em>. Each hint has <em>id</em> (stable, also the dismissal key), <em>title</em>, <em>body</em>, <em>trigger</em> (string-keyed event), optional <em>minDelayMs</em>, optional <em>learnMoreUrl</em>.</li><li>v1 trigger set: <em>editor-first-text-selection</em>, <em>editor-first-paste</em>, <em>sidebar-first-load</em>, <em>first-outline-created</em>, <em>first-byok-prompt-encountered</em>, <em>first-cross-link-created</em>, <em>smart-tools-menu-opened</em>.</li><li>Hook + React provider in <em>src/hooks/use-discovery.tsx</em>. Tiny EventTarget bus so call sites can fire triggers without threading the provider. Global <em>fireDiscovery(trigger)</em> helper exported; debug helper exposed on <em>window.__fireDiscovery</em> in dev builds.</li><li>Sticky toast renderer in <em>src/components/discovery-toast.tsx</em>. Lower-right corner, violet eyebrow + sparkle. NEVER auto-dismisses — only the &ldquo;Got it&rdquo; button removes it. Existing transient Toaster is untouched.</li><li>Storage: localStorage today, behind a small <em>DiscoveryStorage</em> interface. Swap to a per-user DB row once Clerk userIds are wired — call sites stay identical.</li></ul><p><strong>2) Hint copy (5 hints)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>reformat-with-ai</strong> (trigger: first text selection) — the headline hint. Points at the violet wand at the left end of the bubble menu.</li><li><strong>smart-tools-menu</strong> (trigger: first text selection, 6s stagger) — points at the sparkle icon in the toolbar.</li><li><strong>cross-outline-link</strong> (trigger: first outline created, 3s delay) — points at Import → Link to Outline.</li><li><strong>import-export-toolbar</strong> (trigger: first outline created, 10s delay) — points at the book-down / book-up icons.</li><li><strong>byok-unlimited</strong> (trigger: first BYOK prompt) — explains the unlimited-with-own-key path.</li></ul><p><strong>3) Reformat bubble-menu polish</strong></p><ul><li>Moved from the <strong>right end</strong> of the bubble menu to the <strong>first (leftmost) position</strong>.</li><li>Visual: violet-tinted background (<em>bg-violet-500/10</em>) with a ring (<em>ring-violet-500/30</em>) so it visually pops without breaking the bubble menu&rsquo;s rhythm. Button size bumped to 9x9 (vs 8x8 for the rest) so it&rsquo;s a slightly larger touch target.</li><li>Inline pill labelled &ldquo;Most Powerful&rdquo; (<em>uppercase tracking-wide</em>) sits next to the button. Hidden on the smallest mobile widths to keep the menu from wrapping.</li></ul><p><strong>4) Professional mode</strong></p><ul><li>New Settings → Tips → &ldquo;Professional mode&rdquo; toggle. Off by default. Flipping ON immediately clears the visible queue AND prevents future hints. Flipping OFF re-enables future hints (already-dismissed hints stay dismissed).</li></ul><p><strong>Why this design (rationale):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sticky toasts (no auto-dismiss):</strong> a new-user tip must survive looking away, taking a phone call, or reading the explanation twice. Auto-dismiss after 5 seconds defeats the purpose. The existing transient toast system stays untouched for normal feedback.</li><li><strong>Lower-right corner:</strong> matches the standard toast position so users learn one mental model for &ldquo;notifications come from there.&rdquo; The discovery cards are visually distinct (violet eyebrow + sparkle) so users can tell them apart from regular toasts at a glance.</li><li><strong>Staggered fires:</strong> showing 4 tips on first selection would feel like a tutorial avalanche. <em>minDelayMs</em> lets us tell a sequenced story.</li><li><strong>Reformat as headline hint:</strong> Reformat-with-AI is the highest-leverage AI surface — it&rsquo;s the &ldquo;wand&rdquo; that converts plain language into structured content. Surfacing it the moment a user selects text is the maximum-leverage discovery moment.</li><li><strong>localStorage now, DB later:</strong> ship today without blocking on auth; the swap is a one-file change.</li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong></p><ul><li>To disable all hints app-wide: comment out <em>&lt;DiscoveryToastStack /&gt;</em> in <em>src/app/layout.tsx</em>. The registry &amp; hooks stay inert.</li><li>To remove a single hint: delete its entry from the array in <em>src/lib/discovery/hints.ts</em>. Already-dismissed users won&rsquo;t notice; users who haven&rsquo;t seen it simply never will.</li><li>To revert the Reformat polish: restore the original ordering at the end of the BubbleMenu and remove the badge block at the start in <em>src/components/content-pane.tsx</em>.</li></ul>",
      "type": "node",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-expand-collapse-merge-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-expand-collapse-merge-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Expand All + Collapse All merged into one dropdown)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Merge the two side-by-side toolbar buttons (Expand All down-chevron + Collapse All up-chevron) into a single dropdown button. The trigger is the bidirectional <code>ChevronsDownUp</code> icon (chevrons pointing both directions). Its tooltip reads <em>Show or hide all nodes</em>. The dropdown contains two items: <em>Expand all</em> (top) and <em>Collapse all</em> (below), each showing its keyboard shortcut as a <code>DropdownMenuShortcut</code>.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-06</p><p><strong>Howard's prompt:</strong> \"We can also combine the expand all and contract all buttons under a single button.\"</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduces toolbar count by one and groups two opposite-direction actions that previously sat side-by-side. The bidirectional double-chevron icon is the lucide-react idiom for \"both expand and collapse\" — readable at a glance. The tooltip is intentionally plain English (describes the effect, not the mechanism) per the project's two-words-or-fewer label rule + tooltip-for-explanations convention.</p><p><strong>Tooltip wording — \"Show or hide all nodes\":</strong> Chose effect-language over mechanism-language. \"Expand/Collapse\" is mechanism vocabulary; \"Show or hide\" is what the user actually sees happen. Same aria-label.</p><p><strong>Icon choice — ChevronsDownUp:</strong> The lucide-react icon with chevrons pointing both down and up — visually signals bidirectionality. Menu items keep the original single-direction icons (<code>ChevronsDown</code> for Expand, <code>ChevronsUp</code> for Collapse) so the visual association from the previous two-button layout carries forward into the dropdown.</p><p><strong>Order — Expand first, Collapse second:</strong> Expand is the more common action when someone has just opened an outline. Expand-then-Collapse also reads naturally left-to-right / top-to-bottom.</p><p><strong>Keyboard shortcuts UNCHANGED:</strong> Cmd+E still fires Expand All, Cmd+Shift+E still fires Collapse All. The global keydown listener in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> is independent of the toolbar JSX, so the button-merge has zero impact on the shortcut path. The shortcuts also appear as <code>DropdownMenuShortcut</code> hints in the menu items so discoverability is preserved.</p><p><strong>Code change scope:</strong> <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> only — added <code>ChevronsDownUp</code> to the lucide-react import, added <code>DropdownMenuShortcut</code> to the dropdown-menu import, replaced the two Tooltip+Button blocks with a single DropdownMenu trigger + DropdownMenuContent. <code>handleExpandAll</code> and <code>handleCollapseAll</code> in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Documentation (mandatory five places):</strong> User Guide node \"Expand All / Collapse All\" rewritten to describe the dropdown; help-chat-dialog APP_CONTEXT updated; help-chat route APP_CONTEXT mirrored; <em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> toolbar enumeration now lists \"Show or hide all nodes (bidirectional double-chevron)\"; this Decisions Log entry.</p><p><strong>Visual verification:</strong> short headless Playwright probe against the running dev server confirmed: exactly one bidirectional-chevron toolbar button present (not two), tooltip text matches, dropdown opens with both items in expected order with keyboard hints visible, clicking each item triggers the expected recursive expand/collapse, and the bare Cmd+E / Cmd+Shift+E shortcuts (no menu) still work. Probe deleted after passing.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Restore the two Tooltip+Button blocks in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code> from git, drop the new DropdownMenu, and remove <code>ChevronsDownUp</code> + <code>DropdownMenuShortcut</code> from the imports. The keyboard handlers and the underlying expand/collapse functions don't change.</p>"
    },
    "decision-responsive-toolbar-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-responsive-toolbar-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Responsive toolbar + Import as Chapter deletion)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Responsive toolbar + Import as Chapter deletion)</strong></p><p><strong>Why these two changes ship together:</strong> both clean up the toolbar to make it work at every screen size. One removes dead code; the other adds proper responsive behavior.</p><p><strong>1. Deleted \"Import as Chapter.\"</strong></p><ul><li><strong>What was removed:</strong> the <code>handleImportAsChapter</code> callback in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code>, the <code>onImportAsChapter</code> prop on <code>OutlinePane</code>, and the commented-out trigger UI in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>. No remaining references anywhere in <code>src/</code>.</li><li><strong>Why:</strong> copy/paste already covers the same intent (selecting a subtree from one outline and pasting it into another), is more intuitive (every desktop user already knows how to copy/paste), and works across more contexts. The Import as Chapter trigger had been commented out in the UI for an unknown amount of time — no one missed it. Howard authorized the deletion.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Responsive toolbar with overflow menu.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Problem:</strong> the toolbar carries ~14-17 visible icon buttons. On phone (< 640px) they spill off the right edge; on tablet (640-1023px) they crowd uncomfortably; on desktop (≥ 1024px) they fit fine. A static toolbar can't make all three work.</li><li><strong>Design:</strong> three-tier priority system with progressive disclosure. Anchors (sidebar toggle, account avatar) are always visible. Tier 1 (Ask AI, Plus, Delete, Search) is always visible. Tier 2 (Import, Export, Focus Mode, Show or hide all, Share subtree, Command palette, Second Brain, Smart Tools) is hidden on phone and surfaces on tablet+. Tier 3 (Settings, Help, Refresh User Guide) is hidden on phone+tablet and surfaces on desktop. An overflow icon (Lucide <code>MoreHorizontal</code>, three horizontal dots, tooltip \"More tools\") sits just left of the avatar on phone+tablet only and contains every hidden item. On desktop the overflow disappears entirely (zero items, hidden via <code>lg:hidden</code> on the trigger).</li><li><strong>Breakpoints:</strong> Tailwind's <code>sm</code> (640px) and <code>lg</code> (1024px). Implemented as CSS classes (<code>hidden sm:inline-flex</code>, <code>hidden lg:inline-flex</code>) directly on each <code>Button</code>, no JavaScript breakpoint hook needed — the browser handles viewport changes natively, no flicker on resize, no extra state.</li><li><strong>Priority ranking decisions:</strong> I stuck closely to the proposed order with one nuance — Ask AI lives in the title row (always visible) since it's the highest-frequency action and the conversational entry point. Plus, Delete, Search live in the secondary action row as Tier 1. Focus Mode is Tier 2 (not phone-visible) because users on phones tend to be reading rather than restructuring. Smart Tools (AIMenu, sparkles) is Tier 2 — it's a power-user surface, fine to put one tap behind the overflow on phone. Refresh User Guide is grouped with Settings/Help in Tier 3 since it's an administrative action.</li><li><strong>Settings dialog trigger from overflow:</strong> the existing <code>SettingsDialog</code> component owns its own open state and wraps a child button. To trigger it from the overflow menu, the overflow item calls <code>document.querySelector('[data-settings-trigger]').click()</code> on the hidden inline Settings button. <code>HTMLElement.click()</code> fires regardless of CSS visibility, so this works at every viewport. Pragmatic and contained — no API change to <code>SettingsDialog</code> needed.</li><li><strong>Overflow icon choice:</strong> <code>MoreHorizontal</code> (three horizontal dots) over <code>MoreVertical</code> for two reasons — it visually echoes the horizontal row of buttons it represents, and on iPad/iPhone three-horizontal-dots is more recognizable as \"more\" than three-vertical-dots (which often means a per-item context menu).</li><li><strong>Window resize mid-session:</strong> behaves smoothly because every visibility decision is pure CSS. Drag the window narrower and buttons disappear into the overflow as breakpoints cross. No state to reset, no flicker, no double-rendering.</li><li><strong>Visual verification:</strong> drove a scratch Playwright probe (since deleted) at three viewports — 375×812 (iPhone 14), 820×1180 (iPad), 1440×900 (desktop). At each width, screenshotted the toolbar inline and the overflow menu open. Confirmed Tier 1 always inline, Tier 2 surfaces at tablet+, Tier 3 surfaces at desktop, overflow icon disappears on desktop, anchors stay at the edges. No horizontal overflow at any width, no crowded buttons.</li><li><strong>Keyboard shortcuts unchanged:</strong> Cmd+K, Cmd+F, Cmd+E, Cmd+Shift+E, Cmd+Shift+F, Cmd+Shift+I, etc. fire regardless of whether the matching button is inline or hidden — they're bound at the document level, not on the Button DOM.</li><li><strong>TypeScript:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports the same 52 pre-existing errors before and after; zero new errors introduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong> <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> (toolbar markup + new overflow menu + dead-code purge), <code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code> (handleImportAsChapter deletion + prop-passing sites), <code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> (Toolbar Icons node rewritten), <code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> + <code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> (APP_CONTEXT entries duplicated), <code>IdeaM - How it works.idm</code> (new Toolbar — Responsive Tiered Layout node), <code>IdeaM - Development.idm</code> (this entry).</p>",
      "type": "item",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": []
    },
    "decision-stack-canonicalization-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-stack-canonicalization-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Canonicalized tech stack & platform strategy description)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Canonicalized tech stack &amp; platform strategy description)</strong></p><p><strong>What was canonicalized:</strong> a plain-English description of IdeaM's technology stack — TypeScript end to end (React + Next.js), wrapped in Electron for desktop and Capacitor for iOS, with the explicit \"no Python anywhere\" framing and the \"write once, deploy everywhere\" strategic argument.</p><p><strong>Where each version lives:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Canonical (full version):</strong> <em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> → top-level chapter \"Technology Stack &amp; Platform Strategy\" with six sub-sections (glance, desktop, iOS, web, backend, why this stack).</li><li><strong>Investor framing:</strong> <em>IdeaM-BizDev.idm</em> → \"Technology Stack as Capital Efficiency Lever\" — leads with write-once-deploy-everywhere as a runway-extender and headcount-multiplier.</li><li><strong>Competitive differentiator:</strong> <em>IdeaM - Killer Features &amp; Selling Points.idm</em> → \"Cross-platform parity from day one\" — punchy bullets contrasting against web-only or laggy-native competitors.</li><li><strong>Strategic context:</strong> <em>IdeaM-Planning.idm</em> → \"Tech Stack as Strategic Choice\" placed under Product Strategy (sibling to Technical Debt).</li><li><strong>Operational note:</strong> <em>IdeaM - Bootstrap Plan.idm</em> → \"Tech Stack Note — Why platform expansion is cheap\" — short note framing why expanding to Windows/Linux/Android later does not blow up the bootstrap budget.</li></ul><p><strong>Why keep these in sync:</strong> new team members, investors, and Howard himself all need a consistent answer to \"what is IdeaM built in and why?\". The canonical version is the technical reference; the other four are framing-specific summaries pointing back to it. If facts change (e.g. we add a Python ML service, or migrate off Capacitor), the canonical version updates first, then the summaries follow.</p><p><strong>Process for future updates:</strong> edit the canonical \"How it works\" version first, then re-check that the BizDev / Killer Features / Planning / Bootstrap mirrors don't now contradict it. The mirrors are intentionally lighter than the canonical — they are not full duplicates.</p>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-youtube-coming-soon-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-youtube-coming-soon-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (YouTube video export \"coming soon\" messaging surfaced for launch)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (YouTube video export \"coming soon\" messaging surfaced)</strong></p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> The video generation feature itself stays deferred to v1.1 (per the chapter 17 plan in the Bootstrap Plan outline — slide-narration MVP gated on post-launch podcast adoption). What changes today: we set the customer expectation NOW by surfacing \"YouTube video export coming soon\" messaging across the public website, the in-app User Guide, and the help chat.</p><p><strong>Howard's direction (verbatim, 2026-06-06):</strong> \"We can hold off on the podcast expert until one point one — I agree with your reasoning. And besides the initial version, we'll go mainly to the beta testers. But we should indicate that the YouTube export is coming soon. In our documentation and website.\"</p><p><strong>Where the messaging now lives:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Marketing homepage</strong> (<code>src/app/page.tsx</code>) — added a \"From outline to YouTube video — Coming soon · v1.1\" callout below the Export Anywhere card, in the Features block. Matches the existing card design language (rounded card, gradient, lucide <code>Youtube</code> icon). One callout only, not littered across the page.</p></li><li><p><strong>Marketing landing</strong> (<code>src/app/marketing/page.tsx</code>) — same callout, same copy, placed after the Export &amp; Unmerge row for visual symmetry with the homepage.</p></li><li><p><strong>User Guide</strong> (<code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code>) — the Export Formats node now ends with a \"COMING SOON (v1.1)\" subsection that names the video / YouTube export and points to the Bootstrap Plan chapter 17 for the full plan.</p></li><li><p><strong>Help chat in-app context</strong> (<code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT) — added a sentence directing the help-chat AI to answer YouTube / video / MP4 questions with the v1.1 timeline without claiming the feature ships today.</p></li><li><p><strong>Help chat API context</strong> (<code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT) — same sentence, duplicated to keep both contexts in sync.</p></li><li><p><strong>How It Works outline</strong> — Export Menu node extended with a \"Coming soon (v1.1)\" subsection that mirrors the User Guide copy.</p></li><li><p><strong>Killer Features &amp; Selling Points outline</strong> — added a new top-level \"Coming soon — Roadmap highlights\" node featuring YouTube video export as a marquee upcoming differentiator. Includes the marketing framing, the segment story (creators, educators, sales), and the explicit \"what we are not yet promising\" guardrail.</p></li><li><p><strong>BizDev outline</strong> — the 2026 Product Roadmap node Q2 list now includes the YouTube video export bullet with investor framing (next monetizable surface, justifies Pro upgrade and eventual Studio tier, anticipation as a launch asset).</p></li><li><p><strong>Bootstrap Plan outline</strong> — the Marketing &amp; Outreach chapter now carries a cross-reference note pointing out that influencer outreach + Product Hunt copy should mention \"YouTube video export coming in v1.1\" without overpromising. Chapter 17 itself unchanged.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Copy decisions taken unilaterally (reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Headline:</strong> \"From outline to YouTube video\" — outcome-framed per Howard's value-based naming rule, not mechanism-framed (\"Video generation pipeline\" would have been worse).</p></li><li><p><strong>Body:</strong> \"Generate a fully-narrated, publishable video straight from any outline. Pick a target length, hit Generate, get a YouTube-ready MP4 with AI voiceover and slides. Shipping in v1.1.\" One sentence of value + one sentence of mechanism + one sentence of timeline.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sub-line:</strong> \"Beta testers get early access. Everyone else gets it the moment it ships.\" — Per Howard's direction that beta testers are the main initial distribution channel for video. Sets the right expectation without sounding gated.</p></li><li><p><strong>Visual treatment:</strong> medium-prominence card (matches the Export and Unmerge cards in size and style), placed in a new full-width row immediately below the Export row. Bigger than a chip, smaller than a hero band. Single \"Coming soon · v1.1\" amber pill badge for unmissable timeline signal.</p></li><li><p><strong>Email capture deliberately omitted from v1.</strong> Checked the existing landing-page code path — there is no live waitlist / email-capture infrastructure wired up. Building one just for this callout would be net-new infrastructure I shouldn't take unilaterally. If Howard wants \"Notify me when it ships,\" file a follow-up task to wire up the existing PostHog/email plumbing.</p></li><li><p><strong>Tone calibration per outline:</strong> marketing copy is conversational and excited. User Guide copy is informational and matter-of-fact. Help chat copy is instructional to the AI (\"if a user asks about video, say...\"). BizDev copy is investor framing (monetization angle, tier justification). Killer Features copy is differentiator framing. Bootstrap Plan cross-reference is operational.</p></li></ul><p><strong>What this does NOT change:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Chapter 17 of the Bootstrap Plan — the full video generation plan with slide-narration MVP, AI-enhanced v1.2, Studio tier v2 — is untouched. The plan is still gated on post-launch podcast adoption, not surfaced today.</p></li><li><p>No actual video generation code shipped. This change is messaging only.</p></li><li><p>No new dependencies, no new server routes, no Pro/Studio tier changes.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Why now:</strong> the launch story benefits from a clear forward-looking signal so the v1 product feels like \"the start of something\" rather than \"all we have.\" Beta testers in particular need to see the roadmap to stay engaged. Surfacing the YouTube export gives both the marketing site and the in-app surface that signal without committing engineering hours we don't have until launch ships.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> remove the new card from <code>page.tsx</code> + <code>marketing/page.tsx</code>, drop the COMING SOON paragraph from the Export Formats node in <code>initial-guide.ts</code>, delete the video-related sentence from both help-chat APP_CONTEXT blocks, and either remove the new Killer Features node + BizDev bullet + Bootstrap cross-reference + How It Works subsection or leave them as historical roadmap context (lower risk).</p><p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Export Formats node with v1.1 coming-soon subsection. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — video / YouTube / MP4 v1.1 instruction added. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same instruction, duplicated. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> — Import/Export Menu node extended with the Coming Soon subsection; both copies in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. ✓</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gate:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> shows no NEW errors introduced by this change (the codebase carries ~52 baseline errors unrelated to this work). Visual verification via short web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server confirmed the marketing homepage now renders the \"Coming soon · v1.1\" callout below the Export section.</p>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "node-0zkqntm2-mq2y5mzt": {
      "id": "node-0zkqntm2-mq2y5mzt",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Update-distribution mechanics across canonical platform description)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Update-distribution mechanics added to canonical platform description)</strong></p><p><strong>Why this change:</strong> the canonical platform description (added 2026-06-06 across five outlines) explained the technology stack and cross-platform strategy — but it was silent on how users actually receive updates on each platform. That is a real product dimension, not a technical footnote: an outliner that quietly stops updating is an outliner that gets abandoned. Today's edit fills that gap.</p><p><strong>Sections extended:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IdeaM - How it works.idm:</strong> added a new subsection \"How users get updates on each platform\" inside the existing \"Technology Stack &amp; Platform Strategy\" chapter. This is the primary technical home — full breakdown of web, iOS via TestFlight, iOS via App Store, macOS current state (manual DMG), macOS planned v1.1 (<code>electron-updater</code>), and Windows/Linux future.</li><li><strong>IdeaM-BizDev.idm:</strong> extended \"Technology Stack as Capital Efficiency Lever\" with the strategic framing — web auto-deploys are an instant feedback loop, iOS update friction is asymmetric (developer-side heavy, user-side light), macOS auto-updater is a launch-quality follow-up.</li><li><strong>IdeaM - Killer Features &amp; Selling Points.idm:</strong> extended \"Cross-platform parity from day one\" with user-facing strengths — instant web updates and tap-to-update iOS are real selling points; the macOS auto-updater is flagged as a tracked deferred item.</li><li><strong>IdeaM-Planning.idm:</strong> extended \"Tech Stack as Strategic Choice\" with <code>electron-updater</code> integration positioned as a deliberate post-launch v1.1 priority, sequenced after launch because the manual-DMG path is workable for the initial technical audience.</li><li><strong>IdeaM - Bootstrap Plan.idm:</strong> extended \"Tech Stack Note — Why platform expansion is cheap\" with the operational note — listed dependencies (hosted DMG location, Gatekeeper code-signing, Electron main-process registration code).</li><li><strong>IdeaM - Launch Progress.idm:</strong> added \"macOS desktop auto-updater\" to the Post-launch / deferred chapter; chapter count incremented 9 → 10; overall task count 124/129 → 124/130; overall percentage recomputed 96% → 95%.</li></ul><p><strong>Update-procedure content added (canonical summary):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Web:</strong> instant. Vercel auto-deploys on every push to main; users see new versions on next page refresh. Zero user action.</li><li><strong>iOS via TestFlight (beta path, immediate next step):</strong> Apple reviews briefly (minutes to hours for an existing external group; 24–48 hours for the first build to a new external group). Once approved, testers see the build in their TestFlight app and tap Update (or have auto-install enabled). Friction is developer-side, not user-side.</li><li><strong>iOS via App Store (production path, post-launch):</strong> same friction asymmetry. Developer submits, Apple reviews 24–48 hours, update appears in App Store. Users with auto-updates on (most do) get it in the background.</li><li><strong>macOS desktop (Electron, current state):</strong> manual. Users must download a fresh DMG and install it themselves. No in-app \"Update available\" prompt. This is a real launch-quality gap.</li><li><strong>macOS desktop (planned, post-launch v1.1):</strong> integrate <code>electron-updater</code> to check, download, and prompt \"Restart to update\" automatically. Requires a hosted DMG location, macOS code-signing for Gatekeeper, and a small bit of Electron main-process code.</li><li><strong>Windows / Linux desktop:</strong> not yet distributed. Same <code>electron-updater</code> pattern applies when they ship.</li></ul><p><strong>Why electron-updater is a deferred v1.1 item, not a launch blocker:</strong></p><ul><li>The manual-DMG path is workable for the initial Mac audience — Howard's early Mac users are technical enough to download a new build when notified by email or in-app banner.</li><li>The engineering hours are better spent on launch-blocking work (App Store submission, payment, marketing copy) than on auto-update plumbing before there's a revenue stream to protect.</li><li>The technical path is well-trodden — there is no architectural risk to defer, only sequencing. Once revenue is live, closing this gap is high-value-per-hour work.</li><li>Treating it as a tracked deferred item (now visible in Launch Progress) means it cannot quietly slip out of scope.</li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong> 6 outline files in both <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> and <code>docs/outlines/</code>. All dual-syncs verified byte-for-byte via <code>cmp -s</code>. No code changes.</p>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-toolbar-audit-pt2-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-toolbar-audit-pt2-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Toolbar audit pt 2 — BubbleMenu tooltips + menu label casing)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> Follow-up to the 2026-06-08 toolbar audit pt 1 (which covered the outline-pane top toolbar). Pt 2 covered: (A) the Tiptap BubbleMenu, (B) every dropdown menu's contents (Import, Export, Show/Hide, Second Brain, Smart Tools, More tools overflow), (C) the right-click context menu on tree nodes and sidebar rows, (D) the outline-list sidebar. Audit driven at 1200px and 600px via a disposable Playwright probe.</p><p></p><p><strong>Definite bugs fixed:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>BubbleMenu — 7 of 8 buttons had no tooltip at all.</strong> The floating format toolbar (Bold, Italic, Strikethrough, Inline code, H1, H2, H3) used only <code>aria-label</code> and no <code>Tooltip</code> wrapper. Reformat with AI had only a <code>title=</code> attribute (browser-native, slow). On hover, no styled tooltip appeared — users hovering an unfamiliar icon got nothing. Root cause: the BubbleMenu renders via Floating UI outside the editor toolbar's <code>TooltipProvider</code>, so even if individual <code>Tooltip</code> wrappers existed they would have needed their own provider. Fix: added a dedicated <code>&lt;TooltipProvider delayDuration={300}&gt;</code> wrapping the BubbleMenu's contents and wrapped each of the 8 buttons in <code>Tooltip</code> / <code>TooltipTrigger asChild</code> / <code>TooltipContent</code>. Tooltip strings: \"Reformat with AI\", \"Bold (⌘B)\", \"Italic (⌘I)\", \"Strikethrough\", \"Inline code\", \"Heading 1\", \"Heading 2\", \"Heading 3\". Reformat's old <code>title=\"Reformat with AI…\"</code> attribute was removed since the styled tooltip replaces it. Visual verification: Playwright real-hover on Bold, Reformat, and Heading 1 each surfaced the expected styled tooltip text within ~700ms.</p></li><li><p><strong>More tools overflow menu — label casing was a mishmash.</strong> Side-by-side menu items mixed Title Case (\"Open Second Brain\", \"Quick Capture\", \"Search Second Brain\") with sentence case (\"Import outline\", \"Export outline\", \"Focus Mode\", \"Expand all\", \"Collapse all\", \"Share subtree…\", \"Command palette\", \"Help &amp; support\"). The rest of the codebase uses Title Case consistently. Fix: renamed the sentence-case items to Title Case so the entire menu reads uniformly — \"Import Outline\", \"Export Outline\", \"Expand All\", \"Collapse All\", \"Share Subtree…\", \"Command Palette\", \"Help &amp; Support\". \"Focus Mode\" was already Title Case.</p></li><li><p><strong>Show/Hide all nodes dropdown — same casing issue.</strong> The tree-toolbar Show-or-hide dropdown had \"Expand all\" / \"Collapse all\" while the context menu and other surfaces use \"Expand All\" / \"Collapse All\". Renamed for consistency.</p></li><li><p><strong>More tools overflow — \"Smart Tools…\" item was mislabeled.</strong> The overflow item's handler is <code>onOpenKnowledgeChat</code>, which opens \"Ask Your Outlines\" (matching the Smart Tools dropdown's own row by that name). \"Smart Tools…\" suggested it opened the Smart Tools menu itself, which it does not. Renamed to \"Ask Your Outlines\" so the label matches the actual destination — value-based naming per CLAUDE.md (\"name what it DOES, not how it works\").</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>No issues found in these surfaces (worth recording so we don't re-audit unnecessarily):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Import dropdown:</strong> all items (\"Research &amp; Import\", \"Import Outline\", \"Link to Outline…\", \"Restore All Outlines\", \"Refresh from Web\") read cleanly in plain English. \"Refresh from Web\" is the renamed value-based label for what used to be \"LIVE BOOKS\" internally — the user sees what it does, not the codename. Disabled-state tooltip on \"Refresh from Web\" surfaces the reason via <code>title=</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Export dropdown:</strong> all items clean (\"Share Subtree as…\", \"Export Current Outline\", \"Backup All Outlines\"). \"Share Subtree\" disabled state surfaces \"Select a node first\" via <code>title=</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Second Brain dropdown:</strong> all items clean and consistent Title Case (\"Open Second Brain\", \"Quick Capture\", \"Save Selection to Second Brain\", \"Search Second Brain\", \"View Dashboard\", \"Import to Second Brain\").</p></li><li><p><strong>Smart Tools dropdown:</strong> labels are value-named (\"Quick Command\", \"Reformat with AI…\", \"Transform outline with AI…\", \"Generate Subtree from Topic\", \"Translate this section\", \"Ask Your Outlines\"). \"Ask AI\" — the banned phrase — does not appear. Reformat / Translate only render when a node is selected (correct gating).</p></li><li><p><strong>Tree-node right-click context menu (read in source):</strong> labels are consistent Title Case and value-named (\"Add Sibling Node\", \"Insert Link to Outline…\", \"Rename Node\", \"Expand\" / \"Collapse\", \"Expand All\" / \"Collapse All\", \"Create Content for Descendants\", \"Share Subtree As...\", \"Save to Second Brain\", \"Copy Subtree\", \"Move Subtree\" — renamed from \"Cut Subtree\" already, \"Paste Subtree\", \"Duplicate\", \"Delete Node\", \"Properties...\"). The read-only User Guide correctly hides the destructive items.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sidebar right-click + ellipsis dropdown on outline rows:</strong> just \"Rename\" and \"Delete\" — minimal, unambiguous. Both surfaces show the same two items, consistent with each other.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sidebar User Guide row:</strong> correctly has no right-click menu, no delete button, no rename trigger — matches the 2026-06-07 read-only lock.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Design questions DEFERRED to Howard (not fixed; need a product call):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>BubbleMenu has no Link / Underline buttons.</strong> Many editors offer Link insertion (Ctrl+K-style) and Underline directly on the format toolbar. The BubbleMenu currently has Reformat + Bold + Italic + Strike + Code + 3 headings. The <code>Link</code> Tiptap extension is imported but no toolbar button surfaces it. Question: do you want Link and/or Underline buttons in the BubbleMenu, or is that intentional minimalism? Recommendation: add Link at minimum (Ctrl+K behavior) — links are a writing primitive. Underline is more debatable (web typography traditionally uses italics for emphasis; underline = hyperlink in most readers' visual grammar). Trade-off: each new button costs ~32px of width, which matters at narrow Electron widths where the menu already wraps.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sidebar quick-action buttons (\"New Outline\", \"User Guide\", \"Welcome Tour\") have visible text labels but no <code>Tooltip</code> wrappers.</strong> Per CLAUDE.md, every button should have explanation in a tooltip. But these buttons already have full visible text labels, so the value of an additional tooltip is marginal. Question: leave as-is (label is self-explanatory) or add tooltips for keyboard-hint discovery? Recommendation: leave as-is. The text labels are clear; a tooltip would just repeat the label.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sidebar ellipsis (MoreHorizontal) on outline rows has no <code>Tooltip</code>.</strong> Only an <code>aria-label=\"Actions for {name}\"</code>. Hovering surfaces nothing. Recommendation: add a Tooltip reading \"More actions\" so users learn the affordance. Trade-off: very minor — the ellipsis icon is universally understood, and the radix tooltip would only show on hover-and-wait, by which time the user usually has already clicked. Low priority.</p></li><li><p><strong>BubbleMenu \"Most Powerful\" badge accompanies Reformat with AI.</strong> Badge is hidden under 640px (<code>hidden sm:inline-flex</code>) so it doesn't cause overflow at narrow widths. At wide widths the badge takes ~95px. Recommendation: keep — Howard explicitly chose the badge in the 2026-06-05 commit as an eye-magnet. Not flagging as a fix.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Narrow-viewport audit (600px):</strong> the More tools overflow correctly surfaces Tier 2 buttons (Research &amp; Import, Import Outline, Export Outline, Focus Mode, Expand All, Collapse All, Share Subtree…, Command Palette, Open Second Brain, Quick Capture, Search Second Brain, Ask Your Outlines) plus Tier 3 (Help &amp; Support, Settings). Casing now uniform Title Case across the entire menu. No items truncate.</p><p></p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Disposable Playwright web-mode probe against the running dev server on port 9002 verified: BubbleMenu tooltips fire on real-hover (Bold → \"Bold (⌘B)\", Reformat → \"Reformat with AI\", Heading 1 → \"Heading 1\"); More tools dropdown items all read in Title Case at 600px; Show/Hide all nodes dropdown items read \"Expand All\" / \"Collapse All\" at 1200px. Probe scripts deleted after the run. Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> — wrapped the BubbleMenu's button cluster in a dedicated <code>TooltipProvider</code>; wrapped each of the 8 buttons (Reformat + Bold + Italic + Strikethrough + Inline code + H1 + H2 + H3) in <code>Tooltip</code> / <code>TooltipTrigger asChild</code> / <code>TooltipContent</code>; removed the stale <code>title=\"Reformat with AI…\"</code> attribute (replaced by the styled tooltip).</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> — renamed 7 More tools overflow items to Title Case (\"Import Outline\", \"Export Outline\", \"Expand All\", \"Collapse All\", \"Share Subtree…\", \"Command Palette\", \"Help &amp; Support\"); renamed the \"Smart Tools…\" overflow item to \"Ask Your Outlines\" (matches the actual handler); renamed the Show-or-hide dropdown items \"Expand all\" / \"Collapse all\" to \"Expand All\" / \"Collapse All\".</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert the two files. The BubbleMenu fix is purely additive (Tooltip wrappers around existing Buttons + one TooltipProvider) — removing them returns to silent-on-hover. The label fixes are pure string edits — they have no behavior consequences.</p><p></p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>"
    },
    "decision-disabled-tooltips-and-cmdk-dedupe-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-disabled-tooltips-and-cmdk-dedupe-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Disabled-button tooltips + Cmd+K dedupe)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> Two follow-up fixes from the 2026-06-08 toolbar audit. Howard authorized both individually: (1) make disabled toolbar buttons surface tooltips on hover so users learn why a button is greyed out, and (2) remove the duplicate Cmd+K entry point from the tree-toolbar so the title-row MessageSquare is the single primary entry.</p><p></p><p><strong>Fix 1 — Disabled buttons now show tooltips explaining why they are disabled.</strong></p><p>Root cause: the project's Button primitive (src/components/ui/button.tsx) carries the Tailwind class <code>disabled:pointer-events-none</code>. This is correct for click-blocking but it also kills every hover event, which prevents the radix Tooltip from firing. Net effect: greyed-out toolbar buttons were silent on hover — users had no way to learn what would unlock them.</p><p></p><p><strong>Pattern chosen: wrapping span (option A from the brief, simpler).</strong> Each affected tree-toolbar Button was wrapped in a <code>&lt;span tabIndex={-1} className=\"inline-flex\"&gt;</code>. The span receives the hover events (it has live pointer-events), the radix TooltipTrigger renders against the span via <code>asChild</code>, the inner Button keeps <code>disabled</code> for click prevention. No changes to the Button primitive — other consumers of <code>disabled:pointer-events-none</code> stay intact. The aria-disabled / onClick-guard refactor (option B) was rejected as too invasive across six buttons.</p><p></p><p><strong>Six tree-toolbar buttons fixed:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Plus / Add sibling node</strong> — disabled when no node is selected or the User Guide is active. Tooltip now reads &quot;Cannot modify User Guide&quot; (guide) or &quot;Add sibling node — select a node first&quot; (no selection) or &quot;Add sibling node&quot; (enabled).</p></li><li><p><strong>Trash2 / Delete node</strong> — disabled on User Guide, root node, or no selection. Tooltip now reads &quot;Cannot modify User Guide&quot; / &quot;Delete node — cannot delete the root node&quot; / &quot;Delete node — select a node first&quot; / &quot;Delete node&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>Search / Search outline</strong> — disabled when there is no outline loaded. Tooltip now reads &quot;Search outline — open an outline first&quot; or &quot;Search outline (⌘F)&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>Focus / Focus Mode</strong> — disabled when no node is selected. Tooltip now reads &quot;Focus Mode — select a node first&quot; or &quot;Focus Mode (⌘⇧F)&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>ChevronsDownUp / Show or hide all nodes</strong> — disabled when there is no outline loaded. Tooltip now reads &quot;Show or hide all nodes — open an outline first&quot; or &quot;Show or hide all nodes&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>Share subtree</strong> — disabled when no node is selected. Tooltip now reads &quot;Share subtree — select a node first&quot; or &quot;Share subtree as...&quot;.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Fix 2 — Removed the duplicate Command-icon Cmd+K entry from the tree-toolbar.</strong></p><p>Per the toolbar audit, there were two side-by-side entry points to the Cmd+K palette: the MessageSquare button in the title row (more discoverable, always visible) and a Command-icon button in the tree-toolbar (Tier 2: hidden on phone). Howard's call: keep the MessageSquare title-row button, remove the Command-icon. The inline JSX block in <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> (the Tier-2 tree-toolbar Command button) was replaced with a one-line comment explaining the removal. The <code>Command</code> icon import stays because the mobile-overflow dropdown still surfaces &quot;Command palette&quot; as a backup tertiary entry — that one was not in the duplicate-paths concern.</p><p></p><p><strong>Visual verification (web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>Wide viewport (1200×800): hovering the disabled Trash button surfaced the tooltip &quot;Delete node — select a node first&quot;. Screenshot captured.</p></li><li><p>Narrow viewport (600×800): hovering the disabled Plus button surfaced the tooltip &quot;Add sibling node — select a node first&quot;. Screenshot captured.</p></li><li><p>Tree-toolbar Command-palette button count: 0 at both 1200px and 600px viewports.</p></li><li><p>Title-row Quick Command MessageSquare button count: 1 (unchanged).</p></li><li><p>Cmd+K keyboard shortcut still opens the palette — pressing Meta+K rendered the cmdk-root dialog with the expected action list.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> — six tree-toolbar Buttons each wrapped in a <code>&lt;span tabIndex={-1}&gt;</code>; TooltipContent text extended to explain the disabled reason; the inline Cmd+K Command-icon Button removed.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> to its pre-commit state. The wrapping-span layer is purely additive; removing it restores the previous (silent-on-hover) disabled-button behavior. The deleted Command-icon block can be re-pasted from the prior commit if Howard wants two Cmd+K entry points again.</p><p></p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>"
    },
    "decision-toolbar-audit-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-toolbar-audit-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Toolbar audit + targeted fixes)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> Howard reported confusing results on the outline-pane toolbar at narrow Electron widths. Drove every button in Playwright at 1280px, 600px, and 360px viewports; categorized into definite bugs (fixed) vs design questions (deferred to Howard).</p><p></p><p><strong>Definite bugs fixed:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Removed the banned phrase 'Ask AI' from the MessageSquare title-row button.</strong> Tooltip was 'Ask AI (⌘K)' and aria-label was 'Ask AI — natural-language command (Cmd+K)' — both violate the CLAUDE.md rule that forbids 'Ask AI' as a label. Renamed to 'Quick Command (⌘K)' so the title-row entry matches the Smart Tools menu's existing 'Quick Command' row (they open the same Cmd+K palette). Reverse: edit the aria-label + TooltipContent in src/components/outline-pane.tsx around the MessageSquare button.</p></li><li><p><strong>Smart Tools button now has a real Tooltip.</strong> Was using only the HTML title= attribute, which produced the browser-native ~1500ms tooltip instead of the styled radix tooltip every other tree-toolbar button uses. Wrapped DropdownMenuTrigger in Tooltip/TooltipTrigger/TooltipContent in src/components/ai-menu.tsx and removed the title=. Same fix pattern as Import, Export, More tools, and Show or hide all nodes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Mic icon in the Smart Tools menu's Quick Command row replaced with MessageSquare.</strong> The Mic icon implies voice-first, but per the project's voice-pullback memory (2026-06-01) Quick Command is typed by default and voice is opt-in via Settings → Input mode. Showing a red mic icon was visually overpromising. Replaced with MessageSquare in the primary color. Reverse: swap MessageSquare back to Mic in ai-menu.tsx.</p></li><li><p><strong>Removed redundant title= attributes on two buttons that already had radix Tooltip wrappers.</strong> Second Brain button and Command palette button each had both title='X' and a <Tooltip> wrapper. Double-tooltip causes flicker on some browsers. Kept the radix tooltip, dropped the title=.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Design questions deferred to Howard (not fixed; need a product call):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Two Command-Palette entry points side by side.</strong> At wide width the toolbar shows the MessageSquare 'Quick Command' button in the title row AND a Command icon button in the tree-toolbar — both open the same Cmd+K palette. Recommendation: pick one. The MessageSquare entry is more discoverable (top of pane), so the Command-icon duplicate in the tree-toolbar could be removed; that would also free a slot in the dense tree-toolbar.</p></li><li><p><strong>Disabled buttons show no tooltip explaining why they're disabled.</strong> The Button primitive uses 'disabled:pointer-events-none' which blocks radix Tooltip from firing. So when a user hovers a greyed-out Plus/Trash/Focus/Share button to learn 'why can't I click this?', they get nothing. CLAUDE.md says explanations belong in tooltips. Recommendation: switch to an aria-disabled visual treatment that keeps pointer events live, so hover still surfaces the explanatory tooltip (e.g. 'Select a node first', 'Cannot modify User Guide'). This is a one-liner per button but affects ~6 buttons consistently — wants a product yes/no.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Settings button cannot easily wrap in radix Tooltip</strong> because SettingsDialog passes its children to DialogTrigger asChild, and stacking TooltipTrigger asChild + DialogTrigger asChild + Button forwards refs in a way SettingsDialog (not a forwardRef) breaks. Kept the HTML title='Settings' as a compromise — the browser-native tooltip works, just slower and unstyled. Recommendation: refactor SettingsDialog to forward refs so it can be wrapped in a Tooltip cleanly. Low priority — it works.</p></li><li><p><strong>Outline title in the title row is just text with a native title= attribute</strong> (showing the full name on hover for truncated names). No real tooltip. Mostly fine but inconsistent.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Narrow-viewport audit summary (600px + 360px):</strong> the responsive collapse is working correctly. At narrow widths the tree-toolbar hides tier-2 buttons (Focus, Expand/Collapse, Share, Command palette, Second Brain, Smart Tools, Settings, Help) and the title-row 'More tools' button surfaces them in a single dropdown. The Cmd+K MessageSquare button stays visible at every width as the primary AI entry point. No buttons are unrecoverably hidden.</p><p></p><p><strong>Playwright verification:</strong> dedicated probes ran at 1280px and 600px; confirmed the 'Ask AI' string no longer appears anywhere on the page, the Quick Command tooltip reads 'Quick Command (⌘K)', the Smart Tools tooltip reads 'Smart Tools', and the Smart Tools menu opens with all expected items. TypeScript baseline preserved at 52 pre-existing errors (zero new).</p><p></p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert src/components/outline-pane.tsx and src/components/ai-menu.tsx to their pre-commit states.</p>"
    },
    "decision-auto-updater-2026-06-06": {
      "id": "decision-auto-updater-2026-06-06",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-06 (Auto-updater shipped at launch — electron-updater + non-intrusive banner)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The macOS desktop auto-updater moves from \"post-launch v1.1 deferred\" to \"shipped at launch.\" The code-side integration of <code>electron-updater</code> ships now; the production update feed goes live the moment Howard's Apple Developer cert + DMG code-signing + Vercel hosting are wired (those are external punch-list items the code does not block).</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-06</p><p><strong>Howard's direction:</strong> \"electron-updater is the customary, easy way IdeaM keeps desktop versions current. Build it now, not later.\"</p><p><strong>How a desktop user experiences updates:</strong></p><ul><li>8 seconds after launching the app, electron-updater silently checks an update feed hosted on the marketing site.</li><li>If a newer version is published, it downloads in the background while the user keeps working — no interruption, no progress dialog.</li><li>When the download completes, a THIN non-intrusive banner appears at the very top of the app shell: <em>\"Update ready. Version X.Y.Z is ready. Restart to apply.\"</em> with two buttons: <strong>Restart now</strong> (primary) and <strong>Later</strong> (secondary). An X button is a third way to dismiss.</li><li>The banner is NOT a modal dialog. The user can ignore it indefinitely.</li><li><strong>Restart now</strong> calls <code>autoUpdater.quitAndInstall(true, true)</code> — the app quits, the new version installs, and the app relaunches automatically.</li><li><strong>Later</strong> writes a snooze timestamp to localStorage; the banner stays hidden for 24 hours. After that window (or on the next app launch) the banner reappears.</li><li>While the app stays open, electron-updater re-checks every 4 hours.</li><li><strong>Manual check:</strong> Help menu &gt; <em>\"Check for Updates…\"</em>. A toast reports \"Checking for updates…\" then either \"You're on the latest version\" or kicks off the silent-download path.</li></ul><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Feed URL:</strong> <code>https://www.2ndbrainware.com/updates/mac/latest.yml</code> served from the existing Vercel marketing project. electron-builder's <code>generic</code> publish provider writes this URL into <code>app-update.yml</code> at package time, and electron-updater reads it at runtime. No CDN, no S3, no GitHub Releases for v1 — the marketing site already handles low-traffic static files. Reverse: change the <code>publish</code> block in <code>package.json</code> and re-package.</p></li><li><p><strong>Re-check cadence:</strong> 4 hours while the app stays open. Rationale: long enough not to be chatty, short enough that a same-day patch reaches active users without requiring a relaunch. Reverse: change <code>UPDATE_CHECK_INTERVAL_MS</code> in <code>electron/main.js</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Initial-check delay:</strong> 8 seconds after the main window is ready. Rationale: startup is the most CPU-contended moment; we don't want the updater fighting for bandwidth or CPU with dev server warm-up. Reverse: change <code>UPDATE_INITIAL_DELAY_MS</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Snooze duration on \"Later\":</strong> 24 hours, stored in localStorage at <code>idiampro:update-banner-snoozed-until</code>. Rationale: long enough to actually feel like \"later,\" short enough that the user remembers what they snoozed. Reverse: change <code>SNOOZE_MS</code> in <code>src/components/update-banner.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Dev-mode skip:</strong> the updater is registered only when <code>app.isPackaged === true</code>. Rationale: trying to update a dev build produces confusing errors and there's nothing to update. Reverse: remove the <code>app.isPackaged</code> guard in <code>registerAutoUpdater()</code> (NOT recommended).</p></li><li><p><strong>Banner UX — non-intrusive top-of-shell, NOT a modal:</strong> mounted in <code>src/app/app/page.tsx</code> above <code>OutlinePro</code> so it's visible regardless of which outline is open. Subtle accent color (<code>bg-primary/10</code>), never error-red. Mobile-responsive (stacks vertically below 640px). Rationale: an outliner you're writing in should never trap focus into a system-level dialog. Reverse: replace the inline JSX in <code>page.tsx</code> with a modal dialog component.</p></li><li><p><strong>Auto-download enabled (<code>autoUpdater.autoDownload = true</code>):</strong> the user never sees a \"do you want to download?\" prompt. Rationale: download is bandwidth-cheap and gives users an instant \"Restart now\" path when the banner appears. Reverse: set <code>autoDownload = false</code> and add an explicit \"Download\" button to the banner.</p></li><li><p><strong>Auto-install-on-quit enabled (<code>autoUpdater.autoInstallOnAppQuit = true</code>):</strong> if the user closes the app without clicking \"Restart now,\" the update applies automatically the next time they launch. Rationale: maximizes the chance the user ends up on the latest version without nagging them. Reverse: set to <code>false</code> if Howard ever wants explicit user consent on every install.</p></li><li><p><strong>Errors are non-fatal:</strong> any updater event listener that catches an error logs it and continues. The app stays running on the old version. Rationale: a broken update feed must never crash a working app. Reverse: throw inside the <code>error</code> handler (NOT recommended).</p></li><li><p><strong>Graceful degradation if <code>electron-updater</code> isn't installed:</strong> the top-of-file <code>require('electron-updater')</code> is wrapped in try/catch. If the dependency isn't yet on disk (e.g. Howard hasn't run <code>npm install</code> after the package.json bump), the app logs a warning and continues — no auto-updates, but no crash. Reverse: remove the try/catch once the install is verified everywhere.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Apple Developer cert + DMG signing:</strong> still on Howard's external punch list. The code-side integration ships now and works against an unsigned dev DMG; the production feed turns on the moment the cert + electron-builder publish config are wired. No coupling between this PR and Howard's external work.</p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><code>package.json</code> — added <code>\"electron-updater\": \"^6.8.9\"</code> to dependencies (run <code>npm install</code> to actually fetch it) + new <code>build.publish</code> block for electron-builder.</li><li><code>electron/main.js</code> — top-of-file require + <code>registerAutoUpdater()</code> + IPC handlers (<code>restart-to-update</code>, <code>check-for-updates</code>, <code>get-app-version</code>) + new <code>Check for Updates…</code> Help menu item.</li><li><code>electron/preload.js</code> — bridge methods <code>onUpdateDownloaded</code>, <code>onUpdateNotAvailable</code>, <code>onUpdateCheckFailed</code>, <code>onUpdateCheckStarted</code>, <code>restartToUpdate</code>, <code>checkForUpdates</code>, <code>getAppVersion</code>.</li><li><code>src/lib/electron-storage.ts</code> — TypeScript surface for the new bridge methods.</li><li><code>src/components/update-banner.tsx</code> — NEW. Renderer-side banner + toast handlers for the manual-check feedback.</li><li><code>src/app/app/page.tsx</code> — mounts <code>&lt;UpdateBanner /&gt;</code> at the top of the app shell.</li><li>Docs: <code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> User Guide node, <code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT, <code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT, <em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> (both copies), this Decisions Log.</li></ul><p><strong>Visual verification:</strong> short Playwright probe (web-mode against the running dev server on port 9002, since Howard's Electron owns the app) stubbed <code>window.electronAPI</code> with the new bridge methods, used the dev-only <code>window.__idiamProUpdateBannerTestSeed</code> seam to inject a synthetic update-downloaded payload, and confirmed: (1) banner hidden by default, (2) banner renders with the expected copy and version label after seed, (3) Restart now calls <code>restartToUpdate</code> exactly once, (4) Later hides the banner and writes a ~24h snooze timestamp to localStorage. All four assertions passed. Probe deleted after the run.</p><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports the same 52 baseline errors before and after this change; zero new errors in <code>update-banner.tsx</code>, <code>electron-storage.ts</code>, or <code>app/app/page.tsx</code>.</p><p><strong>Launch Progress impact:</strong> macOS desktop auto-updater moves from Post-launch / deferred (was 0/10) to Shipped at launch. Counters bump accordingly across the Launch Progress / Bootstrap Plan / Development root mirrors.</p>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-user-guide-read-only-2026-06-07": {
      "id": "decision-user-guide-read-only-2026-06-07",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-07 (User Guide locked read-only + Wrench removed)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The User Guide outline is now fully read-only, and the Wrench / Refresh User Guide menu has been retired entirely.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-07</p><p><strong>Howard's direction (verbatim):</strong> &quot;Get rid of the wrench, and don't allow the user to edit or delete the user guide. They can always put their own notes in a separate outline. It doesn't have to be in our user guide. Support will refer to the user guide when we assist the user. We have to know what's going to be there.&quot;</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> The guide is what Howard and support staff point users at when helping them. If it can drift from the bundled version, support is debugging an outline that may bear no resemblance to what the app actually does. Locking it read-only and refreshing it from the bundled <code>getInitialGuide()</code> on every app launch makes &quot;what the user sees&quot; deterministic per app version.</p><p><strong>What's gone:</strong></p><ul><li>Wrench dropdown menu in the toolbar (was Tier 3, desktop-only) — removed entirely from <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>.</li><li>Refresh User Guide entry in the More-tools overflow menu — removed.</li><li>Refresh User Guide entry in the Command Palette (Cmd+K) — removed.</li><li><code>handleRefreshGuide</code> callback in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> — removed along with all four <code>onRefreshGuide</code> prop wirings.</li><li><code>onRefreshGuide</code> prop on <code>OutlinePane</code> and <code>CommandPalette</code> — removed from both type definitions.</li><li><code>Wrench</code> icon import from lucide-react in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code> — removed (no remaining usage).</li></ul><p><strong>What a user can no longer do to the User Guide:</strong></p><ul><li>Type into a node's content area (Tiptap editor reports <code>contenteditable=&quot;false&quot;</code> — already enforced).</li><li>Add a sibling node, add a child node, delete a node, duplicate a node, cut/paste a subtree, drag-reorder, or change a node's color/tag in bulk.</li><li>Rename any node (the Rename Node context-menu item is now hidden, F2 + double-click + tap-again-on-selected are all gated by the new <code>isReadOnly</code> flag).</li><li>Run AI mutations: Refresh from Web, Translate, Reformat with AI, Transform Outline with AI (all hidden or disabled when the guide is the active outline; defensive guards in the apply handlers were already in place).</li><li>Delete the outline itself or rename it (the sidebar's User Guide row has no context menu, delete button, or rename trigger — already enforced).</li><li>Insert a cross-outline link inside a guide node (already enforced).</li></ul><p><strong>What still works on the User Guide:</strong> selecting and switching to it; reading; expanding and collapsing; searching; copying text or a subtree to the clipboard; exporting the guide or a subtree (PDF, Markdown, etc.); sharing a subtree; saving a subtree to Second Brain; viewing node properties.</p><p><strong>Auto-restore on every app launch:</strong> the load sequence in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> already adds the bundled <code>getInitialGuide()</code> at the front of the loaded outlines list. Today's edit additionally filters any stale <code>isGuide</code> outlines out of the disk-loaded set (defense against older app versions that may have persisted one). Net effect: every launch, the User Guide is whatever <code>initial-guide.ts</code> compiled into the bundle for that app version.</p><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Hide-vs-disable for guide-incompatible context-menu items:</strong> hide entirely (pass <code>undefined</code> instead of a no-op function so the item doesn't render). Rationale: a graying-out approach was on the table for discoverability, but on a read-only outline the user's mental model should be &quot;this guide is just for reading,&quot; not &quot;these features exist but are off right now.&quot; The read-only badge in the content header already signals the constraint at a higher level. Reverse: in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>, switch the <code>currentOutline.isGuide ? undefined : ...</code> ternaries to the previous <code>... ? () =&gt; {} : ...</code> shape.</p></li><li><p><strong>New <code>isReadOnly</code> prop on <code>NodeItem</code>:</strong> gates the always-shown affordances (Rename context-menu item, F2 rename, double-click rename, tap-again-to-edit on iOS) that aren't covered by the optional-callback-undefined pattern. Propagates to recursive children.</p></li><li><p><strong>Bulk operations (multi-select delete / change-color / add-tag):</strong> short-circuit at the top of each handler in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> with a conversational toast (&quot;User Guide is read-only — Make personal notes in your own outline instead.&quot;) when the active outline is the guide.</p></li><li><p><strong>Toast copy &quot;User Guide is read-only&quot; + &quot;Make personal notes in your own outline instead.&quot;</strong> Conversational tone per the natural-language-error-tone memory; tells the user what to do next (create a separate outline for their notes) rather than just rejecting the action.</p></li><li><p><strong>Defensive disk-load filter:</strong> <code>userOutlines.filter(o =&gt; o &amp;&amp; isValidOutline(o) &amp;&amp; !o.isGuide)</code> at app start strips any stale guide from disk before re-adding the bundled one. Without this, an older app version that had persisted an <code>isGuide</code> outline could create two guides in the sidebar.</p></li><li><p><strong>Self-describing guide intro:</strong> the User Guide's root-node content now explicitly tells the user that the guide is read-only, refreshes on every launch, and that they should create a separate outline for personal notes (path: sidebar &gt; New Outline). The Managing Outlines node and the Toolbar Icons node were updated to drop the Wrench reference and reflect the read-only status.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> — removed Wrench JSX block, removed overflow-menu Refresh User Guide entry, removed <code>Wrench</code> icon import, removed <code>onRefreshGuide</code> prop, added <code>isReadOnly</code> prop on <code>NodeItem</code>, gated AI menu surfaces on <code>!currentOutline?.isGuide</code>, added guide gate to <code>Refresh from Web</code> menu item.</li><li><code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code> — removed <code>handleRefreshGuide</code> callback + all four wirings, added <code>currentOutline?.isGuide</code> guards + conversational toasts to <code>handleBulkDelete</code> / <code>handleBulkChangeColor</code> / <code>handleBulkAddTag</code>, added defensive disk-load filter against stale guides.</li><li><code>src/components/command-palette.tsx</code> — removed Refresh User Guide <code>CommandItem</code> + <code>onRefreshGuide</code> prop.</li><li><code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> — new <code>isReadOnly</code> prop, gates Rename context-menu item + double-click rename + iOS tap-again-to-edit, propagates to recursive child <code>NodeItem</code>s.</li><li><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — root-node welcome content explains read-only + auto-refresh + personal-notes pattern, Managing Outlines node updated, Toolbar Icons node updated (Refresh User Guide line dropped).</li><li><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — replaced three-button toolbar wording with two-button wording, added a dedicated &quot;User Guide is read-only&quot; bullet, removed Refresh User Guide from the responsive-toolbar tier descriptions.</li><li><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edits, duplicated to keep both contexts in sync.</li><li><code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - How it works.idm</code> — Tier 3 toolbar list updated; Sign-out paragraph updated to drop the Wrench reference.</li></ul><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</li><li><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was also NOT touched — parent agent should sync.</li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same baseline; zero new errors introduced.</li><li>Visual verification via <code>tests/_wrench-removed-guide-locked-check.js</code> (a disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002): 11/11 assertions green. Verified that the Wrench is absent from the toolbar, the Tiptap editor reports <code>contenteditable=&quot;false&quot;</code> on the guide, the read-only badge is present, the Add-sibling and Delete-node toolbar buttons are disabled, the right-click context menu on a guide node now contains only read-only actions (Share Subtree As, Save to Second Brain, Copy Subtree, Properties), typing into the guide editor produces no change, the sidebar guide row has no delete affordance, and the Smart Tools menu does not offer Translate / Reformat / Transform when the guide is the active outline. Probe deleted after the run.</li><li>Focus management throughout — the Terminal window was refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and immediately after every screenshot.</li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-search-hide-non-matches-2026-06-07": {
      "id": "decision-search-hide-non-matches-2026-06-07",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-07 (Search truly hides non-matches; status chip added)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The outline search view-shaper now <em>actually hides</em> non-matching, non-ancestor nodes from the rendered tree (instead of merely collapsing them under their parent). A small status chip sits above the tree whenever the filter is active, showing the active term, the hidden-node count, and a Clear button.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-07</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> Yesterday's view-shaper (commit 350e07b) reshaped the tree with <code>isCollapsed</code>, but <code>isCollapsed</code> only hides a node's children — the node itself still rendered as a row if its parent was expanded. Howard's example: searching &quot;translate&quot; with the first match deep under node 6 left nodes 6.1 through 6.9 visible as rows under expanded node 6, when only the ancestor chain and the actual match should have stayed visible. The fix is to add a real hidden-IDs set the renderer respects.</p><p><strong>Option chosen (B): persistent hidden-IDs set on the outline.</strong> Option A — keep the shaper using <code>isCollapsed</code> and add a sibling-row filter at render time — was rejected because the source of truth for &quot;is this node currently hidden by the search&quot; needed to survive across editor sessions, undo/redo, and the existing <code>setOutlines</code> dirty-tracking. Storing it on the <code>Outline</code> means it travels through the same persistence path everything else does and undo just works.</p><p><strong>Schema addition (backward compatible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>searchHiddenNodeIds?: string[]</code> on the <code>Outline</code> interface — IDs the renderer skips entirely.</p></li><li><p><code>searchFilterTerm?: string</code> on the <code>Outline</code> interface — the term that produced the filter, shown in the chip.</p></li><li><p>Both optional; older outlines load fine without them.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Render filter:</strong> a single early-return at the top of <code>NodeItem</code> — if the node's ID is in <code>searchHiddenNodeIds</code>, return <code>null</code>. Because <code>NodeItem</code> is recursive (it renders its own children by mapping <code>childrenIds</code> to <code>&lt;NodeItem&gt;</code>), skipping a parent automatically skips its entire subtree. The hidden set is also passed down the recursion so deeper nodes match individually too.</p><p><strong>Status chip UX:</strong> a single-line bar sitting above the outline tree, subtle accent background with a 1px accent border. Format: <code>Filtered: \"[term]\" · [N] hidden · [Clear]</code>. Clear is a ghost button on the right. Title (browser-native tooltip) reads &quot;Search filter active — only matches and their ancestors are visible. Click Clear to show all.&quot; No Tooltip primitive around the chip itself (an early version using <code>TooltipTrigger asChild</code> swallowed the Clear-button click; reverted to a plain <code>title</code> attribute).</p><p><strong>Clear triggers (filter goes away):</strong></p><ul><li><p>New search submit — the shaper recomputes; an empty-term path explicitly drops <code>searchHiddenNodeIds</code> + <code>searchFilterTerm</code>.</p></li><li><p>Expand All (Cmd+E) — handler clears the fields as part of the same outline update that flips <code>isCollapsed</code> off.</p></li><li><p>Collapse All (Cmd+Shift+E) — same treatment.</p></li><li><p>Clear button on the chip — calls a new <code>handleClearSearchFilter</code> in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code>; also bumps an <code>externalClearSignal</code> so <code>OutlineSearch</code> resets its internal search-term state (without this, the debounced search effect re-applied the old term immediately).</p></li></ul><p><strong>Preserve triggers (filter stays put):</strong></p><ul><li><p>Chevron toggle on a visible node — only mutates <code>isCollapsed</code>, never touches the hidden set.</p></li><li><p>Editing node content / renaming a node / drag-reorder / copy / cut / paste — same: none of these handlers touch the new fields, so the filter persists by default.</p></li><li><p>Closing the search input via Escape — <code>OutlineSearch</code> just hides; the outline-level fields stay on, so the chip stays visible.</p></li></ul><p><strong>The OutlineSearch debounce gotcha:</strong> the search-input component owns its own <code>searchTerm</code> state and re-fires the apply effect whenever that state is set. So clicking Clear on the chip from outside cleared the outline-level fields, but the next debounce tick re-applied the filter with the still-typed term. The fix is a bumpable <code>externalClearSignal</code> prop on <code>OutlineSearch</code> whose <code>useEffect</code> resets the local <code>searchTerm</code> to empty. The chip's Clear button bumps it, and <code>OutlinePane</code> also bumps it automatically whenever <code>currentOutline.searchHiddenNodeIds</code> transitions from non-empty to empty (covers the Expand All / Collapse All paths too).</p><p><strong>Verified visually (Playwright web-mode probe, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>User Guide baseline (after Expand All): 119 visible rows.</p></li><li><p>Search &quot;translate&quot;: <strong>5 visible rows</strong> (one match plus its ancestor chain). The earlier shaper left ~37; the new shaper drops it to the correct 5.</p></li><li><p>Status chip appears with text <code>Filtered: \"translate\" · 114 hidden · Clear</code>.</p></li><li><p>Escape closes the search input but rows stay at 5 (filter persists).</p></li><li><p>Chevron click on a visible parent: rows stay at 5 (filter persists).</p></li><li><p>Clear on the chip: rows go back to 119; chip vanishes.</p></li><li><p>Re-search &quot;translate&quot; → Expand All: rows back to 119; chip vanishes.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/types/index.ts</code> — added optional <code>searchHiddenNodeIds</code> + <code>searchFilterTerm</code> on <code>Outline</code>.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code> — extended <code>handleApplySearchView</code> to compute the hidden-IDs set and write both fields; added <code>handleClearSearchFilter</code>; wired Expand All / Collapse All to clear the fields in the same update; passed the new handler down to both <code>OutlinePane</code> mount points.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/outline-search.tsx</code> — extended the apply callback signature with <code>searchTerm</code>; added <code>externalClearSignal</code> prop + effect that resets local search term.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> — added <code>onClearSearchFilter</code> prop, the status-chip JSX above the tree, the <code>searchHiddenSet</code> memo passed to <code>NodeItem</code>, the <code>externalClearSignal</code> state + auto-bump effect.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> — accepts <code>searchHiddenNodeIds</code>, early-returns <code>null</code> if the current node is in the set, propagates the set to recursive child <code>NodeItem</code>s.</p></li><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> Search node — rewrote the body so it explains the new hide-non-matches behavior and the status chip.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — replaced the &quot;reshapes / collapses&quot; wording with &quot;FILTERS / hidden / status chip&quot;.</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edit, duplicated.</p></li><li><p><code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - How it works.idm</code> — Search Filters node extended with a 2026-06-07 paragraph explaining the hide-non-matches behavior, the chip, and the implementation in plain language.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Chip text format:</strong> <em>Filtered: \"[term]\" · [N] hidden · Clear</em>. &quot;Filtered:&quot; was picked over &quot;Showing:&quot; because the action being communicated is restriction, not summary. Reverse: change the JSX string in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Chip styling:</strong> single-line, subtle accent background (<code>bg-accent/40</code>) with a 1px accent border. The hidden count + Clear button live on the same row. Reverse: edit the classNames in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Browser-native <code>title</code> attribute for the chip tooltip</strong> instead of the project's Tooltip primitive. An earlier attempt with <code>TooltipTrigger asChild</code> wrapping the chip + button container intercepted the Clear-button click event so it never fired. Reverse: re-introduce a Tooltip wrapper around just the descriptive text span (not the Clear button).</p></li><li><p><strong>Render filter lives in <code>NodeItem</code>, not in a list-flattening step earlier in <code>OutlinePane</code>.</strong> Putting the check at the leaf component preserves the recursive parent-renders-children pattern that the rest of the codebase relies on. Reverse: hoist the filter into a pre-flatten step and switch <code>NodeItem</code>'s child render to a flat list.</p></li><li><p><strong>Root node never goes into the hidden set</strong> (defensive against an outline whose root could otherwise be filtered out if the matching algorithm ever excluded it). Reverse: drop the <code>id === o.rootNodeId continue</code> in <code>handleApplySearchView</code>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was also NOT touched — parent agent should sync.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Visual verification via a disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002. All 8 assertions green: baseline 119 rows, search-filtered 5 rows, chip visible with term + count, Escape preserves filter, chevron toggle preserves filter, chip Clear restores 5 → 119 + removes chip, re-search rows 5, Expand All clears filter back to 119 + removes chip. Focus management throughout — the Terminal window was refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and immediately after every screenshot. Probe deleted after the run.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-search-chevron-flicker-bubble-overlap-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-search-chevron-flicker-bubble-overlap-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Search chevron flicker + BubbleMenu overlap fixed)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Two bugs fixed in one commit. Both observed by Howard after the same-day compress-only revert.</strong></p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Bug 1 — Chevron clicks on compressed nodes snapped back closed.</strong> After searching, clicking the chevron on any node the search had compressed appeared to open for a few milliseconds, then immediately close again. <code>aria-expanded</code> stayed false. Repro: 13 different collapsed siblings all snapped back closed on click in an automated probe.</p><p><strong>Root cause:</strong> in <code>src/components/outline-search.tsx</code>, the debounced search effect's <code>useCallback</code> for <code>performSearch</code> listed <code>currentOutline</code> in its dependency array. Every time the user clicked a chevron, the outline state mutated → <code>currentOutline</code> reference changed → <code>performSearch</code> identity changed → the debounce effect re-fired → <code>onApplySearchView</code> was called again → search re-applied the compressed shape, undoing the chevron click. The bug was a side-effect cascade, not a render glitch.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> the debounced effect now depends ONLY on the user's typed/toggled search inputs (term, scope, names, content, restrict-to-open). <code>currentOutline</code>, <code>outlines</code>, and the two callbacks are read via refs that get updated in their own tiny <code>useEffect</code>s. <code>performSearch</code> has an empty dependency array — its identity never changes, so outline mutations no longer re-trigger the apply step. This restores the 1988 spec exactly: search applies a shape ONCE per explicit user input change; after that the user is free to chevron-explore without snap-back.</p><p><strong>Bug 2 — The Tiptap BubbleMenu (floating format toolbar) covered the search input.</strong> When the user clicked into the search input and typed, the BubbleMenu floated over the input and hid the text being typed.</p><p><strong>Root cause:</strong> the BubbleMenu in <code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> used Tiptap's default <code>shouldShow</code> behavior, which evaluates only the editor's own state (focus + selection). It had no awareness of other focusable inputs in the app, so when the editor still held a selection from a previous interaction, the bubble menu rendered even though the user was actively typing into the search input.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> added an explicit <code>shouldShow</code> predicate to the BubbleMenu. The predicate first checks <code>document.activeElement</code>: if the currently focused element carries <code>data-testid=\"search-input\"</code>, the BubbleMenu returns <code>false</code> (hidden) regardless of editor state. Otherwise it falls through to Tiptap's default rule: show only when the editor is editable, has focus, has a non-empty text selection, and the selection is not inside an empty text block. This is the cleanest fix — it kills the menu at the source rather than masking with z-index, so the menu also can't steal clicks intended for the search input.</p><p><strong>Why not React state for the search-input-focus signal?</strong> Considered. Rejected because the BubbleMenu lives in <code>content-pane.tsx</code> and the search input lives several components away in <code>outline-search.tsx</code>; threading a focus boolean through the prop tree (or hoisting it into a context) would be a much larger change for the same result. <code>document.activeElement</code> is a stable, synchronously-readable global and is the right tool for \"is this specific input currently focused?\"</p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002. Probe loaded the User Guide (119 rows), opened search, focused the input (verified BubbleMenu candidate count = 0), typed \"translate\" (verified text appears in input rect, not obscured), submitted, captured the compressed row count (37), clicked the first collapsed outline-row chevron (rows → 39, held at 39 after a 1.5s wait), then clicked a second collapsed chevron (rows → 46, held at 46 after a 1.5s wait). Both chevrons opened and stayed open. Probe deleted after the run.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/outline-search.tsx</code> — added latest-value refs for outline + callbacks; stable <code>performSearch</code> with empty deps; debounce effect now driven purely by user inputs.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> — added <code>shouldShow</code> predicate to the BubbleMenu.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert the two files. The previous behavior (chevron flicker + BubbleMenu overlap) returns. Bug 1's fix is a pure narrowing of effect dependencies; Bug 2's fix is a single predicate prop on the BubbleMenu.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-bubblemenu-responsive-placement-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-bubblemenu-responsive-placement-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (BubbleMenu responsive placement)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The Tiptap BubbleMenu (the floating format toolbar that appears next to a text selection inside a node's content) now anchors <em>below</em> the selection on narrow viewports (under 768px) and keeps its classic <em>above-the-selection</em> placement on wider viewports. Floating UI's <code>flip</code> middleware is disabled so the chosen placement never bounces back to the opposite side; <code>shift</code> keeps the menu inside the viewport horizontally, with an 8px padding.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Symptom Howard saw:</strong> on narrow window widths the floating format toolbar visually overlapped the search input / the row of search results / the top of the tree pane. The earlier same-day fix (a <code>shouldShow</code> predicate that hides the BubbleMenu when the search input is focused) helped one scenario — actively typing in the search box — but it did NOT help when Howard had a text selection inside a node, the BubbleMenu rendered above the selection, and the menu's body landed inside the search-bar's horizontal band.</p><p><strong>Root cause:</strong> the BubbleMenu was configured with <code>options={{ placement: 'top' }}</code>. At wide widths the editor sits in a roomy right-hand pane and the menu has plenty of headroom — it sits well above the search row. At narrow widths the layout compresses; selections near the top of the editor pushed the menu up onto / above the editor's own toolbar, and at certain widths the menu's footprint covered the search bar that lives in the tree pane to its left.</p><p><strong>Approach chosen — A (responsive placement).</strong> Lowest-risk single-prop change: at <code>useIsMobile()</code> truthiness (≤768px or iPhone-class UA) the placement becomes <code>'bottom'</code>; otherwise stays <code>'top'</code>. Adding <code>flip: false</code> stops Floating UI from undoing this decision when the selection is near the bottom of the viewport (the auto-flip would otherwise restore the original problem). <code>shift</code> keeps the menu in the viewport horizontally. <code>offset: 8</code> gives the menu a small visual gap from the selection.</p><p><strong>Approaches rejected (and why):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>B — Add collision boundary against the search bar.</strong> More correct in theory but a much larger change (Floating UI middleware that treats the search/toolbar row as a forbidden zone). Approach A solves the actual symptom with one prop, so the additional middleware is unnecessary for now. Can be added later if a new collision case appears.</p></li><li><p><strong>C — Inline the formatting toolbar below the search bar at narrow widths.</strong> What Howard's verbal description literally said (\"moves to line under the search term\"). But it would be a much larger UX change and would lose the selection-anchored convenience of a bubble menu. Approach A captures the spirit of the request (the menu is no longer covering the search row) without flipping the whole interaction model.</p></li><li><p><strong>D — Hide the BubbleMenu entirely at narrow widths.</strong> Would force users into keyboard shortcuts (Cmd+B etc.) for formatting on small screens, losing the affordance for touch-first users. Rejected outright.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> — the BubbleMenu's <code>options</code> prop changed from <code>{ placement: 'top' }</code> to a responsive object driven by the existing <code>isMobile</code> from <code>useIsMobile()</code>: <code>{ placement: isMobile ? 'bottom' : 'top', flip: false, shift: { padding: 8 }, offset: 8 }</code>. The prior <code>shouldShow</code> predicate (which suppresses the menu when the outline search input is focused) is preserved unchanged.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Visual verification (disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>640×900 viewport: selected text in a node's content. BubbleMenu rendered <em>below</em> the selection (bubble y = 306, selection bottom = 298). No overlap with the toolbar row above.</p></li><li><p>480×800 viewport: same drill, bubble rendered <em>below</em> the selection (bubble y = 331, selection bottom = 323).</p></li><li><p>1200×800 viewport: BubbleMenu rendered <em>above</em> the selection (bubble bottom = 253, selection y = 261) — classic desktop behavior preserved. No regression at wide widths.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and immediately after every screenshot.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced. The two pre-existing errors near the touched region (lines 1933, 3480 in content-pane.tsx) are unrelated to this edit.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> change the BubbleMenu's <code>options</code> prop back to <code>{ placement: 'top' }</code>. The <code>shouldShow</code> focus-aware predicate stays.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-search-bar-tooltips-and-sizing-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-search-bar-tooltips-and-sizing-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Search bar tooltips + narrow-width sizing)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Two coordinated fixes to the outline search bar (the in-pane Find toolbar that opens with Cmd+F): (1) the up-arrow, down-arrow, and eraser icon buttons now show their tooltips on hover even when disabled; (2) the search text input never collapses below a usable width — it holds a 140px minimum, and the row of icon buttons wraps to a second line when the pane is too narrow to fit everything horizontally.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Howard's report (verbatim):</strong> &ldquo;The up arrow, comma, down arrow, and eraser aren't highlighted and don't have any tool tips.&rdquo; (The &ldquo;comma&rdquo; is voice-dictation noise.) &ldquo;When the window is narrow, the text box shrinks to nothing, and only the magnifying glass is visible.&rdquo;</p><p><strong>Bug 1 — Tooltips on the disabled icon buttons.</strong></p><p>Root cause: the previous-match, next-match, and clear-highlights buttons are <code>disabled</code> when <code>totalMatches === 0</code>. A native <code>&lt;button disabled&gt;</code> swallows pointer events, so Radix's <code>Tooltip</code> never sees the hover and never opens. The tooltips were defined in the JSX all along — they just couldn't fire when the user needed them most (when there were no matches and the user was wondering &ldquo;what does this thing do?&rdquo;).</p><p>Fix: wrap each of those three <code>TooltipTrigger</code>s' child <code>&lt;Button&gt;</code> in a focusable <code>&lt;span tabIndex={0} className=\"inline-flex\"&gt;</code>. The span is non-disabled and therefore receives hover/focus events; the tooltip opens normally; the disabled button inside still refuses clicks. The result: hover any of those three buttons (even with zero matches) and the tooltip appears. The fourth icon (Clear search / X eraser inside the input) is never disabled, so no wrapper was needed there.</p><p>Tooltip copy preserved as-is: &ldquo;Previous match (Shift+Enter)&rdquo;, &ldquo;Next match (Enter)&rdquo;, &ldquo;Clear highlights and close search&rdquo;. Each button also already had a matching <code>aria-label</code> for accessibility.</p><p><strong>Bug 2 — Narrow-width sizing strategy chosen: <code>flex-wrap</code> + <code>min-w-[140px]</code> on the input.</strong></p><p>Root cause: the outer flex row laid out all controls (input + 8 icon buttons + scope/search-name/search-content/restrict-to-open toggles) on a single line. The input had <code>flex-1</code> but no minimum width, so as the pane shrank the input was the first to give up width and collapsed to almost zero — leaving Howard with only the magnifying glass icon and no place to type.</p><p>Fix: changed the outer flex from <code>flex items-center gap-1</code> to <code>flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1</code>, and added <code>min-w-[140px]</code> to the input's wrapper. Now: at wide pane widths everything fits on one row exactly as before (no visible change). At narrow widths the input holds 140px, the toggle buttons stay next to it, and the rest of the buttons (Prev / Next / Eraser / Close + extra toggles when present) wrap to a second row below — all still inside the pane.</p><p>Why <code>flex-wrap</code> instead of hiding the match counter or truncating buttons: wrapping preserves every control's affordance, which matters because Howard's Cmd+F is muscle memory and any &ldquo;disappearing button&rdquo; behavior is a worse UX than a slightly taller toolbar. The match counter row beneath the toolbar was already on its own line and is unaffected. The added vertical space at narrow widths is only used when needed; wide layouts retain the original single-row toolbar exactly.</p><p>Why 140px specifically: at 140px the input still shows ~12–15 characters comfortably (enough to read a real search term like &ldquo;translate&rdquo; or &ldquo;summarize&rdquo;) plus the search icon and the clear-X. The Playwright probe confirmed this at 360px viewport width (the pane is even narrower than that in Howard's Electron layout because the sidebar consumes ~120px) and the input remained at the 140px floor with the buttons wrapped to a second row.</p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/outline-search.tsx</code> — outer flex container got <code>flex-wrap</code>; input wrapper got <code>min-w-[140px]</code>; the three disabled-capable Tooltip triggers (prev / next / eraser) each got a focusable <code>&lt;span tabIndex={0} className=\"inline-flex\"&gt;</code> wrapper around the button.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Visual verification (disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>1200×800 viewport: full one-row toolbar, no regression. Input width ~176px.</p></li><li><p>600×800 viewport: still single row, input width ~272px. Tooltip on the eraser (&ldquo;Clear highlights and close search&rdquo;) appeared correctly on hover even with zero matches.</p></li><li><p>360×800 viewport (narrow): input held its 140px minimum; toggle buttons + nav buttons wrapped onto a second row inside the pane; the input remained fully usable and the magnifying glass + Clear-X were visible inside the input. The Previous-match tooltip (&ldquo;Previous match (Shift+Enter)&rdquo;) appeared on hover of the disabled button — confirming the span-wrap fix works.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert <code>src/components/outline-search.tsx</code>. Specifically: drop <code>flex-wrap</code> from the outer flex, drop <code>min-w-[140px]</code> from the input wrapper, and remove the three <code>&lt;span tabIndex={0}&gt;</code> wrappers around the disabled-capable Tooltip triggers (replace each <code>&lt;span&gt;...&lt;Button&gt;</code> pattern with the Button as the direct child of <code>TooltipTrigger asChild</code>). No API changes, no shared component changes.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-bubblemenu-pane-boundary-clip-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-bubblemenu-pane-boundary-clip-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (BubbleMenu clipped to content pane boundary)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The Tiptap BubbleMenu (the floating format toolbar) is now physically clipped to the content-pane wrapper at all viewport widths via Floating UI's <code>flip</code>, <code>shift</code>, <code>size</code>, and <code>hide</code> middlewares — each given the content-pane element as the overflow boundary. When the menu's intrinsic width exceeds the available space, <code>size</code> caps its max-width to the boundary and the toolbar wraps to a second row (<code>flex-wrap</code> on the menu's class). The menu can therefore never extend outside the content pane horizontally or vertically — it cannot cover the outline pane's search input, search-results row, or pane toolbar at any width.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Symptom Howard saw (reproduction confirmed via screencapture of his narrow Electron window):</strong> at narrow Electron window widths the floating format toolbar still crossed the boundary between the outline pane on the left and the content pane on the right. The toolbar's body landed on top of the search input + the &ldquo;N of N matches&rdquo; row + the Expand / Generate buttons in the outline pane. Selection in the reproduction was &ldquo;outlines&rdquo; near the top of the first User Guide paragraph.</p><p><strong>Why the earlier same-day fix (responsive placement) wasn't enough:</strong> the prior commit set <code>placement: 'bottom'</code> under 768px and disabled flip — that addressed VERTICAL overlap (menu no longer sat above the editor's own toolbar) but did NOTHING about HORIZONTAL extent. The bubble menu is intrinsically ~413px wide (8 buttons + dividers + the violet Reformat cluster with the &ldquo;MOST POWERFUL&rdquo; badge). At narrow Electron widths the content pane is only ~400px wide — so even with <code>shift</code> trying to slide the menu inward, the menu simply could not fit and overflowed the right edge of the content pane visually onto the outline pane. <code>shift</code> needs a feasible-fit space; when the floating element is wider than the boundary, it has nowhere to slide to.</p><p><strong>Root cause restated:</strong> two coupled problems. (1) The middlewares had no <em>boundary</em> argument, so Floating UI was overflow-detecting against the viewport, not against the content-pane element — meaning &ldquo;inside the viewport&rdquo; was satisfied by sitting on top of the outline pane. (2) Even with the correct boundary, the menu was wider than the boundary, so no amount of shifting would fit it inside without also constraining its width.</p><p><strong>The fix (three coordinated changes):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Boundary on every middleware.</strong> A new <code>contentPaneRef</code> is attached to the outer wrapper <code>&lt;div data-testid=\"content-pane\"&gt;</code>. The ref's element is passed as <code>boundary</code> to <code>flip</code>, <code>shift</code>, <code>hide</code>, and <code>size</code> in the BubbleMenu's <code>options.middleware</code>. Now &ldquo;overflow&rdquo; is measured against the editor pane, not the viewport.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>size</code> middleware caps max-width.</strong> When the menu would otherwise be wider than the available width inside the boundary, <code>size.apply()</code> sets <code>maxWidth</code> on the floating element to the available width (minus 8px padding). This forces the menu to fit horizontally.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>flex-wrap</code> on the menu's class.</strong> When <code>size</code> shrinks the menu's max-width, the buttons wrap onto a second row instead of being clipped. The visual result at narrow widths: a 2-row toolbar that lives entirely inside the content pane.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Re-enabled flip.</strong> With a proper boundary, <code>flip</code> is safe to re-enable — it now flips between top/bottom only when needed AND only inside the boundary. The previous <code>flip: false</code> hard-disable was a workaround for the missing boundary and is no longer needed. Narrow-width default placement stays <code>'bottom'</code> via <code>useIsMobile()</code> because narrow-pane selections frequently sit near the top of the editor, so anchoring below avoids an immediate flip in the common case.</p><p><strong>Why this is more robust than just flipping placement:</strong> placement is a 1D decision (above vs. below the selection). The actual problem was 2D — horizontal extent across a pane boundary. No choice of placement could have fixed it: a menu wider than the pane will always overflow the pane edge. Boundary clipping + size capping addresses the actual geometric constraint instead of trying to dance around it.</p><p><strong>Visual verification (disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>820×800 viewport (matches Howard's narrow Electron width). Three selection positions: top, middle, bottom of editor content. In all three: bubble fully contained inside content pane, zero intersection with outline pane, zero intersection with search input. The bubble wraps to 2 rows (height ~80px) and stays at width ~389px inside the 405px content pane.</p></li><li><p>1200×800 viewport. Two selection positions (top, middle). Bubble single row (height ~46px, width ~413px) inside the 671px content pane — classic desktop layout preserved, no regression.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and immediately after every screenshot. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/content-pane.tsx</code> — added <code>contentPaneRef</code>; attached it (with <code>data-testid=\"content-pane\"</code>) to the outer wrapper; rewrote the BubbleMenu's <code>options</code> to include <code>boundary</code> on <code>flip</code> / <code>shift</code> / <code>hide</code> / <code>size</code>, re-enabled <code>flip</code>, added <code>size.apply</code> capping max-width to the boundary; added <code>flex-wrap</code> to the menu's className.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> — added <code>data-testid=\"outline-pane\"</code> to the outer wrapper (used by the probe to assert no-overlap; harmless in production).</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert the two files. The previous (broken at narrow widths) behavior returns. The fix is self-contained in <code>options</code> and <code>className</code> on the BubbleMenu plus a ref + testid; no API changes, no shared component changes.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-search-revert-compress-only-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-search-revert-compress-only-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Search filter reverted to compress-only; hidden-set machinery removed)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The outline search view-shaper is reverted to a <em>compress-only</em> reshape. Search no longer removes nodes from the rendered tree; it only flips <code>isCollapsed</code>. Matches and their ancestors are expanded; every other node with children is collapsed. Every node remains a visible row, and chevron-expand works on every branch — even branches the filter compressed away.</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Why we corrected yesterday's design:</strong> The 2026-06-07 iteration introduced a real hidden-set on the <code>Outline</code> object so non-matching, non-ancestor nodes were removed from the rendered row list entirely. That \"fixed\" a perceived bug (sibling rows of ancestors stayed visible) — but the real consequence was that navigation got blocked: the user could not chevron-open any branch that the filter had compressed away. After reviewing the resulting behavior, Howard clarified the actual UX intent: \"When we search a string, the outline unfolds just enough to show nodes with those terms and their ancestors. However, any of the outline items compressed by the search can still be opened manually.\" In other words, search reshapes by collapsing, not by hiding. The hidden-set approach was a wrong abstraction for what the search is actually supposed to do.</p><p><strong>What was undone (versus 2026-06-07):</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>Outline.searchHiddenNodeIds</code> and <code>Outline.searchFilterTerm</code> fields removed from <code>src/types/index.ts</code> — backward compatible (older outlines simply lose the unused fields).</p></li><li><p><code>NodeItem</code>'s early-return-<code>null</code> render guard (driven by the hidden set) removed. The recursive pass-through of the hidden set to child <code>NodeItem</code>s removed.</p></li><li><p>The status chip above the tree (\"Filtered: '…' · N hidden · Clear\") removed.</p></li><li><p><code>handleClearSearchFilter</code> in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> removed (no chip → no Clear button → no handler).</p></li><li><p>The <code>onClearSearchFilter</code> prop on <code>OutlinePane</code> removed; both mount points updated.</p></li><li><p><code>externalClearSignal</code> prop on <code>OutlineSearch</code> + the local <code>useEffect</code> that reset the internal search term removed. Same with the <code>externalClearSignal</code> state and the auto-bump effect that lived in <code>OutlinePane</code>.</p></li><li><p>The <code>onApplySearchView</code> signature dropped its <code>options</code> argument (the <code>searchTerm</code> field was only used to drive the chip; <code>restrictToOpen</code> was never consumed in the parent).</p></li><li><p>Expand All / Collapse All handlers reverted to plain <code>isCollapsed</code> toggles — they no longer destructure away the (now-gone) hidden-set fields.</p></li><li><p>The User Guide's Search section, Help-chat APP_CONTEXT (renderer + API route), and the relevant docs/outlines content all rewritten to describe compress-only behavior.</p></li></ul><p><strong>The new rule (one paragraph):</strong> <code>handleApplySearchView</code> takes the list of matched node IDs. It computes the set of ancestors of those matches, then walks every node in the outline. Matches, ancestors of matches, and the root are forced to <code>isCollapsed=false</code>. Every other node with children is set to <code>isCollapsed=true</code>. Whether a non-match, non-ancestor node renders is now purely a function of its parent's <code>isCollapsed</code> — exactly how every other expand/collapse operation in the app works.</p><p><strong>Why this is the correct algorithm:</strong> a node A that is an ancestor of a match renders expanded. A child B of A — a sibling of the match, not itself a match, not itself an ancestor — gets <code>isCollapsed=true</code>. Because A is expanded, B still renders as a row, but B's children are folded. The user sees \"the matches stand out, the other stuff is here too but quiet, and I can open it if I want.\" That is the UX Howard described.</p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Visual verification via a disposable web-mode Playwright probe against the running dev server on port 9002. Probe deleted after the run.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> and <em>IdeaM - Current Features.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> WERE synced in this same run (both were authorized).</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-settings-polish-legacy-items-answer-quality-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-settings-polish-legacy-items-answer-quality-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Settings: legacy plan removed + items + Answer quality)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Context:</strong> Settings polish pass covering three independent fixes Howard explicitly confirmed. All UI-copy work, no behavior changes.</p><p></p><p><strong>Fix 1 — Removed the “Legacy plan info” button from Settings → Account.</strong></p><ul><li><p>The Account section now shows only the Subscription Plan badge and a single CTA: <em>Manage Subscription</em> (Stripe portal) for paid users, or <em>See plans</em> for free / trial users. The third “Legacy plan info” ghost button was removed entirely.</p></li><li><p>The <code>AIPlanDialog</code> component itself was left in place as dead code (not imported anywhere now) rather than deleted, since it may host content reused by future UI. The import was removed from <code>src/components/settings-dialog.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p>Reverse: re-add <code>import AIPlanDialog from './ai-plan-dialog';</code> and the <code>&lt;AIPlanDialog&gt;</code> wrapper around a ghost button after the See plans / Manage Subscription block.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Fix 2 — Renamed the “Confirm before deleting nodes” toggle to “Confirm before deleting items,” and ran a scoped consistency pass on user-visible “node” strings across the app.</strong></p><ul><li><p>The Settings toggle label + its description text now say “items” / “items or branches.”</p></li><li><p>Consistency pass renamed user-visible &ldquo;node(s)&rdquo; to &ldquo;item(s)&rdquo; in these surfaces: Delete dialog title (“Delete Item?”), all toolbar tooltips + aria-labels in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code> (Add sibling item, Delete item, Show or hide all items, Share branch — select an item first, etc.), the three context-menu items in <code>node-item.tsx</code> (Add Sibling Item, Rename Item, Delete Item) and its “this item was refreshed” title, the multi-select toolbar count (“X items selected”), every <code>node</code>-mentioning toast in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> (“Items Deleted”, “Item Duplicated”, AI-command toasts “I deleted that item” / “I couldn’t find an item matching”, the bulk-color and bulk-tag toasts, Already-in-Second-Brain, recovered-outline count, paste “target item”), the bulk-delete window.confirm prompt, the &ldquo;Item Prefix&rdquo; dialogs (two), the loading display “X / Y items,” the <code>content-pane.tsx</code> empty state (“Select an item”), the three type-switch toasts (“This item is now…”), the “Saved to Item” toast, every <code>live-books-dialog.tsx</code> toast (“X items refreshed,” “No items were approved,” “user-edited item”), and the four <code>outline-search.tsx</code> aria-labels / tooltips (“Search item names,” “Search item content,” “Search only open items”).</p></li><li><p>Intentionally LEFT as “node” — surfaces that teach the concept rather than label an action: the <strong>Keyboard Shortcuts</strong> dialog (a learning cheatsheet, not action labels), the User Guide tutorial steps in <code>initial-guide.ts</code> that introduce “node” as the underlying concept, the Help-chat APP_CONTEXT strings (same teaching purpose, mirrored in <code>help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> + <code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code>), CSS variable names (<code>--node-red</code>, etc., internal identifiers), database / file-format fields, and the <code>docs/outlines/</code> content that describes the data model. Per CLAUDE.md value-based-naming principle: UI labels say &ldquo;item,&rdquo; tutorial/explanation can still say &ldquo;node.&rdquo;</p></li><li><p>Reverse: replace-all “item” → “node” / “items” → “nodes” across the eight component files touched, OR git-revert this commit.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Fix 3 — Renamed the “AI Reasoning Depth” Settings header to “Answer quality” and updated the three dropdown option labels to drop &ldquo;depth&rdquo; (mechanism) in favor of outcome wording.</strong></p><ul><li><p>Header: “AI Reasoning Depth” → “Answer quality.” Description text: “Choose how thorough AI responses should be. Can be overridden per-request.”</p></li><li><p>Options (defined in <code>src/types/index.ts</code> <code>AI_DEPTH_CONFIG</code>): Quick — Fast and concise; Standard — Balanced quality and speed; Thorough — Slower but more complete. The <code>deep</code> internal key now displays as “Thorough.” The internal type name <code>AIDepth</code> and the localStorage key <code>aiDepth</code> were intentionally NOT renamed — those are developer-facing identifiers and renaming them would force a migration with no user benefit.</p></li><li><p>Reverse: in <code>src/types/index.ts</code>, restore the three AI_DEPTH_CONFIG entries to their previous labels (Quick / Standard / Deep) + descriptions (“Fast, surface-level response” / “Balanced depth and speed” / “Thorough analysis with extended reasoning”); in <code>settings-dialog.tsx</code>, restore the &ldquo;AI Reasoning Depth” header + description.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Documentation status:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — no mention of the old toggle name or &ldquo;AI Reasoning Depth&rdquo; in the User Guide content; no edits needed. Tutorial &ldquo;node&rdquo; mentions intentionally preserved per the do-not-touch rule.</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — no specific mention of the toggle or the AI-depth header; no edits needed.</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same; no edits needed.</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> — the underlying app architecture is unchanged; no edits needed.</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. ✓</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Visual verification:</strong> short disposable web-mode Playwright probe at <code>tests/_settings-polish-check.js</code> drove the running dev server on port 9002, opened the Settings dialog, and asserted: (1) NO &ldquo;Legacy plan info&rdquo; button present; (2) the toggle reads “Confirm before deleting items”; (3) the header reads “Answer quality” (old &ldquo;AI Reasoning Depth&rdquo; gone); (4) the dropdown options contain no &ldquo;depth&rdquo; (observed: “Quick — Fast and concise | Standard — Balanced quality and speed | Thorough — Slower but more complete”). All four assertions green. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool. Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launch and after every screenshot. Probe + screenshots deleted after the run.</p><p></p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
