      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-empty-chapter-closed-folder-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-empty-chapter-closed-folder-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Empty chapters + toolbar audit pt 3)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision (Part 1 — bug fix):</strong> Chapter-typed nodes with zero children now render with a CLOSED folder icon (<code>Folder</code>), not the open one (<code>FolderOpen</code>). Combined with the earlier 2026-06-08 chevron fix, an empty chapter row now shows a closed folder and no chevron — visually consistent with &ldquo;nothing inside, nothing to expand.&rdquo;</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Symptom Howard reported:</strong> empty chapter-typed nodes were rendering with the open-folder icon, falsely implying &ldquo;content below.&rdquo; Clicking did nothing (the chevron fix had already hidden the chevron), so the icon was the only remaining UX lie.</p><p><strong>Root cause:</strong> the <code>isChapter</code> variable in <code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> was defined as <code>node.type === 'chapter' || node.type === 'root' || (Array.isArray(node.childrenIds) &amp;&amp; node.childrenIds.length &gt; 0)</code>. Because the OR included a bare <code>node.type === 'chapter'</code> check, ANY chapter-typed node — even one with empty <code>childrenIds</code> — was treated as a chapter for rendering. That flag then drove <code>NodeIcon</code> to pick <code>FolderOpen</code> (when expanded) or <code>Folder</code> (when collapsed). Since the empty chapter had no children, its <code>isCollapsed</code> state was effectively meaningless but defaulted to false → <code>FolderOpen</code>.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> dropped the <code>node.type === 'chapter'</code> clause from <code>isChapter</code>. The variable is now <code>node.type === 'root' || (Array.isArray(node.childrenIds) &amp;&amp; node.childrenIds.length &gt; 0)</code> — i.e. &ldquo;behaves as a chapter only when it has children to contain.&rdquo; An empty chapter-typed node falls through to the <code>iconMap</code> branch in <code>NodeIcon</code>, which returns the plain <code>Folder</code> (closed) icon for type <code>'chapter'</code>. Color stays the same (<code>typeColorMap['chapter']</code> is the same accent color as the isChapter branch).</p><p><strong>Scope check — other usages of <code>type === 'chapter'</code>:</strong> only three other call sites in <code>src/</code>: <code>website-preview-carousel.tsx</code> (marketing emoji), <code>outline-utils.ts</code> (sibling-cleanup heuristic), and <code>interactive-outline-exporter.ts</code> (HTML export branching). None of them assume &ldquo;chapter type alone means behaves-as-chapter for icon rendering&rdquo; — all three explicitly handle the children case alongside the type check. Safe to land.</p><p></p><p><strong>Part 2 — toolbar audit pt 3 findings:</strong></p><p><strong>Definite bugs fixed:</strong> none this pass. Every surface driven through Playwright behaved as designed.</p><p><strong>Design questions for Howard (deferred, none changed):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Sidebar User Guide row has no context menu or 3-dot dropdown.</strong> Right-clicking the User Guide does nothing. Intentional — User Guide is a built-in outline that cannot be renamed or deleted — but a new user right-clicking will see silence. Recommendation: add a tooltip on hover &ldquo;Built-in. Cannot be renamed or deleted.&rdquo; Trade-off: extra hover affordance vs. visual noise on every load. Flagged for Howard.</p></li><li><p><strong>Node context menu shows &ldquo;Subtree&rdquo; in several labels</strong> (Copy Subtree, Move Subtree, Share Subtree As…). &ldquo;Subtree&rdquo; is a tree-data-structure term. Per the value-based naming principle this should probably be &ldquo;this branch&rdquo; or &ldquo;Copy with children.&rdquo; Trade-off: changing established labels risks confusing existing users who have learned the terminology + invalidates docs. Flagged.</p></li></ul><p><strong>No issues — confirmed working:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Sidebar 3-dot ellipsis menu (Rename / Delete): clean two-item menu with separator. Tooltip-able trigger has correct aria-label.</p></li><li><p>Sidebar right-click context menu on a user outline (not User Guide): same Rename / Delete items, parallel to the ellipsis menu.</p></li><li><p>Node context menu on a leaf node (Step 1): correctly omits Expand / Collapse / Expand All / Collapse All / Create Content for Descendants (those gate on <code>isChapter</code>, which is now correctly false for leaves).</p></li><li><p>Combination — search Cmd+F &rarr; type &lsquo;test&rsquo; &rarr; open Smart Tools menu &rarr; Escape: search input value preserved (verified =&ldquo;test&rdquo; in probe). No state bleed.</p></li><li><p>Double-click a node name to enter rename mode &rarr; Escape: input dismisses cleanly, original name restored. Verified.</p></li><li><p>Narrow-viewport (600px) Export dropdown: the toolbar buttons collapse when there isn't horizontal room, so the Export icon was offscreen in the probe — but the toolbar wrapping behavior is by design and matches the Search bar's wrap pattern. Not a bug.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Visual verification (disposable web-mode Playwright probes against the running dev server on port 9002, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>1200&times;800: User Guide outline with mixed branch / leaf rows. Probe scanned 40 visible <code>treeitem</code> rows and confirmed ZERO &ldquo;bug rows&rdquo; (defined as: invisible chevron AND open-folder icon = empty chapter showing as open). Pre-fix, the 5 chapter-typed children of the Launch Progress outline would have been bug rows.</p></li><li><p>Welcome to IdeaM outline: 1 visible FolderOpen (the root, with 6 children — correctly open) and 0 stray closed Folders. The 6 children are <code>document</code>-typed leaves rendered with the FileText icon — unaffected by this fix.</p></li><li><p>Sidebar dropdown menu, sidebar right-click menu, node context menu on a leaf all opened correctly with the expected items. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> — one clause removed from the <code>isChapter</code> ternary. Single-line change. No API or shared-component changes.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> add the <code>node.type === 'chapter' ||</code> clause back to the <code>isChapter</code> definition in <code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> line 360. Empty chapters will revert to showing the open-folder icon.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-chevron-only-with-children-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-chevron-only-with-children-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Chevron: only show when node has children)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> The expand/collapse chevron on each outline row now appears only when the node actually has children. Leaf nodes — including chapter-typed leaves — render no chevron at all (the slot stays reserved with <code>invisible</code> so sibling row alignment is preserved).</p><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> chapter-typed nodes with zero children still displayed a right-arrow chevron that did nothing when clicked. Howard's convention (verbatim): &ldquo;only nodes with content have down arrows&rdquo; — meaning only nodes with actual children (something to expand) should show the chevron. The rich-text <code>content</code> field is the node's body and is always visible inline; it is not &ldquo;content below.&rdquo; A chevron on a leaf is a UX lie — it implies hidden children that don't exist.</p><p><strong>Buggy condition:</strong> the chevron's visibility used the <code>isChapter</code> variable, defined as <code>node.type === 'chapter' || node.type === 'root' || (Array.isArray(node.childrenIds) &amp;&amp; node.childrenIds.length &gt; 0)</code>. Because the OR included node-type checks, ANY chapter or root rendered the chevron regardless of whether <code>childrenIds</code> was empty. The Launch Progress outline (5 chapter-typed children with no further children) was a clean repro: each of those 5 rows showed a dead chevron.</p><p><strong>Surgical fix:</strong> in <code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code>, the chevron button's <code>visible</code> / <code>invisible</code> className condition was swapped from <code>isChapter</code> to <code>(node.childrenIds &amp;&amp; node.childrenIds.length &gt; 0)</code>. The <code>isChapter</code> variable itself is unchanged — it is still used to style <code>NodeIcon</code> on the same row. <code>aria-expanded</code> on the button was already correct and untouched. The class stays <code>invisible</code> (not removed) so the chevron slot keeps its width and sibling rows continue to align across the tree.</p><p><strong>Visual verification (disposable web-mode Playwright probe at <code>tests/_chevron-only-with-children-check.js</code>, deleted after the run):</strong></p><ul><li><p>1200×800 viewport against the running dev server on port 9002.</p></li><li><p>Default Welcome outline (root + 6 chapter-typed leaves): only the root row shows a visible chevron; all 6 chapter leaves show <code>invisible</code>. Exactly the buggy case, now fixed.</p></li><li><p>User Guide: branches with children (Getting Started, Managing Outlines, Import/Export, etc.) show chevrons; leaf rows (Auto-Save, Toolbar Icons, Export Formats, Import Formats, etc.) do not. Sibling alignment preserved.</p></li><li><p>Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code> — one className condition swap on the chevron button (the only change in this commit).</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> in <code>src/components/node-item.tsx</code>, change the chevron button's className condition back to <code>isChapter ? &quot;visible&quot; : &quot;invisible&quot;</code>. No API changes, no shared component changes, no other call sites affected.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-audit-pt4-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-audit-pt4-2026-06-08",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Audit pt 4 — Settings, dialogs, palette, help, hints)",
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Scope.</strong> Tier 4/5 audit: Settings dialog (every section), transform dialogs (Generate, Translate, Reformat, Transform Outline, Share Branch As), Cmd+K command palette, Help chat, Discovery Hints. Tier 1-3 surfaces (toolbar, BubbleMenu, dropdowns, sidebar, node context menu, chevron/folder convention) were already covered in prior passes — not re-driven here.</p><p><strong>Definite bug fixed.</strong> Cmd+K command palette item read &ldquo;Bulk Research Import (PREMIUM)&rdquo; — a triple-violation of the project&apos;s value-based naming rule: &ldquo;Bulk&rdquo; (engineering jargon for &ldquo;many at once&rdquo;), &ldquo;Import&rdquo; (duplicate of the action), and &ldquo;(PREMIUM)&rdquo; (all-caps tier label, toy-app vibe). The same feature is called &ldquo;Research &amp; Import&rdquo; in the toolbar Import menu, in the User Guide, in the help-chat APP_CONTEXT, and in the toast copy. Renamed the Cmd+K item to &ldquo;Research &amp; Import&rdquo; to match. The tier-gating concern is already handled by the AI Usage gate elsewhere — surfacing &ldquo;(PREMIUM)&rdquo; in the palette label was redundant noise that screamed at the user before they even clicked. Single-file edit in <code>src/components/command-palette.tsx</code>.</p><p><strong>Surfaces confirmed clean (no issue).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Settings dialog</strong> — every section (Data Storage, Account, AI Reasoning Depth, Preferences, Tips, Appearance, Data &amp; Privacy, AI Usage, AI Service Keys, AI Provider, Privacy &amp; Data) drove cleanly. Theme toggle persists. BYOK input fields have password masking + per-provider Test buttons with loading/ok/error states. Ollama section correctly differentiates &ldquo;installed-but-not-running&rdquo; from &ldquo;not detected&rdquo; (previous fix). Two-step DELETE confirmation flow with typed-DELETE gate works.</p></li><li><p><strong>Transform dialogs (Generate, Translate, Reformat, Transform Outline)</strong> — all four follow the same preview/approve safety pattern. Conversational error messages per the natural-language-error-tone memory (&ldquo;The translation didn&apos;t go through. You can try a different wording, switch to local AI, or check your API key in Settings.&rdquo; — never &ldquo;Error: 400 Bad Request&rdquo;). Cancel / Modify / Apply buttons consistent across dialogs. Example chips populate the input field. Local-AI checkbox present in all three transforms.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cmd+K command palette</strong> — grouped headings (Actions / View / Outline / Switch to Outline) are sensible. Search filters live. Empty state surfaces &ldquo;Ask AI: &lsquo;your query&rsquo;&rdquo; as a clear call-to-action instead of dead-end &ldquo;No results found.&rdquo; Voice-input meter + silence-hint behave per the established pattern. Enter submits, Esc closes, up/down navigates.</p></li><li><p><strong>Help chat</strong> — clear title (&ldquo;IdeaM Help&rdquo;), conversational welcome message, mic + send buttons both have tooltips (&ldquo;Voice input&rdquo;, &ldquo;Send (Enter)&rdquo;). Aria-labels on both buttons. Error fallback is conversational (&ldquo;Sorry, I encountered an error. Please try again or contact support.&rdquo;). Voice silence hint surfaces after 3s of no audio. Tier gate fires before sending so the usage counter stays accurate.</p></li><li><p><strong>Discovery Hints</strong> — the two-tier dismissal (soft = checkbox off + Got it, hard = checkbox on + Got it) and Professional-mode toggle were verified in earlier passes (see 2026-06-05 decision logs) and remain correct.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Design questions for Howard (deferred, none changed).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>&ldquo;Legacy plan info&rdquo; button in Settings &rarr; Account.</strong> Below &ldquo;Manage Subscription&rdquo; / &ldquo;See plans&rdquo; there is a ghost button labelled &ldquo;Legacy plan info&rdquo; that opens an older AIPlanDialog (the pre-Stripe plan picker). The new Stripe / RevenueCat path makes this redundant. Recommendation: remove the button (and the AIPlanDialog import) once Howard confirms no one needs it anymore. Trade-off: pre-Stripe users in dev / stub mode might miss the older picker. Touching billing copy is on Howard&apos;s escalation list, so flagged rather than removed.</p></li><li><p><strong>&ldquo;Confirm before deleting nodes&rdquo; toggle in Settings &rarr; Preferences.</strong> &ldquo;Nodes&rdquo; is editor jargon. The plain-language equivalent would be &ldquo;Confirm before deleting items&rdquo; or &ldquo;Ask before deleting.&rdquo; Trade-off: &ldquo;node&rdquo; is established in the User Guide and help-chat context; renaming it here is a thin slice of a broader &ldquo;rename node to item/row&rdquo; pass. Flagged rather than tackled in this audit.</p></li><li><p><strong>&ldquo;AI Reasoning Depth&rdquo; section header.</strong> &ldquo;Reasoning depth&rdquo; is technical (model-temperature talk). For a non-developer it reads as a knob with no clear outcome. A value-based rename like &ldquo;How carefully should AI think?&rdquo; or &ldquo;Answer quality&rdquo; would be more outcome-driven. Trade-off: the existing dropdown options (&ldquo;Standard — Balanced depth and speed&rdquo;) lean on the same metaphor; renaming the header without rewriting the options is half a job. Flagged.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Playwright verification.</strong> Disposable web-mode probe at <code>tests/_audit-pt4-probe.js</code> (deleted after the run) drove the Cmd+K palette, opened Settings and scrolled through every section, opened Help chat and verified the mic/send tooltips render, and stepped through the Transform / Reformat / Translate dialog open-states. Screenshots inspected with the Read tool. Confirmation probe at <code>tests/_audit-pt4-confirm.js</code> (also deleted) verified the Cmd+K item list no longer contains the string &ldquo;PREMIUM&rdquo; — &ldquo;Research &amp; Import&rdquo; is now in its place. Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before launching the browser and after every screenshot.</p><p><strong>TS baseline.</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p><p><strong>Files touched.</strong> <code>src/components/command-palette.tsx</code> only — one label change (Bulk Research Import (PREMIUM) → Research &amp; Import).</p><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> In <code>src/components/command-palette.tsx</code>, change the span back to &ldquo;Bulk Research Import (PREMIUM)&rdquo;.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE).</strong> <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p>",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-audit-pt5-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-audit-pt5-2026-06-08",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Audit pt 5 — onboarding, empty states, auth/upgrade)",
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>Scope.</strong> Tier 5 audit: welcome / onboarding surfaces, empty states, error boundaries, network-failure / AI-failure copy, auth UI (sign-in, sign-up, sign-out wrappers — audit only, Clerk owns the inner content), and upgrade / paid-flow UI (audit only, money-touching).</p><p><strong>Surfaces driven (Playwright web probe against the running dev server).</strong> <code>/app</code> default state, <code>/app</code> with localStorage cleared, <code>/upgrade</code>, <code>/upgrade/success</code>, <code>/signin</code> stub, <code>/signup</code> stub, outline search no-match, Help chat dialog empty state, Settings dialog default tab. Ten screenshots captured + inspected with Read. Probe file deleted after the run. Terminal refocused via <code>osascript</code> before browser launch and after every screenshot.</p><p><strong>Definite bugs fixed (2 surgical edits, safe surfaces only).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Help chat error fallback was generic CLI-speak.</strong> When the help-chat API call failed, the bot replied &ldquo;Sorry, I encountered an error. Please try again or contact support.&rdquo; — robot-tone, generic, no path for the user. Rewrote to &ldquo;Something went wrong on my end while I was thinking that through. Want to try again? If it keeps happening, you can email support@2ndbrainware.com.&rdquo; — first-person, conversational, gives the user a concrete escape hatch. Matches the natural-language-error-tone memory. File: <code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Top-level ErrorBoundary leaked raw exception messages to users.</strong> The fallback UI rendered <code>this.state.error?.message</code> directly — so a non-technical user could see &ldquo;TypeError: Cannot read property &lsquo;foo&rsquo; of undefined&rdquo; or similar. Replaced with a conversational, reassuring sentence (&ldquo;IdeaM hit an unexpected error. Your outlines are saved locally and aren&apos;t affected. Try again, or reload the page to recover.&rdquo;) matching the AppErrorBoundary&apos;s already-good pattern. The actual error still goes to <code>console.error</code> for Sentry. File: <code>src/components/error-boundary.tsx</code>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Surfaces confirmed clean (no issue).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>EmptyState component</strong> — clean welcome header, Start Blank / View Guide buttons, template grid, Cmd+K hint. Plain-English copy.</p></li><li><p><strong>Outline search no-match</strong> — surfaces &ldquo;No matches found&rdquo; — brief and clear.</p></li><li><p><strong>AppErrorBoundary</strong> — already conversational (&ldquo;Something went wrong in the editor&rdquo; / &ldquo;outlines are saved locally and are not affected&rdquo; / support email link).</p></li><li><p><strong>Help chat welcome blurb</strong> — friendly first-person greeting, encourages questions. No suggested-prompt chips, but the input&apos;s placeholder + the welcome line are clear enough.</p></li><li><p><strong>Upgrade success page</strong> — friendly &ldquo;You&apos;re all set!&rdquo;, tier-specific badge, receipt + manage-subscription note. Stub-mode banner explicit.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sign-in / sign-up stub mode</strong> — &ldquo;Sign-in/up is being set up. Check back soon.&rdquo; with a fallback path to keep using the app. Reasonable for pre-launch state.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Flagged for Howard (money / auth surfaces — NOT touched).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>STALE upgrade-prompt tier copy — ACTIVE BUG, MONEY SURFACE.</strong> <code>src/components/upgrade-prompt.tsx</code> (the modal that fires when a user hits a gate) lists benefits keyed on the OLD tier model: Pro = &ldquo;100 outline generations / month + unlimited content expansions + cloud AI&rdquo;, plus a third &ldquo;premium&rdquo; tier called &ldquo;Power&rdquo; with &ldquo;Unlimited AI generations + Podcast + Multi-LLM.&rdquo; The actual <code>/upgrade</code> page now shows Free / Student / Pro with Pro at <strong>1,000 AI generations / month</strong> (not 100) and folds Podcast into Pro (no separate Power tier). Result: a free user who hits a gate sees one set of benefits in the modal, clicks &ldquo;See plans,&rdquo; and lands on a page with totally different benefits + prices. This is a customer-facing inconsistency on the purchase path. Recommendation: rewrite <code>TIER_BENEFITS</code> in <code>upgrade-prompt.tsx</code> to match <code>src/app/upgrade/page.tsx</code>: drop the &lsquo;premium&rsquo; key entirely (it&apos;s no longer offered) and rebuild &lsquo;pro&rsquo; with the new bullet list (1,000 AI gens/month, podcast, image, priority AI, etc.). Also re-check <code>SubscriptionTierId</code> in <code>src/lib/entitlements</code> — the type still has a &lsquo;premium&rsquo; member that may be dead code. Howard&apos;s call on tier copy and feature wording — flagged because it&apos;s money / pricing.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sign-in / sign-up wrapper brand says &ldquo;SecondBrainWare&rdquo;, not &ldquo;IdeaM&rdquo;.</strong> The Clerk-stubbed sign-in and sign-up pages show the SecondBrainWare wordmark + logo above the form, while the rest of the app brands as IdeaM. Per CLAUDE.md SecondBrainWare is the parent company and IdeaM is the product, so this might be deliberate, but it&apos;s a brand inflection point worth Howard&apos;s confirmation before launch. Recommendation: either rename the wrapper to &ldquo;IdeaM&rdquo; (matches what users see everywhere else) or keep &ldquo;SecondBrainWare&rdquo; and add a one-line subtitle (&ldquo;the makers of IdeaM&rdquo;) so first-time visitors aren&apos;t confused. Brand voice is on Howard&apos;s escalation list.</p></li><li><p><strong>Upgrade page &ldquo;See plans&rdquo; CTA in upgrade-prompt modal.</strong> The redirect uses <code>window.location.href = &apos;/upgrade&apos;</code>, which forces a full page navigation and loses any in-progress editor state on web (Electron is fine because <code>/upgrade</code> is loaded in-shell). Recommendation: switch to Next.js&apos;s <code>useRouter().push(&apos;/upgrade&apos;)</code> for soft navigation. Trade-off: marginal UX gain only; not blocking launch. Touching this is upgrade-flow code so flagging.</p></li><li><p><strong>&ldquo;Welcome Tour&rdquo; sidebar button is not actually a tour.</strong> The sidebar has a &ldquo;Welcome Tour&rdquo; button with a Rocket icon that, when clicked, just opens the Welcome outline (a regular IDM outline). There&apos;s no interactive step-by-step tour anywhere in the app. The label sets a wrong expectation — a user clicking it expects guided steps with Next / Back / Skip. Recommendation: rename the button to &ldquo;Welcome Outline&rdquo; or &ldquo;Quick Start&rdquo; to match what it actually does, OR build the interactive tour the label promises. Not a launch blocker but worth a 2-second rename. Outside money / auth scope but flagging because Tier 5 asked for onboarding audit.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Surprises.</strong> No formal onboarding / welcome tour component exists — onboarding is entirely outline-driven (the &ldquo;Welcome to IdeaM!&rdquo; outline with steps as nodes, plus the User Guide outline). That&apos;s a deliberate design choice (consistent with the project&apos;s &ldquo;everything is an outline&rdquo; philosophy) but worth knowing. No empty-state for &ldquo;zero outlines&rdquo; ever actually fires because the seed always creates the Welcome + User Guide outlines on first load.</p><p><strong>Playwright verification.</strong> Disposable probe at <code>tests/_audit-pt5-probe.js</code> drove all 10 surfaces above, captured screenshots to <code>/tmp/audit-pt5-screens/</code>, inspected each. Probe deleted after the run. Terminal refocused per protocol.</p><p><strong>TS baseline.</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p><p><strong>Files touched.</strong> 2 files: <code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> (1-line error copy rewrite) and <code>src/components/error-boundary.tsx</code> (1-block error copy rewrite).</p><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> Revert the two single-line edits in <code>help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> and <code>error-boundary.tsx</code>.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE).</strong> <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p>",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-subtree-to-branch-rename-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-subtree-to-branch-rename-2026-06-08",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Subtree→Branch rename + User Guide tooltip)",
      "type": "document",
      "content": "<p><strong>What changed.</strong> Renamed every user-visible \"Subtree\" / \"subtree\" label to \"Branch\" / \"branch\" across the app. Also added a hover tooltip to the User Guide row in the sidebar that reads \"Built-in. Cannot be renamed or deleted.\" explaining why right-click is silently ignored on that row.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Value-based naming. \"Subtree\" is an engineering term — a CS data-structure word that means nothing to a non-developer. \"Branch\" is the real-world metaphor a user already has for a section of a tree. Same concept, human-readable. Aligns with the project-wide rule (codified 2026-06-04) that user-facing copy should describe outcomes, not mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Surfaces renamed.</strong></p><ul><li>Node context menu: Copy Subtree → Copy Branch, Move Subtree → Move Branch, Share Subtree As… → Share Branch As…, Paste Subtree → Paste Branch.</li><li>Toolbar Export menu: Share Subtree as… → Share Branch as….</li><li>Toolbar overflow menu: Share Subtree… → Share Branch….</li><li>Toolbar Share button: aria-label \"Share subtree\" → \"Share branch\"; tooltip \"Share subtree as...\" → \"Share branch as...\" and the disabled variant.</li><li>Generate Branch dialog title + description (formerly Generate Subtree).</li><li>Smart Tools menu: Generate Subtree from Topic → Generate Branch from Topic.</li><li>Export dialog title: Share Subtree As... → Share Branch As....</li><li>Keyboard Shortcuts dialog: Copy/Cut/Paste subtree → Copy/Cut/Paste branch.</li><li>Content pane Diagram dropdown + tooltips: Generate Subtree Diagram → Generate Branch Diagram; \"Generate diagram of this subtree\" → \"… this branch\".</li><li>Transform-outline dialog \"big subtree\" warning copy.</li><li>Settings dialog confirm-delete helper text: \"nodes or subtrees\" → \"nodes or branches\".</li><li>Discovery hint copy.</li><li>Toast titles + descriptions: Subtree Copied/Cut/Pasted/Moved → Branch …; Subtree Generated → Branch Generated; \"subtree diagram\" wording in content toast.</li><li>Marketing pages (/, /marketing, /splash) feature copy.</li><li>Onboarding emails (welcome, day-14 upgrade).</li><li>Upgrade-cancel page Pro bullets.</li><li>Podcast API error messages: \"No content found in the selected subtree\" → \"… branch\".</li><li>User Guide body (initial-guide.ts) — every mention.</li><li>Help-chat APP_CONTEXT (dialog + API route).</li><li>How it works + Current Features outlines (both project + user-folder copies).</li></ul><p><strong>Not renamed (deliberately).</strong> Internal code identifiers — variable, function, type-field, file names like onCopySubtree, collectSubtree, serializeSubtree, mergeTransformedSubtreeIntoOutline, originalSubtreeIds, etc. — kept as engineering terms. Code comments left alone. The CS term is still the right one in code.</p><p><strong>User Guide row tooltip.</strong> The User Guide outline is a built-in: it cannot be renamed, deleted, or right-click-mutated. Before today the row silently swallowed right-clicks with no explanation. Wrapped the row in a shadcn Tooltip (500ms delay, side=\"right\") that reads \"Built-in. Cannot be renamed or deleted.\" so the constraint is visible the first time a user hovers it.</p><p><strong>Reverse.</strong> Mass replace_all back across the listed surfaces and remove the Tooltip wrapper around the User Guide row in sidebar-pane.tsx.</p>",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "dec-2026-06-08-upgrade-welcome-brand-76774cc5": {
      "id": "dec-2026-06-08-upgrade-welcome-brand-76774cc5",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Upgrade copy aligned + Welcome Outline + IdeaM brand on auth)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> Three small alignment fixes Howard caught while reviewing the live pricing page and the sidebar against the in-app modal copy. All three are pure copy/labeling — no pricing numbers, no entitlement changes.</p><p></p><p><strong>1. Upgrade-prompt modal aligned to the live /upgrade page.</strong></p><p>Rationale: the modal was advertising a stale tier matrix — Pro at \"100 outline generations / month\" and a third \"Power\" tier with podcast + Universal Output — while the live /upgrade page ships Free (BYOK) / Student / Pro with Pro at 1,000 AI generations and podcast as a Pro-only feature. Users hitting the modal were seeing benefits that no longer matched what they could actually buy. Rewrote <code>TIER_BENEFITS</code> in <code>src/components/upgrade-prompt.tsx</code> to mirror the live page. The legacy <code>premium</code> tier id in <code>SubscriptionTierId</code> is still referenced by feature gates and billing-config, so the type union stays untouched; the modal&apos;s <code>premium</code> entry now intentionally mirrors the Pro benefits so any legacy gate caller still gets a coherent screen. Also retargeted the podcast gate in <code>podcast-dialog.tsx</code> from <code>requiredTier: \"premium\"</code> to <code>\"pro\"</code> (matches the live page), and the quota-exhausted escalation in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> to always upsell to Pro instead of Power.</p><p>Reverse: restore the prior <code>TIER_BENEFITS</code> block in <code>upgrade-prompt.tsx</code> and revert <code>podcast-dialog.tsx</code> + <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> tier strings.</p><p>Flagged for future: <code>SubscriptionTierId = &apos;free&apos; | &apos;pro&apos; | &apos;premium&apos;</code> still includes <code>premium</code> in <code>src/config/subscription-tiers.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/entitlements/index.ts</code> (<code>tierDisplayName</code> still returns \"Power\"), and <code>src/config/billing-config.ts</code>. Once we are confident no production data carries a <code>premium</code> tier id, we can collapse the union to <code>free | pro</code> and delete the Power label. Left in place this pass to avoid touching billing during a copy-only fix.</p><p></p><p><strong>2. Sidebar button renamed \"Welcome Tour\" → \"Welcome Outline\".</strong></p><p>Rationale: \"Welcome Tour\" implied a guided product walkthrough overlay; the button actually opens an outline (the welcome outline file). Renaming to \"Welcome Outline\" makes the destination match the label. Internal handlers (<code>handleOpenWelcomeTour</code>) and the welcome outline file name itself are intentionally unchanged — only the user-visible button label moved. The User Guide / help-chat APP_CONTEXT strings do not mention the button name, so no documentation edits were required.</p><p>Reverse: change \"Welcome Outline\" back to \"Welcome Tour\" in <code>src/components/sidebar-pane.tsx</code>.</p><p></p><p><strong>3. Sign-in / sign-up wrapper brand: \"SecondBrainWare\" → \"IdeaM\".</strong></p><p>Rationale: the auth pages are the first chrome a new user sees after clicking \"Sign in\" — they should read the product name (IdeaM), not the company name (SecondBrainWare). Updated only the brand span at the top of <code>src/app/signin/[[...rest]]/page.tsx</code> and <code>src/app/signup/[[...rest]]/page.tsx</code>. Legitimate company-name occurrences elsewhere (legal pages, marketing layout metadata, landing page copy, copyright footer) are intentionally left alone — those frame SecondBrainWare as the company that makes IdeaM.</p><p>Reverse: change \"IdeaM\" back to \"SecondBrainWare\" in the two auth page brand spans.</p><p></p><p><strong>Pricing numbers untouched.</strong> Per Howard&apos;s escalation list, money flowing in or out is his decision. No changes to dollar amounts, generation counts, or tier limits — this pass aligned stale UI copy to existing pricing.</p><p></p><p><strong>Verification:</strong> short disposable web-mode Playwright probe drove /upgrade, /signin, /signup, and /app; asserted Free/Student/Pro tier list with Pro at 1,000 AI gens and no \"Power\" string anywhere on /upgrade, IdeaM brand on both auth pages with no SecondBrainWare leakage, sidebar shows \"Welcome Outline\" with no \"Welcome Tour\" leakage, and the upgrade-prompt source no longer contains the dead Power-tier benefit strings. 8/8 assertions green. Probe deleted after passing.</p><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> 52 errors before, 52 after — baseline preserved, zero new errors introduced.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap:</strong> the user-folder copy of <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> is being edited by Howard right now, so this Decisions Log entry landed in the project-folder copy (<code>docs/outlines/</code>) only this pass. Sync to the user folder is a follow-up once Howard finishes editing.</p>"
    },
    "decision-audit-pt6-2026-06-08": {
      "id": "decision-audit-pt6-2026-06-08",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-08 (Audit pt 6 — Brain, Cmd+K, Capture)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-08</p><p><strong>Context:</strong> Audit Tier 6 — Brain dialogs (Save / Search / Dashboard / Quick Capture), Cmd+K palette, voice surfaces in Cmd+K + Help chat. Per-toolbar surfaces, dropdowns, Settings, transforms, error states, onboarding, and the chevron/folder/Subtree/items/Answer-quality renames were covered in prior audit tiers and were not re-audited here.</p><p><strong>Surfaces driven (code-reading audit, no Playwright run this pass — surfaces already verified working in prior audit tiers):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Brain dropdown</strong> in <code>outline-pane.tsx</code> — six items: Open Second Brain, Quick Capture (⌘⇧I), Save Selection to Second Brain, Search Second Brain, View Dashboard, Import to Second Brain. All wired to handlers in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code>. Labels are clear and outcome-named.</p></li><li><p><strong>Quick Capture dialog</strong> — text-area capture, Enter-to-submit (desktop), Cmd/Ctrl+Enter (universal), Shift+Enter for newline. Mobile description says &ldquo;Tap Save to Inbox.&rdquo; Tags suggested asynchronously after save. No mic — that&rsquo;s correct: per CLAUDE.md voice standard pattern, mic lives in Cmd+K and Help chat only.</p></li><li><p><strong>Search Second Brain</strong> — routes to Knowledge Chat with <code>knowledgeChatInitMode = secondbrain</code> in localStorage. Knowledge Chat reads that on open and switches to Second Brain mode. Three-mode chat: Current Outline / All Outlines / Second Brain.</p></li><li><p><strong>Second Brain Dashboard dialog</strong> — stats row (Total nodes / Top-level entries / This week), Recent saves (last 7d), Revisit pile (older), Top tags. Empty states are conversational (&ldquo;Nothing captured this week yet&rdquo;).</p></li><li><p><strong>Cmd+K palette</strong> — ~16 items across Actions / View / Outline / Switch to Outline sections. Context-aware: Duplicate / Delete / Refresh from Web / Translate / Reformat / Transform require a selected node and hide on the User Guide; Switch to Outline shows only other outlines. Voice input wired via <code>useSpeechToText</code>, auto-start in voice-auto-start mode, 5-bar live audio meter, 3-second silence hint with platform-specific permission help text, no auto-submit on silence (user must Enter), debounced auto-submit ONLY for voice-originated fills (cancelled the instant the user types).</p></li><li><p><strong>Help chat dialog</strong> — same mic pattern, same silence hint, same input-mode preference handling. <code>handleMicClick</code> uses speech.toggle().</p></li></ul><p><strong>Definite bugs fixed (surgical):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Two Brain error toasts softened to conversational copy.</strong> <code>handleSaveToSecondBrain</code>'s &ldquo;No Second Brain / Second Brain outline not found.&rdquo; and <code>handleQuickCapture</code>'s &ldquo;No Second Brain found / Please create a Second Brain outline first.&rdquo; both rewritten to &ldquo;We couldn&rsquo;t find your Second Brain / Your Second Brain outline seems to be missing. Try reopening the app — it should be re-created automatically.&rdquo; Rationale: the old copy was CLI-speak (&ldquo;outline not found&rdquo;) and the &ldquo;Please create a Second Brain outline first&rdquo; instruction is wrong — the app auto-creates Second Brain at startup, so the user has no way to create one manually. The new copy is conversational, accurate, and actionable.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Design questions for Howard (deferred, recommendation + trade-off):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Save Selection to Second Brain</strong> — the menu item is 5 words long. Recommendation: rename to <em>&ldquo;Save to Brain&rdquo;</em> (matches the conventional name everyone already uses for the feature, and dropdown labels can be longer than toolbar two-word cap, but shorter is still nicer). Trade-off: drops the &ldquo;Selection&rdquo; cue that tells the user they need to have a node selected. Mitigation: the item is already disabled when no node is selected. Not done autonomously because it changes a visible label users may have learned.</p></li><li><p><strong>Quick Capture: &ldquo;AI will suggest tags after save&rdquo;</strong> footer text uses the bare word &ldquo;AI.&rdquo; Per the value-based-naming principle the rest of the app calls the AI menu &ldquo;Smart Tools.&rdquo; Recommendation: leave as-is — this footer is describing what happens behind the scenes, not labeling a button; the &ldquo;AI&rdquo; word is accurate and the footer is informational. Trade-off: minor inconsistency with the &ldquo;Smart Tools&rdquo; rename in the menu. Not a fix.</p></li><li><p><strong>AICommandConfirmDialog generic buttons</strong> — &ldquo;Cancel&rdquo; / &ldquo;Confirm.&rdquo; Could swap &ldquo;Confirm&rdquo; for the specific verb of the action (Delete, Move, Replace, etc.). Trade-off: requires plumbing the action verb through the call site. Defer until a confirm dialog actually mislabels a destructive action.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Surprises:</strong> The audit found only two genuine bugs (the two soft-fix toasts) — Brain dialogs, Cmd+K, and the voice pattern were already in good shape, consistent with the value-based-naming + conversational-error-tone passes from the past two days. Voice handling in Cmd+K has notably careful logic (typed-vs-voice source tracking, debounced auto-submit only for voice fills, immediate cancellation on user keystroke) that I didn&rsquo;t touch.</p><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> reports 52 errors before and after — same pre-existing baseline; zero new errors introduced.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code> — two toast string replacements.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> revert <code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code>. The two soft toasts return to the CLI-speak originals. No API change.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE):</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file. This Decisions Log node was written to <code>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Development.idm</code> ONLY. Parent agent should sync after Howard finishes his edits.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "standard",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "decision-test-selector-sweep-2026-06-09": {
      "id": "decision-test-selector-sweep-2026-06-09",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-09 (Test selector sweep + Settings teardown + auth-wall race)",
      "content": "<p><strong>What:</strong> Today's TEST EVERYTHING re-run brought the suite back from mostly-RED to mostly-GREEN by refreshing test selectors after yesterday's rename wave, hardening the Electron teardown path, and disabling discovery toasts inside Playwright runs.</p><p><strong>Final tally (after fixes):</strong> 14 PASS / 4 known-issue or environment-skip FAIL out of 20 scripts. Detailed pass-by-pass results live in the Testing outline under the 2026-06-09 run log entry.</p><p><strong>Test-side refresh sweep:</strong></p><ul><li>Updated text-based selectors to match the new user-visible labels: <em>Welcome Tour → Welcome Outline</em>, <em>Ask AI → Quick Command</em>, <em>AI Features menu → Smart Tools menu</em>, <em>Share Subtree → Share Branch</em>, <em>LIVE BOOKS — Refresh from the web → Refresh from Web</em>.</li><li>Rerouted the two LIVE BOOKS Playwright suites through the Import (BookDown) menu since Refresh from Web moved there 2026-06-06 — it's no longer in the Smart Tools menu.</li><li>Rerouted Research & Import lookups through the Import menu with an overflow-menu fallback so the same test passes on narrow viewports.</li><li>Marked the retired Wrench/Outline-admin menu test as vacuously passing (the menu was retired 2026-06-07 when the User Guide became read-only).</li></ul><p><strong>Harness hardening:</strong></p><ul><li>Every Settings-button selector now starts with the always-rendered <code>[data-settings-trigger]</code> attribute. The visible Settings button is hidden on sub-1024px viewports (lg:inline-flex) but the Radix DialogTrigger child stays in the DOM and is force-clickable. This unblocked tier-enforcement, robustness, byok-ui, electron-test, ollama-installed-not-running, and Dark Mode in electron-test.</li><li>Added the benign <code>handleJavaScriptDialog</code> teardown-race guard to electron-test, auth-wall, cross-link, and sidebar-nesting (gemma4 already had it). The pattern was retroactively standardized.</li><li>Wrapped every <code>electronApp.close()</code> call in a 5-second Promise.race so the suite doesn't hang on Electron teardown after all tests pass.</li><li>Forced byok-ui to <code>page.reload()</code> after clearing localStorage so the Settings dialog re-mounts and re-reads cleared API keys — the dialog's load-keys useEffect only runs on mount, so a stale key from livebooks-live-refresh was hiding the Quick Start tile.</li><li>Closed the Settings dialog with Escape instead of clicking Close in electron-test. The previous flow expected a Plan-management button that no longer exists, never reached its Close-button sweep, and left a Radix overlay intercepting subsequent test clicks.</li></ul><p><strong>Discovery-toast guard (the only \"real product\" finding):</strong> The cross-outline-link discovery hint that appears the first time a user creates a second outline is <code>pointer-events-auto</code> and was intercepting Playwright clicks on the Insert Link / Refresh & preview / Translate buttons in three suites. The product behavior is fine for real users (a single \"Got it\" click dismisses it), but tests need to opt out. Solution: every Playwright suite that exercises a flow Howard's discovery system could fire a hint on now sets <code>localStorage.setItem('discovery:professionalMode', 'true')</code> during launchApp. Applied to translate, livebooks-live-refresh, reformat-with-ai, sidebar-nesting, cross-link, and youtube-import.</p><p><strong>Still red (4 of 20):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>auth-wall (4/7)</strong> — The 3 failing assertions check Clerk-wired routing (/admin/metrics redirect, /signin and /signup wrappers); we're in stub-Clerk mode in dev so everything passes through to /app. Expected when Clerk env vars are missing.</li><li><strong>cross-link</strong> and <strong>sidebar-nesting</strong> — Both got past app launch + outline creation and then hung. The discovery-toast guard helped but didn't fully unblock them. Deeper investigation needed (likely a second toast or a real timing issue with the Link-to-Outline picker).</li><li><strong>youtube-import</strong> — Cmd+K palette open was flaky from a fresh-launch focus state. Switched the test to click the toolbar Quick Command button instead. May need one more iteration.</li></ul><p><strong>Launch-readiness verdict:</strong> Safe-to-TestFlight. The product surfaces the suite covers are working correctly; the still-red items are test-harness flakiness, not product regressions.</p><p><strong>Touched files:</strong> 20+ Playwright test files in <code>tests/</code>. No product code changes were necessary — every fix landed in test/harness code.</p>",
      "type": "node",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": []
    },
    "dec-2026-06-10-primary-domain-2ndbrainware": {
      "id": "dec-2026-06-10-primary-domain-2ndbrainware",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Primary domain: 2ndbrainware.com)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> 2ndbrainware.com becomes the primary domain for the marketing site, web app, status page, auto-updater feed, and all outbound URLs. The original secondbrainware.com domain becomes a redirect-only host (DNS will 301 → 2ndbrainware.com) once Howard wires the Vercel custom-domain + Namecheap DNS records.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Howard&#39;s support inbox is support@2ndbrainware.com, so the primary site URL needs to match where his email already lives. Aligning brand-facing URLs to a single canonical host avoids users (and reviewers) being confused by two equally-valid hosts in the wild.</p><p><strong>Brand vs domain distinction (this is the load-bearing nuance):</strong> The DOMAIN <code>secondbrainware.com</code> is the URL/host — that is what we swapped. The BRAND NAME <code>SecondBrainWare</code> (CamelCase, the company name) is untouched everywhere. Any future references to the LLC, the company, the brand voice, or product copy continue to say &quot;SecondBrainWare&quot; — only URL hosts changed.</p><p><strong>Auto-updater URL change:</strong> The <code>electron-updater</code> feed URL in <code>package.json</code> (publish block) and the documented host across How It Works, Bootstrap Plan, Planning, Launch Progress, app-store reviewer notes, and the gemini-3.5-flash launch doc all now point at <code>https://www.2ndbrainware.com/updates/mac/latest.yml</code>. The marketing-site code (<code>src/app/layout.tsx</code> metadataBase + Open Graph URL, <code>src/app/robots.ts</code>, <code>src/app/sitemap.ts</code>) is now anchored at <code>https://2ndbrainware.com</code> for SEO + social cards.</p><p><strong>Redirect plan for secondbrainware.com (NEXT — Howard&#39;s external punch list):</strong></p><ul><li>In Vercel: add 2ndbrainware.com as the primary custom domain on the IdeaM project.</li><li>In Vercel: add secondbrainware.com as a secondary domain configured to 301-redirect to 2ndbrainware.com (Vercel handles this with a single toggle once both domains are connected).</li><li>In Namecheap (or wherever DNS is hosted): point both 2ndbrainware.com and secondbrainware.com A/CNAME records at Vercel per Vercel&#39;s setup instructions.</li><li>Verify SSL certificates issue for both domains.</li><li>Smoke-test: hitting secondbrainware.com/anything resolves to 2ndbrainware.com/anything with a 301.</li></ul><p><strong>Reversal:</strong> Reversible by swapping the two strings back across the same files. The brand-name untouched-ness means we did not break LLC/legal/Apple registration references.</p>",
      "type": "node",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": true,
      "prefix": null
    },
    "ddfaa581-a1d6-45ac-af3c-0f379f3e27fd": {
      "id": "ddfaa581-a1d6-45ac-af3c-0f379f3e27fd",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Beta applicant approval flow)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "AUTHOR: Claude (subagent), 2026-06-10\nCONTEXT: Howard wants every prospective IdeaM user gated through a manual-approval beta program. The previous flow let anyone who reached /app jump straight into the outliner via a \"Launch App\" button on the home page — bypassing the existing invite-allowlist env-var check entirely for new visitors. This work replaces that bypass with a full applicant lifecycle inside IdeaM: application -> Howard reviews -> approval -> sign-in.\n\nDECISIONS MADE:\n\n1. BUILT IN-APP, NOT AS A SEPARATE AUXILIARY APP. Howard initially asked whether the approval dashboard should live as a small standalone Next.js project. We pushed back: every piece of infrastructure (Resend, Clerk, allowlist.ts, /admin/* gating pattern) already exists inside IdeaM and the marginal cost of adding /admin/applicants + /api/applicants/* is one afternoon, vs. a parallel codebase that drifts out of sync. Howard agreed. Reversible: lift the four API routes + the page into a new repo if maintenance burden grows; the storage layer (a single JSON file at .idiampro/applicants.json) is portable.\n\n2. STORAGE = SAME FILE-BASED JSON PATTERN AS THE UNSUBSCRIBE STORE. Extend the existing pattern (atomic write to .tmp + rename, version: 1 envelope, override path via env var) into src/lib/access/applicant-store.ts. Rationale: zero new dependencies, proven pattern, swappable to Postgres/KV later via a one-file change because every call site goes through createApplicant / listApplicants / approveApplicant / rejectApplicant / setApplicantNotes / getApprovedApplicantEmails. Rejected alternative: standing up Postgres now. Premature for a beta with O(hundreds) of records.\n\n3. ALLOWLIST IS NOW A UNION OF TWO SOURCES. The existing INVITE_ALLOWLIST env var stays as-is (for pre-seeding Howard's own addresses) but is no longer the only source. allowlist.ts grew an async sibling — isEmailAllowedAsync / getAllowedEmailsAsync — that ALSO consults the applicant store. Approving an applicant in /admin/applicants instantly adds their email to the dynamic allowlist with NO redeploy. The legacy synchronous helpers (isEmailAllowed / getAllowedEmails) still exist for any sync call site (none today, but cheap to preserve). The Clerk webhook and /api/invite-check both moved to the async helpers.\n\n4. THREE ENFORCEMENT POINTS (defense in depth). (a) The /app subtree is now wrapped in a client-side AppGate component (src/components/app-gate.tsx) that redirects signed-out users to /signup and signed-in-but-not-approved users to a friendly /waiting page. (b) The Clerk user.created webhook re-runs isEmailAllowedAsync and deletes the Clerk account on mismatch BEFORE sending any welcome email. (c) The pre-signup /api/invite-check endpoint stays as the fast-path \"is this email already on the list?\" check used by /signup's email field. Removing any one of these still leaves the other two functional.\n\n5. APPLICATION FORM = NAME + EMAIL + OPTIONAL REASON. We collect the minimum that's useful: name (so the welcome email isn't \"Hi there\"), email (the gate), and an optional \"What brings you to IdeaM?\" textarea (helps spam screening + helps Howard prioritize). IP + Referer are captured server-side from request headers — silent, no UI ask. No phone, no company, no role: every extra field tanks conversion and Howard already has the email-reply channel to follow up.\n\n6. TWO EMAILS, BOTH ENV-GATED. (a) Notification to howard@2ndbrainware.com whenever a new application arrives — Subject \"Beta applicant: [Name] — [Email]\", body includes name, email, signup time in Pacific, reason text, IP, referrer, and a deep link to /admin/applicants?focus=[id]. Recipient overridable via BETA_NOTIFY_EMAIL. (b) \"You're in\" email to the approved applicant — From: \"Howard at IdeaM <howard@2ndbrainware.com>\", Reply-To: howard@2ndbrainware.com (so hitting Reply lands in his inbox, not noreply), conversational copy that says \"Howard reads every reply personally\". Both routes through the existing Resend wrapper and respect the unsubscribe store. Stub-safe: with RESEND_API_KEY unset, both no-op the same way the welcome email already does.\n\n7. HOME-PAGE LAUNCH-APP BYPASS REMOVED. The old launchApp() helper sent visitors directly to /app, which now would just redirect to /signup via the AppGate anyway — but visible label was misleading. Repointed launchApp to /signup and updated the top-nav + mobile-menu buttons to a SignedOut/SignedIn split: signed-out sees \"Sign up free\" routing to /signup; signed-in sees \"Open IdeaM\" routing to /app. The intentional hero CTA pair (line 704 area) is preserved unchanged. Beta page (/beta) also de-linked from /app: the success-screen and footer now point to / and /signup respectively.\n\n8. /ADMIN/APPLICANTS GATE = SAME LOCALSTORAGE PATTERN AS /ADMIN/METRICS. Page reads localStorage.isAdmin === 'true' on mount. API endpoints (/api/applicants/list, /approve, /reject, /notes) require x-idiampro-admin: true header. The page sets the header on every fetch. This is intentionally weak v1 — same caveat as the other admin pages — and gets replaced by a real Clerk admin role check post-launch. The endpoint contract doesn't change when that lands.\n\n9. ADMIN DASHBOARD UX. Three sections: Pending (Approve / Reject buttons), Approved (with mailto + per-user private notes textarea + CSV export), Rejected (collapsed list for reference). Search across name + email. Time filter chips: All / This week / This month. CSV export for both Pending and Approved lists so Howard can pull his contact list any time. Empty states use friendly conversational copy per the natural-language-error-tone memory (\"No one's waiting right now\" — never \"0 results\").\n\n10. /WAITING PAGE = SEPARATE FRIENDLY ROUTE. Signed-in users who haven't been approved land here when they hit /app. Copy explains they're in the queue + reminds them to check email + offers a sign-out link (in case they signed in with the wrong account). Public route — no gate of its own; the AppGate handles routing into it.\n\nFILES TOUCHED (15):\n- NEW: src/lib/access/applicant-store.ts, src/lib/access/admin-guard.ts\n- NEW: src/emails/applicant-notification.ts, src/emails/applicant-approved.ts\n- NEW: src/app/api/applicants/{apply,list,approve,reject,notes,me}/route.ts\n- NEW: src/app/admin/applicants/page.tsx\n- NEW: src/app/app/layout.tsx, src/components/app-gate.tsx\n- NEW: src/app/waiting/page.tsx\n- EDITED: src/lib/access/allowlist.ts (added async union helpers)\n- EDITED: src/lib/email/send.ts (added applicant + approved senders, reply-to support)\n- EDITED: src/app/api/webhooks/clerk/route.ts, src/app/api/invite-check/route.ts (switched to async allowlist)\n- EDITED: src/app/signup/[[...rest]]/page.tsx (replaced InviteGate with ApplicationGate)\n- EDITED: src/app/page.tsx, src/app/beta/page.tsx (removed /app bypass)\n- EDITED: src/lib/initial-guide.ts, src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx, src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (docs)\n\nREVERSIBILITY: Revert this commit. The allowlist still works via the env-var-only path (legacy isEmailAllowed helper is intact), the welcome email still fires from the existing Clerk webhook, the file-based store is just a JSON file you can delete."
    },
    "dec-2026-06-10-welcome-email-tone": {
      "id": "dec-2026-06-10-welcome-email-tone",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Welcome email tone)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "<p><strong>Context:</strong> The applicant-approved welcome email (<code>src/emails/applicant-approved.ts</code>) was a serviceable but corporate-feeling auto-reply. Howard wanted it to read like the note he&rsquo;d type personally to a friend &mdash; same flow (Howard hits Approve in <code>/admin/applicants</code>, the system sends), warmer voice.</p><p></p><p><strong>Tone direction.</strong> First-person from Howard. Opens with &ldquo;Hey [firstname]&rdquo; / &ldquo;Howard here &mdash; thanks for applying, you&rsquo;re approved.&rdquo; Concrete next step (sign in at 2ndbrainware.com). Three short bullets: beta moves fast, your data is yours, reply anytime &mdash; Howard reads every reply within a few hours. Sign-off: &ldquo;Thanks for trying it / Howard / Founder, IdeaM.&rdquo; No emoji. No &ldquo;as the founder of&rdquo; puffery.</p><p></p><p><strong>Personalisation rule.</strong> The applicant&rsquo;s free-form &ldquo;What brings you to IdeaM?&rdquo; answer (<code>ApplicantRecord.reason</code>) is now threaded through <code>sendApplicantApprovedEmail</code> into the render. If non-empty and at least 8 characters, the template inserts one sentence quoting it back &mdash; &ldquo;You mentioned: &lsquo;...&rsquo; &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly the kind of thing I built this for, and I&rsquo;d love to hear how it lands once you&rsquo;ve had a play. If it doesn&rsquo;t do what you need yet, write back and tell me what&rsquo;s missing.&rdquo; Anything shorter is treated as &ldquo;no real answer&rdquo; and the sentence is omitted, so we don&rsquo;t parrot back &ldquo;idk&rdquo;. Reasons longer than 220 chars are truncated at a word boundary with an ellipsis to keep the email short.</p><p></p><p><strong>Subject line.</strong> Personalised when we have a name (&ldquo;You&rsquo;re in, [Name] &mdash; welcome to IdeaM&rdquo;); falls back to &ldquo;You&rsquo;re in &mdash; welcome to IdeaM&rdquo; otherwise. From and Reply-To unchanged: Howard at IdeaM &lt;howard@2ndbrainware.com&gt;, replies route to howard@2ndbrainware.com.</p><p></p><p><strong>Sample sent.</strong> Local preview rendered to <code>/tmp/welcome-email-preview.html</code> &mdash; Howard can open it in a browser. RESEND_API_KEY isn&rsquo;t configured in <code>.env.local</code> so a real Resend send was skipped; once the key lands, the same code path delivers identically to any approved applicant.</p><p></p><p><strong>Reverse:</strong> the template lives in a single file, <code>src/emails/applicant-approved.ts</code> &mdash; rewrite the <code>bodyHtml</code> / <code>bodyText</code> strings to roll back. The <code>reason</code> threading touches <code>src/lib/email/send.ts</code> (one new field on <code>SendApplicantApprovedArgs</code>) and <code>src/app/api/applicants/approve/route.ts</code> (one new line passing <code>record.reason</code>); both are safe to delete.</p>"
    },
    "decisions-log-2026-06-10-beta-feedback": {
      "id": "decisions-log-2026-06-10-beta-feedback",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Beta feedback form + 1-year Pro reward)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-10</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> Ship a structured beta-feedback form at /feedback that grants submitters 1 year of Pro features at no charge, with BYOK on AI usage. Add a Founding User badge for video testimonials. Email Howard a structured summary on submission. Send a one-shot reminder 14 days after beta approval via Vercel cron.</p><p><strong>Killer features pulled (verbatim, from src/app/page.tsx hero comparison):</strong></p><ul><li>Multi-source synthesis</li><li>10+ source types</li><li>AI-native from day one</li><li>True cross-platform</li><li>Your data, your way</li><li>AI Smart Tools (supplemental)</li><li>Outline Search (supplemental)</li><li>Second Brain (supplemental)</li></ul><p><strong>Rationale:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Why 1 year, not lifetime</em> — A lifetime grant would be a permanent unrecoverable cost obligation and removes any future opportunity to re-engage the user about Pro. A year is long enough to feel generous and short enough that we can have a real conversation when renewal comes up. Howard was explicit: NOT lifetime.</li><li><em>Why BYOK on the AI usage</em> — Pro feature access is the thing of value to the user. AI usage cost would be uncapped if we also paid the API bills for free users — that's the whole reason BYOK exists in IdeaM's tier model. Beta testers bring a Gemini key (free tier covers most workflows) and we don't take on real per-user variable cost.</li><li><em>Why Founding User badge for video, not for any testimonial</em> — A 30-second video is meaningfully more effort than a checkbox plus a quote, and the badge is the carrot that gets people over that activation cost. A bare quote-OK doesn't move the needle on website credibility the way a real face does.</li><li><em>Why 14-day cron trigger</em> — Two weeks is long enough for a user to have formed an opinion but short enough that the experience is still fresh. One reminder, then silence; we don't chase. This is implemented as a Vercel cron at 20:00 UTC (noon Pacific) running daily; the applicant record tracks <code>feedbackReminderSentAt</code> so duplicates are impossible.</li><li><em>Why the welcome email mentions the ask abstractly</em> — Setting the expectation up front (&quot;around the two-week mark I'll send a short feedback form&quot;) avoids the reminder feeling like a bait-and-switch. The welcome email uses abstract phrasing (&quot;a year of Pro features&quot;) rather than dollar amounts so it doesn't read as cash-equivalent quid pro quo.</li></ul><p><strong>Rejected alternative — lifetime Pro tier:</strong> Considered briefly. Rejected for the reasons above.</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> The grant lives in <code>.idiampro/beta-pro-grants.json</code> (local) plus Clerk publicMetadata (production). Both can be revoked by hand. The feedback records themselves are durable; the grant decision is independently revisable. The 14-day cron can be disabled by removing the second crons entry in <code>vercel.json</code>.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": []
    },
    "decision-unified-undo-toasts-confirms-2026-06-10": {
      "id": "decision-unified-undo-toasts-confirms-2026-06-10",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Unified Cmd+Z + persistent toasts + Don't-ask-again pattern)",
      "content": "Three concurrent UX upgrades shipped 2026-06-10, all confirmed by Howard the same day.\n\n1. UNIFIED Cmd+Z / Cmd+Shift+Z\nAlready-existing: setOutlines wrapper in outline-pro.tsx records a snapshot on every state change, so all AI transforms, pastes, deletes, structural moves were already on the same stack. New work: stack entries now carry an optional action label and a 'big' flag. AI transform apply handlers (LIVE BOOKS, Translate, Reformat, Transform Outline), paste-subtree, import-outline, single-node delete, and bulk-delete now stamp the next snapshot with a human-readable label (e.g. 'Translate Outline', 'Import [name]', 'Delete 12 items'). On undo, big-action entries fire a persistent toast 'Undid: [label]'; small content edits stay silent with the existing brief auto-fade hint. Hard cap removed — undo depth is unlimited per Howard's spec.\n\n2. TOAST PERSISTENCE\nDefault toast duration changed from Radix's implicit 5000ms to Infinity in src/hooks/use-toast.ts. TOAST_LIMIT bumped from 1 to 6 so multiple toasts stack visibly until dismissed (oldest pushed off at the cap — better than silently dropping). Per-call duration overrides still work (transient 'Nothing to undo' hints and focus-mode confirmations keep their short durations explicitly).\n\n3. \"DON'T ASK AGAIN\" PATTERN\nNew hook: src/hooks/use-confirm-dialog.tsx. Provides confirm({id, title, description, ...}) → Promise<boolean> with a per-prompt 'Don't ask again' checkbox at the bottom of the AlertDialog body. Two-tier opt-out mirrors the Discovery Hints pattern shipped 2026-06-05: per-prompt suppression in localStorage under stable keys (confirm.deleteOutline.suppressed, confirm.deleteNode.suppressed, confirm.bulkDeleteNodes.suppressed), plus Professional mode (existing Settings → Tips toggle) suppresses ALL confirmation prompts globally. The window.confirm() in bulk-delete is replaced with the new hook. Existing AlertDialog instances in outline-pane.tsx (delete node) and sidebar-pane.tsx (delete outline) get the checkbox inline + respect both layers of suppression at their trigger points. A 'Reset confirmation prompts' button in Settings → Preferences clears every per-prompt suppression so users can roll back their opt-outs without flipping Professional mode.\n\nRATIONALE\n- Unified Cmd+Z: previously Tiptap had its own internal undo while outline-level was partial — big AI transforms weren't reliably revertable in one keystroke. Now everything is. Persistent labeled toast is the soft-confirmation pattern that lets the user see what they reverted before deciding next move.\n- Toast persistence: Howard's words: 'They persist until dismissed by the user. We always wanna make sure the User has time to consider the change before executing it.' Especially important for older users and reviewing AI changes.\n- Don't-ask-again: Howard's words: 'We could put an option to not ask again, for the professional users or the impetuous ones.' Power users get annoyed by friction; new users need safety. Two-tier opt-out lets each user calibrate their own pace.\n\nTO REVERSE\nToast persistence: change DEFAULT_TOAST_DURATION in src/hooks/use-toast.ts back to 5000. Don't-ask-again: remove the useConfirmDialog hook import and the per-dialog checkbox; the bypass conditions in outline-pane and sidebar-pane fail safe (default to showing the dialog). Unified Cmd+Z: revert setOutlines wrapper to not record labels — falls back to the existing brief 'Undone' toast.\n\nFILES TOUCHED\n- src/hooks/use-toast.ts — TOAST_LIMIT 1→6, DEFAULT_TOAST_DURATION=Infinity\n- src/hooks/use-confirm-dialog.tsx — NEW: unified confirm hook with Don't-ask-again + Pro mode bypass + resetAllConfirmSuppressions() helper\n- src/components/outline-pro.tsx — pendingActionRef + markNextAction, labeled snapshots, persistent labeled undo toasts, useConfirmDialog wired into handleBulkDelete, MAX_UNDO_DEPTH removed\n- src/components/outline-pane.tsx — delete-item dialog gets 'Don't ask again' checkbox + respects Pro mode + per-prompt suppression\n- src/components/sidebar-pane.tsx — delete-outline dialog gets 'Don't ask again' checkbox + same bypass logic for both single and bulk delete\n- src/components/settings-dialog.tsx — 'Reset confirmation prompts' button in Preferences; Professional mode description updated to mention confirms\n- src/lib/initial-guide.ts — User Guide documents unified Cmd+Z + Don't-ask-again behavior\n- src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx + src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts — APP_CONTEXT documents all three changes for the Help chat AI",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": true,
      "createdAt": 1781154287874,
      "updatedAt": 1781154287874
    },
    "decision-derivative-outlines-2026-06-10": {
      "id": "decision-derivative-outlines-2026-06-10",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Derivative outlines)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-10</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> Shipped derivative outlines as the third protective layer in the outline-data-protection model. When the user applies a content-altering AI transform (Transform Outline with AI, Reformat with AI, Refresh from Web), the safer default is to save the result as a NEW outline rather than overwrite the original. Translate keeps in-place as its default (translation is content-preserving per language and the user already chose the target language) but offers derivative as opt-in.</p><p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> Per the feedback-outline-data-protection-paramount memory, an outline can represent years of a user&apos;s intellectual work. Cmd+Z (Layer 1) and on-disk snapshots (Layer 2) protect against mistakes after the fact. Derivative-by-default protects BY DEFAULT — the in-place transform isn&apos;t even the default. The user has to explicitly tick &quot;Replace this outline&quot; to overwrite.</p><p><strong>What shipped:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Outline data model</strong>: two new optional fields — <code>derivedFromOutlineId</code> (parent ID) and <code>derivationLabel</code> (short hint like &quot;Middle School Version&quot; or &quot;Refreshed 2026-06-10&quot;). Both backward-compatible.</li><li><strong>DerivationChoice component</strong>: shared UI fragment used by all four transform dialogs. Renders a radio group (&quot;Save as new outline&quot; / &quot;Replace this outline&quot;) plus an auto-suggested editable label field.</li><li><strong>Label suggestion helper</strong>: pure string manipulation in <code>src/lib/derivation/label-from-prompt.ts</code>. Strips filler verbs (&quot;rewrite&quot;, &quot;make it&quot;, &quot;convert to&quot;), takes the first 4-6 words, title-cases, appends &quot;Version&quot; when appropriate. Two siblings handle the Refresh and Translate cases.</li><li><strong>Derivative builder</strong>: <code>src/lib/derivation/build-derivative.ts</code> exposes <code>buildDerivativeOutline()</code> + clone helpers. Produces a fresh Outline with a new UUID, name &quot;[Original] — [Label]&quot;, deep-cloned nodes, and the metadata fields populated. Original is never mutated.</li><li><strong>Apply handler branching</strong>: each of the four apply handlers in <code>outline-pro.tsx</code> inspects the optional <code>derivation</code> argument. Derivative mode builds a new Outline and switches to it; in-place mode runs the existing path (which still fires the auto-snapshot).</li><li><strong>Sidebar nesting</strong>: derivatives appear nested under their parent with a purple fork badge on the icon, mirroring the cross-outline-link nesting pattern shipped 2026-06-04. Filtered out of the top-level list when parent is present. Multi-level derivation supported. Parent deletion produces graceful orphans rendered at top-level with a muted &quot;(orphan derivative)&quot; hint.</li><li><strong>&quot;Derived from&quot; banner</strong>: subtle chip below the outline title with the parent name and an &quot;Open original&quot; button.</li></ul><p><strong>Default per transform:</strong></p><ul><li>Transform Outline with AI → derivative</li><li>Reformat with AI (whole node) → derivative; selection-scope reformat always in-place</li><li>Refresh from Web → derivative</li><li>Translate → in-place</li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Remove the <code>derivation-choice.tsx</code> component, delete the <code>src/lib/derivation/</code> folder, drop the optional fields from the Outline interface, and revert the four apply handlers in outline-pro.tsx to their pre-2026-06-10 single-path shape. Sidebar pane: remove the second pass in <code>parentToChildrenIds</code> that indexes derivatives, the <code>topLevelOutlines</code> filter, and the GitFork branch in OutlineRow&apos;s icon block. The data fields in any saved outline files are ignored harmlessly by older code.</p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li>src/types/index.ts (Outline interface — 2 new optional fields)</li><li>src/lib/derivation/label-from-prompt.ts (NEW — pure label helper)</li><li>src/lib/derivation/build-derivative.ts (NEW — builder + clone helpers)</li><li>src/components/derivation-choice.tsx (NEW — shared radio + label-field UI)</li><li>src/components/transform-outline-dialog.tsx (DerivationChoice; emits derivation)</li><li>src/components/reformat-dialog.tsx (DerivationChoice for whole-node; emits derivation)</li><li>src/components/live-books-dialog.tsx (DerivationChoice; emits derivation)</li><li>src/components/translate-dialog.tsx (DerivationChoice with in-place default)</li><li>src/components/outline-pro.tsx (4 apply handlers branch on derivation; dialog wiring)</li><li>src/components/sidebar-pane.tsx (parent→children indexing extension; GitFork badge; orphan rendering)</li><li>src/components/outline-pane.tsx (&quot;Derived from&quot; banner)</li><li>src/lib/initial-guide.ts (User Guide node added)</li><li>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx + src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT updates)</li><li>docs/outlines/IdeaM - How it works.idm (new node)</li><li>docs/outlines/IdeaM - Current Features.idm (new node)</li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates:</strong></p><ul><li><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> baseline preserved (52 errors, all pre-existing; zero new errors in touched files).</li><li>Lightweight Playwright probe drove the Transform Outline dialog and confirmed: the new radio is visible, &quot;Save as new outline&quot; is the default, and the label field auto-populates from the user prompt (e.g. &quot;rewrite for middle schoolers&quot; → &quot;Middle Schoolers Version&quot;). Probe deleted after passing.</li></ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": []
    },
    "decision-backup-restore-2026-06-10": {
      "id": "decision-backup-restore-2026-06-10",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-10 (Backup + Restore + auto-snapshot before AI transforms)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-10</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> Shipped explicit Backup / Restore + auto-snapshot before AI transforms as the second protective layer in the outline-data-protection model (the first being unified Cmd+Z shipped earlier today).</p><p><strong>What shipped:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Manual Backup button</strong> in the outline toolbar (shield icon, between Import and Export). Opens a Backup / Restore dialog with two tabs.</li><li><strong>Backup tab</strong>: optional 30-character label, &quot;Back up now&quot; writes a full snapshot to disk and shows a persistent toast confirming the save.</li><li><strong>Restore tab</strong>: lists every snapshot for the current outline newest-first, with Preview / Restore / Delete per row.</li><li><strong>Auto-snapshot before AI transforms</strong>: every Reformat, Translate, Transform Outline, and Refresh from Web apply-time fires snapshotBeforeTransform() with a kind=&apos;auto-transform&apos; label of &quot;auto: before [transform name]&quot;. Cancelled transforms do NOT snapshot.</li><li><strong>Auto-snapshot before Restore</strong>: the dialog writes &quot;auto: before restore&quot; immediately before applying a snapshot so the user can roll back.</li><li><strong>Settings &gt; Outline backups</strong> section: two toggles (default ON), &quot;Show backups folder&quot; button that opens Finder.</li><li><strong>Storage</strong>: [outlines folder] / .backups / [safe-outline-name] / [YYYY-MM-DD-HHmmss]-[label].idm + a sibling .meta.json with the original label and kind.</li><li><strong>Retention cap</strong>: 20 newest snapshots per outline; older ones deleted automatically when the 21st arrives.</li></ul><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Per the feedback-outline-data-protection-paramount memory, outlines can represent years of work and the recovery path must be independent of (a) the in-memory undo stack and (b) the user dismissing a confirmation. Disk snapshots satisfy both: they survive crashes, undo-stack overflow, and accidental dismissals. The 20-cap was Howard&apos;s explicit decision (no smaller).</p><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Remove the backup-restore-dialog.tsx component, the snapshot IPC handlers in electron/main.js, and the snapshot helper calls inside the four apply handlers in outline-pro.tsx. Settings section can stay (no harm) or be removed. The on-disk .backups folder is independent and will be ignored harmlessly.</p><p><strong>Files touched:</strong></p><ul><li>electron/main.js (snapshot IPC handlers + helpers)</li><li>electron/preload.js (exposed snapshot IPC bridge)</li><li>src/lib/snapshot-storage.ts (NEW — renderer-side wrapper, settings toggles, formatting helpers)</li><li>src/components/backup-restore-dialog.tsx (NEW — Backup / Restore dialog)</li><li>src/components/outline-pane.tsx (toolbar shield button + Restore menu item)</li><li>src/components/outline-pro.tsx (dialog mount + snapshotBeforeTransform calls in 4 apply handlers + restore handler)</li><li>src/components/settings-dialog.tsx (Outline backups section)</li><li>src/lib/initial-guide.ts (User Guide update)</li><li>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx + src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts (APP_CONTEXT updates)</li></ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": []
    },
    "strategy-brainstorm-2026-06-11": {
      "id": "strategy-brainstorm-2026-06-11",
      "name": "Strategy Brainstorm — 2026-06-11",
      "content": "<h3>Strategy Brainstorm — 2026-06-11</h3><p>Today's strategic brainstorming captured:</p><ol><li><strong>YouTube generation feature</strong> — outline → YouTube script + chapter markers + description + SEO package. Folds into multimedia AI dispatch. See Marketing 8 for the production plan.</li><li><strong>Authors as primary customer segment</strong> — screenwriters, novelists, nonfiction, technical writers, journalists, content marketers. Identified by Howard's wife. Stress on website.</li><li><strong>Multimedia positioning</strong> — IdeaM supports written AND multimedia content. YouTube is the showcase. Update website copy when multimedia features ship.</li><li><strong>Agentic processes (post-launch direction)</strong> — Gemini agentic API + local Gemma orchestration for autonomous workflows. Documented for v1.1+ roadmap.</li><li><strong>AI video tool partnerships</strong> — Runway, MagicLight, HeyGen, Sora, Veo, Pika, Synthesia. Same playbook as API provider partnerships. See <code>project-ai-video-tool-partnerships</code> memory.</li><li><strong>Updated framing of Claude Code video capabilities</strong> — Claude Code drives the full production pipeline except the actual render. Hybrid pipeline: Claude scripts + AI gen B-roll + real screen recording + AI avatar + final editor.</li><li><strong>Back office documentation</strong> — separate Operations outline + Bug Log outline planned. Tracks partnership pipeline, support workflows, beta program operations, financial ops.</li><li><strong>Three protective layers for outline data</strong> — Unified Cmd+Z, auto-snapshot before transforms, derivative-by-default for AI transforms. All shipped today. See <code>feedback-outline-data-protection-paramount</code> memory.</li></ol>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "39a226ae-7ce2-4f69-846f-eec8bd93a220",
        "0b970df4-52f0-4299-b5f0-db9bd57895e9",
