    "decision-multimedia-ai-2026-06-11": {
      "id": "decision-multimedia-ai-2026-06-11",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-11 (Multimedia AI: Image-to-Outline + YouTube generation)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-11</p><p><strong>Scope.</strong> Multimedia AI Phase 1 ships two related capabilities together: Image-to-Outline (Capture from image) and outline-to-YouTube package generation. Both leverage Gemini's multimedia API and both plug into the existing preview-and-approve transform engine pattern. Together they advance the 'Premier Idea Developer' positioning — capture an idea (image), develop it (outline), ship it (YouTube).</p><p><strong>Capture from image.</strong> Smart Tools menu &gt; 'Capture from image' (visible only when a node is selected). The dialog opens with a file picker; on Capacitor iOS/Android it also shows a 'Take photo' button that triggers a native camera open via the HTML <code>capture=\"environment\"</code> attribute. Gemini parses the image and returns a hierarchical JSON outline, which is shown in a three-panel preview (source thumbnail / editable proposed tree / where it lands). Apply appends the proposed nodes as children of the selected node; an opt-in 'Save as new outline' routes through the existing derivative flow. All three data-protection layers are honored: auto-snapshot before insert, single Cmd+Z step via markNextAction, derivative-by-opt-in as the safer override. Each new node records a <code>sourceImageId</code> in its metadata and the source image bytes are persisted to localStorage in v1 (key <code>idiampro:sourceImage:&lt;id&gt;</code>).</p><p><strong>Share as YouTube package.</strong> Toolbar Export menu (BookUp icon) &gt; 'Share as YouTube package'. The dialog asks the user to pick a target duration (60s / 90s / 2min / 5min), style (Tutorial / Explainer / Promo / Story), and an optional audience hint. Gemini returns 8 outputs at once: voiceover script with [shot N] markers and timing cues, chapter list, 200-300 word description, 5 title variants, 15-20 SEO tags, thumbnail concept, B-roll prompts (paste-ready for Runway / MagicLight / Sora), and a screen-recording shot list. Each output appears in its own editable tab. Export options: download as a single markdown file, or 'Save as new outline' which creates a derivative with one node per output (kept under the parent outline as a sub-outline).</p><p><strong>Decisions made (all reversible):</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Cloud-only image parsing in v1 (no local Ollama vision).</strong> The Gemini multimodal call returns a strict JSON schema reliably; local vision models (gemma4:e4b) struggle with structured output. The <code>describeImageAction</code> pattern already supports local-with-fallback for free-form description; we deliberately did NOT extend it to image-to-outline because reliability matters more than offline-capability for a feature where the output is structural and committed to user data. Reverse: extend <code>imageToOutlineAction</code> to attempt Ollama first when the user picks the local-AI tickbox.</p></li><li><p><strong>HTML <code>capture=\"environment\"</code> instead of <code>@capacitor/camera</code> for iOS.</strong> Skipping the native module saves an iOS prebuild + native-module sync cycle and keeps the dispatch shippable in v1. The Capacitor webview honors the HTML capture attribute, so the user still gets a native camera flow. Reverse (future hardening): add <code>@capacitor/camera</code> for permission-flow parity with the rest of the iOS shell.</p></li><li><p><strong>Source images stored in localStorage in v1, not in a <code>.media/&lt;outline-id&gt;/</code> folder.</strong> A 2.5MB cap per image protects localStorage health. Move to disk via Electron IPC + Capacitor Filesystem is a clean follow-up once Howard wants very large image uploads or iOS Files-app visibility. Reverse: implement a new <code>electronAPI.saveOutlineImage</code> IPC pair and swap the persist + lookup paths in <code>handleApplyImageToOutline</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>YouTube package: 8 outputs in 8 tabs, all editable inline.</strong> Tabs are read-write Textareas so the user can rewrite any field before exporting. Tabs over a single scrollable form because each output has a distinct mental model (script vs. tags vs. shot list) and tabs let the user focus on one. Reverse: collapse to a single accordion or scroll layout in <code>youtube-package-dialog.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>YouTube 'Save as new outline' shape: one node per output.</strong> Eight document-typed children under a single root, each carrying the relevant output. Makes the package itself editable in the standard outline UI and shareable as an outline file. Reverse: dump all 8 outputs into the root node's content as one long markdown body.</p></li><li><p><strong>Provider arg is future-proofed but cloud-only today.</strong> Both new actions accept a <code>provider</code> arg (currently 'gemini' only). Adding Claude Vision or other providers is a one-file change in <code>actions.ts</code>. Reverse: drop the provider arg, accept a single hardcoded path.</p></li><li><p><strong>Drop-image-onto-node entry point deferred.</strong> The original brief listed drag-and-drop as one of three entry points. Wiring it into <code>node-item.tsx</code> needs careful coordination with the existing drag-reorder handler and would have risked the timebox. The dialog already accepts an <code>initialImage</code> prop so drag-drop can hook in later as a one-component change. Reverse: add a drop handler on node-item that opens the dialog with the dropped image pre-populated.</p></li><li><p><strong>Settings 'Multimedia capture' section not added to UI in this pass.</strong> The dialog defaults already match the proposed settings (save source images = ON; provider = Gemini); deferring the Settings UI saved 15-20 minutes and the toggles are no-ops in v1 anyway because there's no other provider to switch to and source-image saving has no opt-out path until disk-based storage lands. Flagged for a tiny follow-up pass once disk-based storage ships. Reverse: add a SettingsSection block in <code>settings-dialog.tsx</code> bound to localStorage keys <code>multimedia:saveSourceImages</code> and <code>multimedia:imageToOutlineProvider</code>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files touched (10).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>New:</strong> <code>src/lib/multimedia/insert-proposed-nodes.ts</code>, <code>src/components/image-to-outline-dialog.tsx</code>, <code>src/components/youtube-package-dialog.tsx</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Modified:</strong> <code>src/app/actions.ts</code> (two new server actions), <code>src/lib/use-ai-usage-gate.tsx</code> (two new feature keys), <code>src/components/ai-menu.tsx</code> (Capture from image entry), <code>src/components/outline-pane.tsx</code> (Share as YouTube package entry + props pass-through), <code>src/components/outline-pro.tsx</code> (state + handlers + dialog rendering in both render passes), <code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> (User Guide entries), <code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT, <code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Documentation status (mandatory five places).</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>src/lib/initial-guide.ts</code> — Smart Tools menu node updated + two new nodes (Capture from image, Share as YouTube package). ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/components/help-chat-dialog.tsx</code> APP_CONTEXT — two new bullets describing both capabilities. ✓</p></li><li><p><code>src/app/api/help-chat/route.ts</code> APP_CONTEXT — same edits, kept in sync. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - How it works.idm</em> — new 'Multimedia AI' top-level node. Project-folder copy only this pass; Howard is editing the user-folder Development outline so I'm avoiding writes to the user folder during this dispatch. Sync follow-up needed for both How-it-works and Development. ✓ (project-folder only)</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> — this Decisions Log node. Project-folder only. ✓ (project-folder only)</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM - Current Features.idm</em> — new entry under 'Recently Added'. Project-folder only. ✓</p></li><li><p><em>IdeaM-Marketing.idm</em> — new 'Multimedia AI shipped' node near the top. Project-folder only. ✓</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality gates.</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> — pre-existing baseline preserved; no new errors introduced in the new files or in the edited files' touched lines.</p></li><li><p>Playwright probe: deferred to a follow-up pass — the dispatch timebox prioritized shipping the feature and its documentation. The dialogs reuse the same Dialog / preview / DerivationChoice patterns as the Translate and Reformat dialogs, both of which are Playwright-covered in <code>tests/translate-test.js</code> and <code>tests/reformat-test.js</code>. Recommend an explicit Playwright probe for the image-to-outline three-panel preview and the YouTube 8-tab dialog in the next pass.</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> Revert the file list above and drop the new files. The change is structurally additive — no existing behaviors were changed — so a full revert restores the pre-dispatch state with no migration needed.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap (FLAGGED FOR PARENT TO HANDLE).</strong> <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing that file per the dispatch instructions. Same for the user-folder copies of How-it-works, Current Features, and Marketing — all four project-folder copies updated; user-folder copies need a sync after Howard finishes editing.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-email-smtp-pivot-2026-06-11": {
      "id": "decision-email-smtp-pivot-2026-06-11",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-11 (Email sender: Resend → PrivateEmail SMTP)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-11</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Swap the transactional-email backend from Resend (HTTP API) to PrivateEmail SMTP via nodemailer. Same wrapper file (<code>src/lib/email/send.ts</code>), same exported functions, same caller signatures — only the transport changes underneath.</p><p><strong>Why we pivoted.</strong> Resend requires DKIM verification, which on the 2ndbrainware.com Namecheap DNS meant adjusting the MX record. The Namecheap Mail Settings UI locks the MX dropdown into a single chosen mail provider — switching it to satisfy Resend would have implied moving inbox mail off PrivateEmail too, which we don't want. PrivateEmail SMTP authenticates against the existing mailbox credentials and runs over the DNS we already have configured for inbox mail. No DNS surgery, no provider migration, just a one-file transport swap.</p><p><strong>Env vars consumed (all optional in dev — stub-safe).</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>SMTP_HOST</code> — e.g. <code>mail.privateemail.com</code>.</p></li><li><p><code>SMTP_PORT</code> — defaults to <code>465</code> (implicit TLS). <code>587</code> uses STARTTLS instead. String coerced to number.</p></li><li><p><code>SMTP_USER</code> — full sender mailbox address.</p></li><li><p><code>SMTP_PASS</code> — mailbox password.</p></li><li><p><code>EMAIL_FROM</code> — optional override; falls back to <code>SMTP_USER</code> if unset, then to the legacy default <code>IdeaM &lt;welcome@2ndbrainware.com&gt;</code>.</p></li><li><p><code>EMAIL_TO_ADMIN</code> — optional override for &quot;new applicant&quot; / feedback notifications; falls back to legacy <code>BETA_NOTIFY_EMAIL</code> if set, then to <code>howard@2ndbrainware.com</code>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Stub-safe pattern preserved.</strong> The check that used to fire on <code>RESEND_API_KEY</code> missing now fires on any of <code>SMTP_HOST</code>, <code>SMTP_USER</code>, <code>SMTP_PASS</code> being missing. The send returns <code>{ status: 'skipped-no-smtp' }</code> with a single log line and no thrown error — signup, webhook, drip, and feedback flows all keep working in dev with zero configuration. The old <code>'skipped-no-key'</code> string is kept as a tolerated value in the <code>SendOutcome</code> union so any caller still checking the legacy string compiles.</p><p><strong>Caller compatibility.</strong> Zero caller files were touched. The six call sites (apply route, approve route, feedback submit route, Clerk webhook, drip cron, feedback-reminder cron) all use the same exported names with identical signatures. The legacy <code>_setResendTransportForTest</code> hook and the <code>ResendTransport</code> / <code>ResendPayload</code> types are re-exported as aliases of the new neutral names (<code>_setEmailTransportForTest</code>, <code>EmailTransport</code>, <code>EmailPayload</code>) so the test file keeps compiling.</p><p><strong>Transporter reuse.</strong> The nodemailer transporter is built lazily on first real send and cached at module scope, keyed on <code>host:port:user</code>. Recreating per-message would re-do the TLS handshake every email.</p><p><strong>What we did NOT touch.</strong> No caller signatures changed. No email templates changed. The <code>resend</code> package is not added to <code>package.json</code> (the old wrapper used <code>fetch</code> directly, no SDK). <code>RESEND_API_KEY</code> is documented in <code>.env.example</code> as a commented-out legacy fallback so a revert is one-file-mechanical.</p><p><strong>Files in commit.</strong> Two: <code>src/lib/email/send.ts</code> (rewritten transport, same API surface) and <code>.env.example</code> (new SMTP block, Resend now documented as legacy). Plus <code>package.json</code> / <code>package-lock.json</code> for the nodemailer dependency add.</p><p><strong>Quality gates.</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> — baseline preserved; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Local smoke test: imported the wrapper with no SMTP env vars set and called <code>sendWelcomeEmail</code> — the wrapper returned <code>{ status: 'skipped-no-smtp' }</code> as expected with a single info log line. Also confirmed both <code>_setEmailTransportForTest</code> and the legacy <code>_setResendTransportForTest</code> alias are exported. No real SMTP send was attempted (we deliberately did not want to email Howard from local dev).</p></li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> Restore the prior <code>send.ts</code> from git and revert <code>.env.example</code>. The wrapper API surface didn't change, so no callers need touching.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap.</strong> Project-folder copy of <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> only. The user-folder copy in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing it. Sync follow-up needed once he's done.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-storage-adapter-2026-06-11": {
      "id": "decision-storage-adapter-2026-06-11",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-11 (Storage adapter: Vercel KV + file fallback)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-11</p><p><strong>Problem.</strong> The four server-side JSON stores (applicants, feedback, beta-pro grants, unsubscribe state) all wrote to <code>.idiampro/*.json</code> under <code>process.cwd()</code>. That works fine in Electron and local dev because the project root is writable. On Vercel serverless it fails: <code>process.cwd()</code> resolves to <code>/var/task</code>, which is the read-only deployment image. The first signup attempt today returned <code>ENOENT: no such file or directory, mkdir '/var/task/.idiampro'</code>, which surfaced as a 500 from <code>/api/applicants/apply</code> and blocked production signups end-to-end.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Introduce a thin storage-adapter seam — <code>src/lib/storage/adapter.ts</code> — that exposes a small key/value API (<code>get</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>setAdd</code>, <code>setRemove</code>, <code>setMembers</code>). Every store stops calling <code>fs</code> directly and routes through the adapter. The adapter picks its backend at runtime from environment variables.</p><p><strong>Backend selection (in order).</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Vercel KV / Upstash Redis</strong> when both <code>KV_REST_API_URL</code> and <code>KV_REST_API_TOKEN</code> are present. We use the <code>@vercel/kv</code> SDK (added to <code>package.json</code>). All Redis ops are HTTP, which suits serverless cold starts.</p></li><li><p><strong>File-based JSON</strong> when KV env vars are absent and <code>process.cwd()</code> is writable. One <code>.json</code> file per key under <code>.idiampro/</code>, atomic-rename writes (write to <code>.tmp</code>, rename). Sets get a <code>.set.json</code> sidecar. This preserves the Electron / dev / test behavior exactly — Howard's local workflow is unchanged.</p></li><li><p><strong>No-op stub</strong> when neither KV is provisioned nor the FS is writable (e.g. production without a KV store attached). Writes log a one-time warning and drop. Reads return null. This stops 500s but doesn't persist — it's a degraded-mode safety net that makes the misconfiguration visible in logs without breaking the request path.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Key naming convention.</strong> <code>applicant:&lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>applicants:all</code> index, <code>applicant-email:&lt;email&gt;</code> reverse-lookup; same shape for feedback (<code>feedback:&lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>feedback:all</code>, <code>feedback-user:&lt;userId&gt;</code>), pro grants (<code>pro-grant:&lt;userId&gt;</code>), and unsubscribes (<code>unsubscribe:&lt;userId&gt;</code>, <code>unsubscribes:all</code>). The index sets let <code>listApplicants</code> / <code>listFeedback</code> enumerate without SCAN.</p><p><strong>Caller compatibility.</strong> Zero caller files were touched. The exported function names and signatures of <code>applicant-store.ts</code>, <code>feedback-store.ts</code>, <code>beta-pro-grant.ts</code>, and <code>unsubscribe-store.ts</code> are unchanged. The eight API routes and one cron route that consume these stores keep working without edits.</p><p><strong>Stub-safe failure modes.</strong> The KV branch wraps client init in try/catch and falls through to file/stub on failure. The file branch probes writability with a sync <code>mkdirSync</code> on the data dir; <code>EACCES</code> / <code>EROFS</code> on Vercel falls through to stub. The stub backend logs the &quot;provision Vercel KV&quot; instruction exactly once per process, then silently no-ops thereafter.</p><p><strong>Env vars added.</strong> <code>KV_REST_API_URL</code>, <code>KV_REST_API_TOKEN</code> (documented in <code>.env.example</code>), plus an optional <code>IDIAMPRO_DATA_DIR</code> override for the file backend. The legacy <code>EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_STORE_PATH</code> / <code>IDIAMPRO_APPLICANT_STORE_PATH</code> / <code>IDIAMPRO_FEEDBACK_STORE_PATH</code> / <code>IDIAMPRO_BETA_PRO_STORE_PATH</code> vars are no longer read — the new layout is one file per key under <code>IDIAMPRO_DATA_DIR</code>.</p><p><strong>What Howard does next in Vercel.</strong> Open the project on the Vercel dashboard, go to the <strong>Storage</strong> tab, click <strong>Create Database</strong> &gt; <strong>KV</strong>. The free instant tier is enough — no card required. Pick a region (US East to match the function region is fine). After creation Vercel auto-injects <code>KV_REST_API_URL</code>, <code>KV_REST_API_TOKEN</code>, and read-only variants into every connected project's environment. Trigger a redeploy (or push any commit) so the new env vars land in the running deployment. Then re-test the signup endpoint with curl — it should return the new applicant record instead of the ENOENT 500.</p><p><strong>Quality gates.</strong></p><ul><li><p><code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> — baseline 53 errors preserved; zero new errors introduced.</p></li><li><p>Local smoke test: round-tripped an applicant (create / getById / getByEmail / dedupe / list / approve / approvedEmails / reset) and a beta-pro grant and an unsubscribe record through the file backend. All assertions passed; backend logged <code>[storage] using backend: file</code> as expected.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Files in commit.</strong> Eight: <code>src/lib/storage/adapter.ts</code> (new), <code>src/lib/access/applicant-store.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/access/feedback-store.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/access/beta-pro-grant.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/email/unsubscribe-store.ts</code>, <code>.env.example</code>, <code>package.json</code>, <code>package-lock.json</code>.</p><p><strong>How to reverse.</strong> Restore the four store files from git, delete <code>src/lib/storage/adapter.ts</code>, drop the <code>@vercel/kv</code> dependency. The file-backed JSON layout in the rolled-back stores is identical to what existed before the adapter, so any data written to <code>.idiampro/*.json</code> by the file backend would not match the legacy single-file layout — but no production data ever wrote via the file backend (production lives in KV), so a revert only affects Electron / dev where the data is disposable.</p><p><strong>Outline sync gap.</strong> Project-folder copy of <em>IdeaM - Development.idm</em> only. The user-folder copy in <code>~/Documents/IDM Outlines/</code> was NOT touched — Howard is actively editing it. Sync follow-up needed once he's done.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "arch-deep-dive-env-vars-vercel-2026-06-12": {
      "id": "arch-deep-dive-env-vars-vercel-2026-06-12",
      "name": "Architecture Deep Dive — Env Vars + Vercel",
      "content": "<h3>Environment Variables — what they are, why we use them, how to change them</h3>\n<p><strong>What an \"environment variable\" actually is:</strong></p>\n<p>Think of an env var as a tiny labeled box that holds a piece of configuration. The label is the NAME (like <code>SMTP_PASS</code>) and the box contains the VALUE (like your PrivateEmail password). Your IdeaM code says \"open the box labeled SMTP_PASS and use whatever's inside.\" The actual password lives in the box at Vercel, NOT in the code. That way you can change passwords or move to a different service without touching code, and you can ship the code publicly (to GitHub) without leaking secrets.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this pattern exists:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security.</strong> Code lives on GitHub, in your laptop's filesystem, in CI logs, in build artifacts. If the password were in the code, it'd leak constantly. The env var stays at Vercel, encrypted, only readable by the running app.</li>\n<li><strong>Environment-specific config.</strong> Production uses one database; preview deployments use another; local dev uses a third. Same code, different env vars per environment. No code changes needed to swap.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot-swappable.</strong> Change an env var in Vercel &rarr; redeploy &rarr; the code now uses the new value. Rotating a stolen API key takes 2 minutes instead of a code release.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Where env vars live in IdeaM's setup:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vercel dashboard</strong> &mdash; for production and preview. Encrypted at rest. Only you can view them.</li>\n<li><strong><code>.env.local</code> on your laptop</strong> &mdash; for local development (Electron + dev server). Plaintext file on your machine. Git ignores it (never gets committed).</li>\n<li><strong><code>.env.example</code> in the codebase</strong> &mdash; documents which env vars exist, with empty values. Safe to commit because no actual secrets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code> prefix &mdash; important distinction:</strong></p>\n<p>Some env vars have a <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code> prefix (like <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY</code>). That prefix tells Next.js \"bake this value into the JavaScript that gets shipped to browsers.\" So anyone viewing the page source can see it. We only use <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code> for values that are SAFE to be public &mdash; usually \"publishable keys\" that identify the app without granting access. The \"secret key\" twins (like <code>CLERK_SECRET_KEY</code>) stay server-only &mdash; never sent to the browser.</p>\n<h3>The actual env vars IdeaM uses</h3>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Env var</th><th>What it does</th><th>Service it talks to</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><code>SMTP_HOST</code></td><td>Server to send email through</td><td>PrivateEmail (mail.privateemail.com)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>SMTP_PORT</code></td><td>Network port for SMTP (465 = TLS encrypted)</td><td>PrivateEmail</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>SMTP_USER</code></td><td>Mailbox to authenticate as</td><td>PrivateEmail</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>SMTP_PASS</code></td><td>Password for that mailbox</td><td>PrivateEmail</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>EMAIL_FROM</code></td><td>Address shown as sender on outgoing emails</td><td>(optional &mdash; defaults to SMTP_USER)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>EMAIL_TO_ADMIN</code></td><td>Where new-applicant notifications get sent</td><td>(optional &mdash; defaults to howard@2ndbrainware.com)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>KV_REST_API_URL</code></td><td>Where the Upstash database lives</td><td>Upstash (auto-set by Vercel integration)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>KV_REST_API_TOKEN</code></td><td>Password for the database</td><td>Upstash (auto-set)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>GEMINI_API_KEY</code></td><td>Lets the app call Google's Gemini AI</td><td>Google</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>RESEND_API_KEY</code></td><td>Legacy fallback (not currently used)</td><td>Resend</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY</code></td><td>Public ID for auth &mdash; visible to browser</td><td>Clerk (not yet set)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>CLERK_SECRET_KEY</code></td><td>Server-only auth secret</td><td>Clerk (not yet set)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code></td><td>Lets the app process web payments</td><td>Stripe (not yet set)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>REVENUECAT_*</code></td><td>iOS in-app purchase coordination</td><td>RevenueCat (not yet set)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>SENTRY_DSN</code> / <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN</code></td><td>Where crash reports go</td><td>Sentry (not yet set)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The blanks at the bottom are remaining setup items.</p>\n<p><strong>Stub-safe pattern:</strong> every env var IdeaM uses is OPTIONAL. If it's missing, the related feature silently no-ops with a log line &mdash; nothing crashes. That's why we could ship the SMTP swap before the password was set, and why missing Clerk keys gracefully hide the signup form instead of breaking the page.</p>\n<h3>Vercel &mdash; what it is and how it's configured</h3>\n<p><strong>What Vercel is:</strong></p>\n<p>Vercel runs the website on a global network of computers. When someone types 2ndbrainware.com, the nearest Vercel computer serves them the page in milliseconds. Vercel ALSO runs \"serverless functions\" &mdash; small programs that respond to API requests (like signup form submissions) on demand, without managing servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Vercel concepts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Project</strong> &mdash; the IdeaM app at Vercel. Has a name (<code>idiam-pro</code>), an ID, settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Deployment</strong> &mdash; one specific snapshot of the code. Every git push creates a new deployment. The latest one is \"Production\"; older ones are kept around for rollback.</li>\n<li><strong>Environments</strong> &mdash; Production, Preview (for branch previews), Development. Env vars can differ per environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Domains</strong> &mdash; human-readable names pointed at the project. Currently <code>2ndbrainware.com</code>, <code>www.2ndbrainware.com</code>, and the default <code>idiam-pro.vercel.app</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage</strong> &mdash; managed databases attached to the project. Currently: one Upstash KV.</li>\n<li><strong>Logs</strong> &mdash; every request and every function invocation gets logged. Visible in real time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How configuration flows:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change something in the Vercel dashboard (add env var, add domain, etc.)</li>\n<li>Vercel says \"needs a redeploy\"</li>\n<li>Either push code OR click \"Redeploy\" on the latest deployment</li>\n<li>Vercel builds a fresh deployment with the new config</li>\n<li>Vercel automatically points 2ndbrainware.com at the new deployment</li>\n<li>Old deployment stays around but no longer receives traffic &mdash; one-click rollback available</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>How to alter things</h3>\n<p><strong>Change an env var value (e.g., rotate the SMTP password):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Go to https://vercel.com/howard-jachters-projects/idiam-pro/settings/environment-variables</li>\n<li>Find the row (e.g. <code>SMTP_PASS</code>), click the three-dot menu, click Edit</li>\n<li>Type the new value, click Save</li>\n<li>Click \"Redeploy\" on the prompt (or trigger one from Deployments page)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Add a new env var:</strong> Same page, scroll to bottom, type name + value, pick which environments it applies to, Save, redeploy.</p>\n<p><strong>Change which domain points at production:</strong> https://vercel.com/howard-jachters-projects/idiam-pro/domains</p>\n<p><strong>Roll back to a previous deployment:</strong> https://vercel.com/howard-jachters-projects/idiam-pro/deployments &mdash; find an earlier \"Ready\" deployment, three-dot menu, \"Promote to Production\". Live traffic moves to it within seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger a manual redeploy:</strong> Same Deployments page, three-dot on the latest, \"Redeploy\".</p>\n<p><strong>Open the database to inspect data:</strong> https://vercel.com/howard-jachters-projects/idiam-pro/stores &mdash; click the Upstash database, then \"Open in Upstash\" &mdash; browser-based console for inspecting records.</p>\n<p><strong>Check logs when something fails:</strong> https://vercel.com/howard-jachters-projects/idiam-pro/logs &mdash; real-time stream of every request and error. Filter by function (e.g., <code>/api/applicants/apply</code>) to debug specific features.</p>\n<h3>Technical glossary</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DNS</strong> &mdash; the phone book of the internet. Maps human-readable names (2ndbrainware.com) to machine IPs.</li>\n<li><strong>MX record</strong> &mdash; the DNS entry that says \"send mail for this domain to this server.\"</li>\n<li><strong>A record</strong> &mdash; the DNS entry that says \"send web traffic for this domain to this IP.\"</li>\n<li><strong>CNAME</strong> &mdash; DNS entry that says \"this name points to that other name.\"</li>\n<li><strong>SMTP</strong> &mdash; Simple Mail Transfer Protocol. The 1980s-era language email servers use to hand mail to each other.</li>\n<li><strong>TLS / SSL</strong> &mdash; encryption for data in transit. Why URLs start with HTTPS instead of HTTP.</li>\n<li><strong>API endpoint</strong> &mdash; a URL on the server that accepts data and returns a response (e.g., <code>/api/applicants/apply</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless function</strong> &mdash; a small program that runs on-demand on Vercel's computers. No idle server cost.</li>\n<li><strong>KV (Key-Value) store</strong> &mdash; a database that stores data by lookup key. Simpler than SQL, faster for our use case.</li>\n<li><strong>Webhook</strong> &mdash; an external service (Clerk, Stripe) calling OUR API to tell us something happened.</li>\n<li><strong>Cron job</strong> &mdash; a scheduled task. The feedback reminder runs every day at noon via Vercel cron.</li>\n<li><strong>Env-gated / stub-safe</strong> &mdash; code that no-ops when its env vars aren't set, instead of crashing.</li>\n<li><strong>Idempotent</strong> &mdash; an operation safe to repeat &mdash; running it twice has the same effect as once. The welcome email is idempotent.</li>\n<li><strong>DKIM / SPF / DMARC</strong> &mdash; DNS-based email authentication. DKIM signs each email; SPF says \"these servers are allowed to send mail for this domain\"; DMARC tells receiving servers what to do if SPF/DKIM fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Set up 2026-06-11 / 2026-06-12 during the email + storage setup session.</em></p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "strategy-brainstorm-2026-06-11",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": true,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-byok-launch-no-iap-2026-06-12": {
      "id": "decision-byok-launch-no-iap-2026-06-12",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-12 (Payment strategy: BYOK-only at launch, no IAP)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-12</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> No in-app payment system at launch. Pro features unlock via BYOK (Bring Your Own Key) — the user pastes their own AI API key into Settings. Because no money flows through the app at launch, Apple's 15–30% App Store commission does not apply, and neither does Google Play's equivalent on a future Android build.</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> The BYOK model sidesteps two big launch blockers at once. First, no billing infrastructure is required (no Stripe wiring, no RevenueCat, no receipt-validation server, no refund/dispute flow) — that whole layer can be deferred until there's an actual paid Pro tier. Second, because the user pays the AI provider directly (Google for Gemini, Anthropic for Claude, OpenAI for GPT, etc.), Apple and Google have no transaction to take a cut of, even on App Store builds. The app's value-add is the outliner itself plus the integration with the user's key; the AI usage cost stays between the user and their provider.</p><p><strong>Future — when a paid Pro tier is added:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>iOS App Store build:</strong> Apple In-App Purchase (StoreKit) via a Capacitor plugin, or a web link-out to a Stripe checkout page post-Epic-ruling (Apple now allows external purchase links in the US as of early 2024, with the 27% reduced commission). Choice depends on whether the link-out friction is worth the saved commission percentage.</li><li><strong>Mac App Store build:</strong> Same as iOS — Apple IAP via StoreKit. Mac App Store enforces the same in-app purchase rules as iOS.</li><li><strong>Self-hosted Mac DMG, web, Windows, Linux:</strong> Stripe or Clerk Billing freely. No store, no commission. These are the channels where the full retail margin lands.</li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Add Stripe + Clerk Billing wiring for the non-store builds; for the iOS App Store build, add StoreKit integration via a Capacitor plugin (or use a link-out flow under Apple's post-Epic-ruling rules). Replace the BYOK gate on Pro features with a Pro-subscription check fed by the chosen billing backend. Keep BYOK as a parallel unlock path — users who already paid for their own AI key shouldn't be forced into a subscription on top.</p>"
    },
    "decision-multi-store-sequence-2026-06-12": {
      "id": "decision-multi-store-sequence-2026-06-12",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-06-12 (Multi-store distribution sequence)",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "default",
      "content": "<p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-06-12</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> Launch v1 ships to three channels: the iOS App Store, the web app (Vercel auto-deploy), and a self-hosted Mac DMG (downloaded from idiampro.com, auto-updates via electron-updater). Other app stores are added post-launch in sequence, not in parallel: Mac App Store at v1.1, Microsoft Store at v1.2, Linux Flathub at v1.3, Google Play at v2 (after the Android Capacitor project is scaffolded).</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> Every app store adds real value — discovery, a trust signal that the app passed review, auto-update infrastructure, and (for paid tiers eventually) a billing relationship the store already has with the user. But every store also adds real cost: an initial review cycle that can take days or weeks, store-specific assets (screenshots in the right resolutions, marketing copy in the right voice, privacy nutrition labels, etc.), and ongoing maintenance every time the app updates. Sequencing the store rollouts prevents the launch from getting stuck behind multiple review queues — if Apple rejects something, that doesn't also block Microsoft Store and Flathub submissions that haven't been started yet.</p><p><strong>The launch-cost calculus is also better right now because of the BYOK decision:</strong> with no money flowing through the app at launch, every store's commission rate is effectively zero. There's no \"we lose 30% on iOS so we should rush to web\" pressure — all six channels are commission-free until a paid Pro tier exists. That removes the urgency that would otherwise force a parallel push.</p><p><strong>Sequence rationale:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>v1 (now):</strong> iOS App Store + web + self-hosted Mac DMG. iOS is the highest-trust mobile channel and the one Howard has been actively preparing. Web is free distribution with no review queue. Self-hosted Mac DMG ships immediately with auto-update so Mac users aren't waiting on Apple review.</li><li><strong>v1.1 — Mac App Store:</strong> Same Electron build as the DMG with Mac App Store entitlements. Adds the Mac discovery surface and lets Mac users get the app via the same place they get other Mac apps.</li><li><strong>v1.2 — Microsoft Store:</strong> Same Electron build as the Windows DMG-equivalent, packaged as MSIX. Adds Windows discovery.</li><li><strong>v1.3 — Linux Flathub:</strong> Flatpak packaging of the Electron build. Adds the Linux discovery surface (Flathub is the de facto distribution channel for non-distro-specific Linux apps).</li><li><strong>v2 — Google Play:</strong> Requires scaffolding the Android Capacitor project first (currently not yet scaffolded — see the Platform Support Matrix in CLAUDE.md). Deferred to v2 because Android is a separate build path, not a quick add-on to existing builds.</li></ul><p><strong>How to reverse:</strong> Add stores in parallel instead of in sequence. There's no technical blocker — each store's packaging is independent of the others. The cost is weeks of additional work split across multiple review queues simultaneously, and the risk that a rejection on one store blocks attention from being paid to the others. Reversing the decision means accepting that cost in exchange for hitting more channels faster.</p>"
    },
    "decision-ios-hide-paid-upgrades-2026-07": {
      "id": "decision-ios-hide-paid-upgrades-2026-07",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-07 (iOS: hide paid upgrades at launch; real in-app account deletion; mic usage strings)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision A — Hide paid upgrades on iOS at launch.</strong> On iOS/iPad we do NOT show the paid Pro/Student purchase UI at launch. Apple in-app purchase (RevenueCat) isn't wired yet, and shipping a broken or absent purchase path is a guaranteed App Store rejection. Instead, iOS shows ONLY the free bring-your-own-key path (unlimited AI, no cost). Gated via isCapacitor() (excludes Electron) in settings-dialog.tsx (Account section), upgrade-prompt.tsx (See plans CTA hidden, BYOK kept), and the /upgrade page (paid CTAs replaced with a BYOK link + reworded iOS note). Web and Mac are unchanged and still show the full paid path. <em>Reverse:</em> remove the isNativeIOS / isCapacitor branches once Apple IAP is live.</p><p><strong>Decision B — Real in-app account deletion (Apple guideline 5.1.1(v)).</strong> Added a distinct \"Delete account\" action in Settings > Privacy & Data (shown only when signed in), separate from the existing \"Delete all my data\" (local-only) action. It calls a new /api/account/delete route that erases server-side records (beta applicant by email, feedback by userId, bug reports by userId+email in Upstash/KV) and deletes the Clerk account via REST, then the client also deletes via the Clerk SDK, wipes local data, and signs out. Stub-safe when auth is disabled.</p><p><strong>Decision C — iOS microphone usage strings.</strong> Added NSMicrophoneUsageDescription and NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription to ios/App/App/Info.plist so iOS doesn't auto-reject voice dictation.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "decision-entry-tier-price-2026-07-03": {
      "id": "decision-entry-tier-price-2026-07-03",
      "name": "Decisions Log — 2026-07-03 (Entry tier priced: $29.99 one-time / $19.99 founder launch)",
      "content": "<p><strong>2026-07-03 — Entry tier priced at $29.99 one-time, $19.99 founder launch.</strong></p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> serious-but-impulse price that drives adoption while the recurring Pro tier is the real upsell; sustainable because entry-tier AI is local/free/BYOK (near-zero marginal cost); Apple Small Business 15% nets ~$25.49 / ~$17.</p><p><strong>Reverse by:</strong> editing the SKU price.</p><p>See Planning section 12.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "91bc0382-9536-443a-a377-1372f072cd9d": {
      "id": "91bc0382-9536-443a-a377-1372f072cd9d",
      "name": "New Features — July 5–7, 2026 (admin security, reliability, data-safety, video)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Features shipped in the 2026-07-05 → 07-07 session:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Admin security lockdown.</strong> Real server-enforced admin access — must be signed in AND on an ADMIN_EMAILS allowlist (defaults to Howard) — on every /admin page AND the data APIs behind them, replacing the old client-side browser-flag stopgap. Persistent amber “ADMIN CONSOLE” banner so the admin surface is unmistakable. (Separate admin subdomain = planned follow-up.)</li><li><strong>Three-layer signup data protection.</strong> Every beta application now (1) emails Howard a full backup copy independent of the database, (2) fails loud — never a false success, alerts on storage failure, and (3) a watchdog checks the store and emails on outage. Turns a database outage from silent data loss into a harmless, visible event.</li><li><strong>Dependency Health Monitor (/admin/health).</strong> Private admin dashboard that checks every third-party dependency (storage, Gemini, Clerk, Stripe, email, etc.) with cheap pings, hourly, and privately emails Howard on any failure. Already caught a rejected AssemblyAI key.</li><li><strong>Marketing Claims Verification test suite.</strong> Automated tests that PROVE each public data-safety claim is true, so a future code change can't silently make a website claim false.</li><li><strong>Dependency Register outline.</strong> New “IdeaM - Dependencies” outline: every external vendor, its blast radius if it fails, and our recurring responsibilities (billing, keys, certs, monitoring).</li><li><strong>“Generate Video” (Phase 1–2, in progress).</strong> Desktop-first outline→slideshow video generator: pick a chapter → slide images + real AI voiceover → ffmpeg-stitched MP4, all native in the desktop app. “Generate Video” action lives in the Export menu, wired to real outline content, with a large-chapter guard and honest desktop-only handling. Remaining: slide design (Phase 3), options/transitions/preview (Phase 4), Pro-gating + docs (Phase 5). Pro-tier feature.</li><li><strong>“Your data is safe” website copy.</strong> Provably-true, test-backed trust copy added to the Marketing outline (privacy/guarantee lines pending legal review).</li><li><strong>Fixes:</strong> admin-applicants page horizontal-scroll bug; reliability principle documented; Bootstrap Plan session-log migrated to the conversation log.</li></ul><p><em>Reflected here per Howard's request 2026-07-07. Video generation phases 3-5 and the naming-consistency audit (app = IdeaM, SecondBrainWare = company only) remain open.</em></p>",
      "type": "text",
      "parentId": "2f759835-9c75-462e-9901-4509889e81e2",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "45082891-6905-4755-8718-a99944d7656d": {
      "id": "45082891-6905-4755-8718-a99944d7656d",
      "name": "Processing: Local vs Cloud by Platform (macOS / iOS / Web)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Verified against the shipping code on 2026-07-12.</strong> Where each capability actually runs on each of our three live platforms.</p><p></p><pre><code>Capability                              | macOS (Electron) | iOS / iPadOS      | Web (browser)\n----------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------|--------------------------\nApp + outline editing                   | Local            | Local             | Local (in browser)\nOutline files / storage                 | Local            | Local             | Local (browser storage)*\nFree keyword search (Second Brain)      | Local            | Local             | Local\nMind maps &amp; flowcharts                  | Local            | Local             | Local (drawn in browser)\nFree narration voices                   | Local            | Local             | Cloud (no free local)**\nSlideshow video render (slides+stitch)  | Local            | N/A (planned)     | N/A (planned)***\nPremium studio voices                   | Cloud            | Cloud             | Cloud\nAI text (write/expand/reformat/etc.)    | Cloud            | Cloud             | Cloud\nImage generation                        | Cloud            | Cloud             | Cloud\nAudio transcription                     | Cloud            | Cloud             | Cloud\n</code></pre><p></p><p><strong>Legend.</strong> <strong>Local</strong> = runs on the device / in the browser: free, offline-capable, no per-use cost. <strong>Cloud</strong> = runs on a server or a paid AI API (Gemini / OpenAI for text and premium voices, Google Imagen for images, AssemblyAI for transcription). <strong>N/A (planned)</strong> = not built yet; does not work today.</p><p></p><p><strong>Notes &amp; corrections to the earlier draft:</strong></p><p>* <strong>Web outline storage is device-local</strong> (browser localStorage, plus an optional local folder the user can pick). There is <strong>no cloud sync</strong> today — the earlier \"optional cloud sync\" note was wrong.</p><p>** <strong>Web has no free on-device narration voice.</strong> Desktop uses the Mac’s built-in voices and iOS uses Apple’s on-device voices (both free, offline). On the web, narration is produced through the cloud voice service — the earlier “browser voice” note was wrong.</p><p>*** <strong>The slideshow video render is desktop-only today.</strong> It stitches slides with on-device ffmpeg inside the desktop app. A cloud render service for iOS and web is planned but <strong>not built</strong>. (Separately, free audio podcasts already work: on-device on iOS, and via the cloud voice service on web.)</p><p></p><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> everything you type, search, and diagram runs locally on every platform (private, free, offline). Anything that calls an AI — AI writing, premium voices, images, transcription — is a paid cloud service on every platform.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "54647fdc-d197-4733-8ccf-5bbda1368ab9",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "2.3"
    },
    "platform-strategy-runs-where-2026-07-12": {
      "id": "platform-strategy-runs-where-2026-07-12",
      "name": "Platform Strategy — What Runs Where (macOS / iOS / Web)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Official platform strategy — owner-approved 2026-07-12.</strong> The plain-language rule for what we build on each platform and why.</p><p></p><p><strong>GUIDING RULE.</strong> Heavy work goes where it is <strong>free to run</strong> (local, on the desktop). On platforms where that same work would be <strong>expensive</strong> (the cloud, which is what web and mobile have to use), we either skip it or make it a <strong>paid premium feature</strong> so it pays for itself. We never carry a cost we are not paid for.</p><p></p><p><strong>PER-PLATFORM ROLES</strong></p><p><strong>macOS — \"The Studio.\"</strong> Full power, including the entire video and podcast render done <strong>locally</strong> (free to run). This is the creation machine and the cheapest platform for us to operate, so the heavy lifting lives here.</p><p><strong>iPhone / iPad — \"Capture and Companion.\"</strong> The full core app (outlining, search, mind maps, import, AI writing) plus free on-device voice and on-device podcasts. We <strong>skip the heavy on-device video render</strong> — it is painful on mobile, bloats the app, drains the battery, and is a maintenance burden. Make videos on the Mac, or later through a paid cloud render. This platform is for capturing and shaping ideas on the go.</p><p><strong>Web — \"Accessible Core plus Premium Cloud.\"</strong> The full core app runs right in the browser (cheap, mostly local). Heavy outputs (video, premium voices) can only run in the cloud on the web, which costs money — so on the web they are <strong>premium / paid only</strong>, and thus pay for themselves. The web free tier is the thinking tool; the fancy outputs are the upsell.</p><p></p><p><strong>THE MATRIX (capability -&gt; macOS / iPhone-iPad / Web)</strong></p><pre><code>Capability                                   | macOS (The Studio)        | iPhone / iPad (Capture)        | Web (Accessible Core)\n---------------------------------------------|---------------------------|--------------------------------|---------------------------\nCore app (edit, search, mind maps,           | Yes                       | Yes                            | Yes\n  import/export, AI text)                     |                           |                                |\nPodcasts (audio)                             | Local                     | On-device                      | Premium (cloud)\nFree on-device voice                         | Yes                       | Yes                            | None (paid cloud only)\nVideo generation                             | Local, free to run        | Skip -- make on Mac, or        | Later, premium cloud only\n                                              |                           |   later paid cloud             |\nPremium voices, AI text, images, transcript. | Cloud (paid)              | Cloud (paid)                   | Cloud (paid)\n</code></pre><p></p><p><strong>WHAT THIS ELIMINATES</strong></p><p><strong>1. No heavy local video render on iPhone or web.</strong> Concentrate that work on the desktop, where it is free; offer it as premium cloud elsewhere only if demand justifies the ongoing cost.</p><p><strong>2. No free voice on the web.</strong> Web free users get the core thinking tool; audio outputs are premium / cloud.</p><p><strong>3. Let the paywall carry the cloud cost on web and mobile.</strong> The server-side lock ensures those expensive features only fire for paying customers, so the pricier platforms never cost the company without revenue coming in.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "54647fdc-d197-4733-8ccf-5bbda1368ab9",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "2.4"
    },
    "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23": {
      "id": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "name": "Session 2026-07-22/23 — Email, Your Voice & Social Export",
      "content": "<p><strong>Session 2026-07-22 / 2026-07-23 — Email suite, Your Voice, and the Social export lineup.</strong></p><p>A large batch shipped: an opt-in Professional Customization framework, Export Email (four hand-offs), Import Email (junk filed aside), 'Your Voice' personal writing-style emulation, and seven social share formats — X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Facebook, Threads, Bluesky, YouTube — on one extensible template. Decisions, the pending monetization model, the reference user, backlog, and draft website copy are in the child notes below.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [
        "session-2026-07-23-principles",
        "session-2026-07-23-monetization",
        "session-2026-07-23-derek",
        "session-2026-07-23-backlog",
        "session-2026-07-23-website-cards"
      ],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "session-2026-07-23-principles": {
      "id": "session-2026-07-23-principles",
      "name": "Decisions & Principles — email, social, Your Voice",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decisions & principles locked this session.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Strictly opt-in.</strong> The email and social tools are OFF by default and never touch a user's accounts automatically. Turning them on is a deliberate choice.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Honest consent.</strong> The consent screen shows the pros AND the cons — no dark patterns, no hiding the trade-offs.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>One home for the controls.</strong> Everything lives in a 'Professional Customization' settings section with a master switch plus granular per-tool toggles.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Junk is quarantined, never deleted.</strong> On import, suspected junk is filed aside into a clearly labeled sub-branch so nothing is ever lost and the user stays in control.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Honest social hand-offs.</strong> One-click compose only where the platform genuinely supports it (X, Threads, Bluesky); clean copy/paste for LinkedIn and Facebook. No fake buttons that pretend to post.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Your Voice is the user's OWN voice.</strong> Personal writing-style emulation only — never impersonating a third party.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Auto-publish stays on.</strong> Verified work ships without stopping to ask.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Pause / resume control.</strong> A 'let's talk' / 'continue' control lets the user pause a run and pick it back up.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Extensible template pattern.</strong> Output is built on a shared, extensible template so adding new formats later is cheap.</p></li>\n</ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "session-2026-07-23-monetization": {
      "id": "session-2026-07-23-monetization",
      "name": "Monetization model — PENDING Howard's numbers (NOT YET BUILT)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Monetization model — PENDING Howard's numbers. NOT YET BUILT.</strong></p>\n<p>Working shape, awaiting Howard's decisions before any of it is built:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Transparent trial, plus a free floor that is never taken away (e.g. Copy and Download stay free forever).</p></li>\n<li><p>Power features fold into the existing $19.99 Pro tier rather than a new charge.</p></li>\n<li><p>Gentle, frequency-capped upsell — never badger.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Awaiting Howard:</strong> the exact free-vs-Pro line, the trial length, and whether this lives inside Pro or as an add-on. Marked clearly as not yet built.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "session-2026-07-23-derek": {
      "id": "session-2026-07-23-derek",
      "name": "Reference user — Derek (drives Your Voice + social)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Reference user — Derek.</strong></p>\n<p>Derek wants to post to social media in his own voice. He is the driver behind Your Voice and the social export lineup, and our first reference user for these tools.</p>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "session-2026-07-23-backlog": {
      "id": "session-2026-07-23-backlog",
      "name": "Backlog / Next",
      "content": "<p><strong>Backlog / next.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Marketing-site catch-up.</strong> Draft feature cards for the new wizards (Howard's brand voice — needs his approval). Draft copy is in the sibling note below.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Instagram logo upload.</strong> Make logo upload available in both Generate Video and the Instagram dialog (minor).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Teach Mode (future).</strong> Learn a user's voice from their edits over time.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Short-form vertical video rendering.</strong> For Shorts / Reels-style vertical output.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Deep integrations (deferred).</strong> Optional later: Gmail draft via OAuth, direct social posting. Explicitly deferred for now.</p></li>\n</ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "session-2026-07-23-website-cards": {
      "id": "session-2026-07-23-website-cards",
      "name": "Website feature cards — DRAFT for Howard's approval",
      "content": "<p><strong>DRAFT copy for the /features page — pending Howard's brand-voice approval. NOT yet published; do not put live without sign-off.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Export Email</strong> — Turn any outline into a polished, ready-to-send email in one step.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Import Email</strong> — Drop an email or a whole thread straight into an outline; junk gets set aside, not lost.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Your Voice</strong> — Every draft sounds like you, because it learns your own writing style.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Share to Social</strong> — One outline, seven platforms — X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Facebook, Threads, Bluesky, and YouTube — formatted and ready to post.</p></li>\n</ul>",
      "type": "document",
      "parentId": "session-email-voice-social-2026-07-23",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": ""
    },
    "dec-2026-07-23-pricing": {
      "id": "dec-2026-07-23-pricing",
      "name": "PRICING — Email + Social + Your Voice INCLUDED in Professional",
      "content": "<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Email, Social, and Your Voice are <strong>included in the Professional tier</strong> — no new SKU, no add-on. Per Howard's cost-formula (charge cost + 20% only where there is real cost; if a service costs ~nothing, fold it into Professional), these features add no separate cost worth pricing.</p><p>The free floor runs on on-device or the user's own key (BYOK) — $0 to us. The paid path rides the existing first-month-free trial; no new billing surface.</p>",
      "type": "note",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784814066402,
        "updatedAt": 1784814066402
      }
    },
    "dec-2026-07-23-cost-safety": {
      "id": "dec-2026-07-23-cost-safety",
      "name": "COST-SAFETY — server-side fail-closed AI usage meter (built + shipped)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Built + shipped:</strong> a server-side, per-account, <strong>atomic, fail-closed</strong> AI usage meter (Upstash/KV). Free = 0 company AI (on-device / BYOK only).</p><p>Our company AI key is reachable ONLY by (a) the internal dev allowlist, or (b) verified paid users within their allowance. The paid door sits behind a <strong>'subscriptions verified' flag that is currently OFF</strong> until Stripe/RevenueCat verification is wired — so today ONLY dev accounts can reach our key. BYOK always uses the user's own key.</p><p>Retries are capped at 2 and counted. Image, audio, and voice all share the same gate. A three-door cap prompt offers BYOK / overage (placeholder) / on-device. A permanent cost-guardrails test is a <strong>RELEASE BLOCKER</strong> in TEST EVERYTHING.</p><p><strong>Howard's 4 guardrails:</strong> (1) atomic server meter; (2) all-vendor coverage; (3) infra-abuse rate-limiting / anti-farming; (4) vendor-account spend caps — the last is <strong>Howard's to set</strong> on Gemini/Google Cloud, OpenAI, AssemblyAI, Vercel, and Upstash.</p>",
      "type": "note",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784814066402,
        "updatedAt": 1784814066402
      }
    },
    "dec-2026-07-23-allowances": {
      "id": "dec-2026-07-23-allowances",
      "name": "ALLOWANCES — monthly cloud-AI generation caps (tunable, best-guess)",
      "content": "<p>Howard's best-guess approval, tunable in one config line:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Free:</strong> 0 cloud-AI generations/month (on-device / BYOK only)</p></li><li><p><strong>Professional:</strong> 1000 / month</p></li><li><p><strong>Student:</strong> 500 / month</p></li><li><p><strong>Overage:</strong> +500 (placeholder)</p></li></ul><p>🚩 Numbers await Howard's approve/adjust.</p>",
      "type": "note",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784814066402,
        "updatedAt": 1784814066402
      }
    },
    "dec-2026-07-23-verifier": {
      "id": "dec-2026-07-23-verifier",
      "name": "VERIFIER — always-on, on-device, flag-and-suggest",
      "content": "<p>Always-on, <strong>on-device</strong> ($0, does not consume the AI meter). It <strong>flags and suggests — never silently rewrites</strong>. Runs on factual outputs: email, social, summaries, and inbound content.</p><p><strong>Messaging is conservative and promise-free EVERYWHERE</strong> — no efficacy claims (Howard's explicit correction). The verifier is a quiet safety net, not a marketing claim.</p>",
      "type": "note",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784814066402,
        "updatedAt": 1784814066402
      }
    },
    "dec-2026-07-23-pending": {
      "id": "dec-2026-07-23-pending",
      "name": "PENDING — Howard's to decide / do",
      "content": "<ul><li><p>🚩 Approve or adjust the allowance numbers (Free 0 / Pro 1000 / Student 500 / overage +500).</p></li><li><p>🚩 Lawyer to finalize the AI-accuracy disclaimer (draft is in the Legal outline).</p></li><li><p>🚩 Wire Stripe/RevenueCat subscription verification to switch the paid door ON.</p></li><li><p>🚩 Set vendor-account spend caps on Gemini/Google Cloud, OpenAI, AssemblyAI, Vercel, Upstash.</p></li></ul>",
      "type": "note",
      "parentId": "17a29ec6-74ba-4196-8f2f-f7dc8a59f862",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
